/**
 * Teach Me explanations for economics, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["economics"] = {
 "|Which of the following best defines 'opportunity cost'?|The total cost of producing a good|The value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made|The cost of raw materials only|The market price of a product": "Because resources are scarce and wants are unlimited, every choice means giving something up. Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. If Chidi has ₦5,000 and his scale of preference ranks a textbook first and a pair of sandals second, buying the textbook costs him the sandals: that is the opportunity cost, not the ₦5,000. It is real cost measured in alternatives, not money: B.\nMemory tip: Opportunity cost = the \"second choice\" you let go.",
 "|The diagram shows supply and demand curves. At price P₁, there is a situation of:|Equilibrium|Excess demand (shortage)|Excess supply (surplus)|Zero demand": "Equilibrium is where the demand and supply curves cross (point E): quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. In the diagram, P₁ lies below the equilibrium price. At this low price buyers want more (Qd moves right along the demand curve) while sellers offer less (Qs moves left along the supply curve). So Qd is greater than Qs, and the gap Qd − Qs is excess demand, a shortage: B. The shortage makes buyers bid the price up until equilibrium is restored.\nThe trap: C (excess supply) is the answer when the price is ABOVE equilibrium, so always check which side of E the price line lies.\nMemory tip: Price too Low → shortage; price too High → surplus (\"Low-Short, High-Heap\").",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of a perfectly competitive market?|Few large sellers|Product differentiation|High barriers to entry|Many buyers and sellers dealing in a homogeneous product": "Perfect competition has: many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous (identical) product, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge of the market, and perfect mobility of factors. Because each firm is tiny and the product is identical, no firm can affect the price; each is a price-taker facing a horizontal demand curve: D. Few large sellers describes oligopoly, product differentiation describes monopolistic competition, and high barriers to entry describe monopoly.\nMemory tip: Perfect competition = \"many, same, free, know\" (many firms, same product, free entry, full knowledge).",
 "|A fall in the price of good A leads to a fall in the demand for good B. This means goods A and B are:|Complements|Substitutes|Inferior goods|Giffen goods": "Substitutes are goods that serve the same purpose, such as Peak and Three Crowns milk, or rice and beans for a meal. If the price of A falls, people buy more A and switch away from B, so the demand for B falls (its whole curve shifts left). For substitutes, the price of one and the demand for the other move in the SAME direction, and cross elasticity is positive: B. For complements (car and petrol) a fall in A's price would RAISE demand for B.\nThe trap: A (complements) catches students who mix up the two directions; for complements the price of one and demand for the other move in opposite directions.\nMemory tip: Substitutes \"Same direction\"; Complements \"Contrary direction\".",
 "|National income is best defined as:|The total taxes collected by government|The total money value of all goods and services produced in a country over a period|The amount of money in circulation|The government's annual budget": "National income is the total money value of all final goods and services produced by a country in a given period, usually one year. GDP counts what is produced inside Nigeria's borders; GNP = GDP + net factor income from abroad; NNP = GNP − depreciation; national income in the strict sense is NNP at factor cost. It is measured by the output, income and expenditure methods, and in Nigeria the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS) publishes it: B. Taxes, money in circulation and the budget are different things.\nMemory tip: National income = value of a year's FINAL output (no double counting).",
 "|The production possibility curve (PPC) shows:|The maximum combination of two goods an economy can produce given its resources|The minimum level of production needed to sustain the economy|The imports and exports of a country|The population growth of a country": "The production possibility curve (frontier) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce when all its resources are fully and efficiently used with the given technology: A. Points on the curve are efficient; a point inside shows unemployed or wasted resources; a point outside cannot be reached now. Moving along the curve shows opportunity cost: more of one good means less of the other. Growth (more resources, better technology) shifts the curve outward.\nMemory tip: PPC = the \"menu limit\" of an economy; its slope is opportunity cost.",
 "|Price elasticity of demand is said to be elastic when:|PED = 1|PED > 1 (percentage change in quantity demanded > percentage change in price)|PED < 1|PED = 0": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price (sign ignored). If PED > 1, quantity demanded changes by a larger percentage than price, so demand is elastic: B. Example: price rises 10% and quantity demanded falls 25%: PED = 25 ÷ 10 = 2.5 (elastic). PED = 1 is unitary, PED < 1 is inelastic, and PED = 0 is perfectly inelastic. When demand is elastic, a price rise lowers total revenue.\nThe trap: A (PED = 1) is unitary elasticity, which is the borderline, not \"elastic\".\nMemory tip: Elastic = Exceeds 1; Inelastic = Inside 1.",
 "|Which of the following is a tool of monetary policy?|Government expenditure|Changes in tax rates|Open market operations (buying and selling of government securities)|Subsidies on essential goods": "Monetary policy is action by the central bank (in Nigeria, the CBN) to control money supply, credit and interest rates. Its tools include open market operations (buying and selling government securities such as Treasury bills), the monetary policy rate, the cash reserve ratio, the liquidity ratio, special deposits and moral suasion: C. When the CBN sells securities, money leaves the banks and money supply falls. Government expenditure, tax rates and subsidies are fiscal policy tools, used by the government through the budget.\nMemory tip: Monetary = Money (CBN); Fiscal = Finance of government (budget: tax and spending).",
 "|Inflation that is caused by an increase in production costs (e.g., rising wages or oil prices) is called:|Demand-pull inflation|Cost-push inflation|Imported inflation|Hyperinflation": "Cost-push inflation happens when the costs of production rise, for example higher wages, higher fuel prices after a subsidy removal, costlier imported raw materials or a weaker naira, and firms pass the costs on as higher prices: B. Demand-pull inflation is the opposite cause: too much money chasing too few goods. Imported inflation comes from rising prices of imports, and hyperinflation is extremely rapid inflation (for example over 50% a month).\nThe trap: A (demand-pull) is the other main type; remember it starts from buyers, not costs.\nMemory tip: Cost-push = sellers' costs PUSH prices up; Demand-pull = buyers PULL prices up.",
 "|The 'invisible hand' concept was introduced by:|John Maynard Keynes|Karl Marx|Adam Smith|Milton Friedman": "Adam Smith, the Scottish economist often called the father of economics, wrote \"An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations\" (1776). He argued that when individuals pursue their own interest in free markets, an \"invisible hand\" guides them to produce what society wants. This idea underlies the capitalist (free market) system: C. Keynes (1936) argued for government intervention, Karl Marx inspired socialism, and Milton Friedman is linked with monetarism.\nMemory tip: Smith = 1776 = invisible hand = capitalism.",
 "|Which of the following best describes a 'public good'?|A good sold by the government|A good that is non-excludable and non-rival — everyone can use it without reducing its availability to others|A good given free to the poor|A good with high production costs": "A public good has two features: it is non-rival (my use does not reduce what is left for you) and non-excludable (nobody can be stopped from enjoying it, even if they do not pay): B. Examples are national defence, street lights and lighthouses. Because people can \"free ride\", private firms cannot charge for them, so the government provides them from taxes. A good sold by the government (like NNPC fuel in the past) is not automatically a public good.\nThe trap: A (a good sold by the government) sounds right because of the word \"public\", but the definition depends on non-rivalry and non-excludability.\nMemory tip: Public good = \"can't stop you, can't use it up\".",
 "|An increase in government spending, holding taxes constant, will most likely cause:|A decrease in national income|An increase in national income through the multiplier effect|A fall in aggregate demand|A rise in interest rates only": "Government spending (G) is an injection into the circular flow, like investment. Holding taxes constant, a rise in G increases aggregate demand. The people who receive the money spend part of it, which becomes someone else's income, and so on. Total income rises by more than the first injection: ΔY = multiplier × ΔG, where multiplier = 1/(1 − MPC). For example, with MPC = 0.8, a ₦10bn rise in G raises income by 5 × ₦10bn = ₦50bn: B.\nMemory tip: Injections (I, G, X) raise income; withdrawals (S, T, M) lower it.",
 "|The law of diminishing marginal returns states that:|As more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, total output eventually falls|As more units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, marginal product eventually decreases|Output always increases with more inputs|Fixed costs always decrease": "The law of diminishing returns (variable proportions) says that when more and more units of a variable factor (labour) are added to a fixed factor (land), marginal product eventually falls: B. Example: on 1 hectare, the 1st worker adds 10 bags of maize, the 2nd adds 14, the 3rd adds 8 and the 4th adds 4. Marginal product starts falling after the 2nd worker, while total output still rises. Total output falls only later, when marginal product becomes negative.\nThe trap: A (total output falls) describes a later stage; the law is about MARGINAL product falling first.\nMemory tip: Marginal falls first; total falls last.",
 "|In a closed economy with no government: Y = C + I. If C = 0.7Y and I = ₦500m, find national income (Y).|₦1,000m|₦1,500m|₦1,667m|₦2,000m": "In a two-sector economy, equilibrium income is where Y = C + I.\nStep 1: Substitute: Y = 0.7Y + 500.\nStep 2: Collect Y terms: Y − 0.7Y = 500, so 0.3Y = 500.\nStep 3: Divide: Y = 500 ÷ 0.3 = ₦1,666.67m ≈ ₦1,667m: C.\nCheck with the multiplier: MPC = 0.7, MPS = 0.3, multiplier = 1/0.3 = 3.33, and 3.33 × ₦500m ≈ ₦1,667m.\nThe trap: B (₦1,500m) is what you get by multiplying 500 by 3 instead of dividing by 0.3.\nMemory tip: Y = I ÷ MPS (when C = bY): divide autonomous spending by the leakage.",
 "|Economies of scale refer to:|Cost advantages gained as output increases in the long run|The disadvantage of being too large a firm|The benefits of international trade|Price reductions in a competitive market": "Economies of scale are the cost advantages a firm (or industry) gains when it expands its scale of production in the long run, so that average (unit) cost falls: A. Internal economies come from within the firm: bulk buying, technical (bigger machines), managerial, financial (cheaper loans), marketing and risk-bearing. External economies come from the growth of the industry, such as skilled labour and ancillary firms clustered around Lagos or Aba. Beyond a point, diseconomies of scale make unit cost rise.\nMemory tip: Economies of scale = \"bigger is cheaper per unit\" (until it isn't).",
 "|Which of the following is a regressive tax?|Income tax with higher rates for higher earners|A flat rate tax where all pay the same percentage|A specific commodity tax (e.g., petrol tax) which takes a higher proportion of income from the poor|Inheritance tax": "Taxes are classed by how the tax rate relates to income. Progressive: the rate rises as income rises (PAYE income tax). Proportional: the same percentage for everyone (a flat rate tax). Regressive: the tax takes a larger share of a poor person's income than a rich person's. Indirect taxes on goods everyone buys, such as a tax on petrol or VAT, are regressive in effect because ₦500 of tax is a bigger part of a small income: C.\nThe trap: B (flat rate) is proportional, not regressive, because everyone pays the same percentage.\nMemory tip: Progressive = Poor pay a lower rate; Regressive = Rich pay a lower share.",
 "|The balance of trade is defined as:|Total imports minus total exports of goods and services|The difference between the value of visible exports and visible imports (goods only)|The government budget balance|Foreign exchange reserves": "The balance of trade is the difference between the value of a country's visible exports and visible imports, that is, physical goods only, such as crude oil, cocoa and imported machinery: B. If visible exports exceed visible imports, the balance of trade is favourable (surplus). Services such as shipping, banking, insurance and tourism are invisibles; together with goods they make up the current account of the balance of payments.\nThe trap: A mentions goods AND services and is the wrong way round; that describes neither the balance of trade nor the current account.\nMemory tip: Trade = Things you can see and touch (visibles).",
 "|A devaluation of a country's currency makes its exports:|More expensive abroad and imports cheaper at home|Cheaper abroad and imports more expensive at home|Cheaper both at home and abroad|The same price — devaluation has no effect on trade": "Devaluation is the deliberate lowering of a currency's official exchange rate. If ₦1,000 = $1 becomes ₦1,500 = $1, a foreign buyer's dollar buys more Nigerian goods, so Nigerian exports become cheaper abroad; but a $10 import now costs ₦15,000 instead of ₦10,000, so imports become dearer at home: B. It aims to boost exports and cut imports, helping the balance of payments, but it works only if demand for exports and imports is elastic (Marshall-Lerner condition).\nThe trap: A states the exact opposite and catches students who think a \"weaker\" naira makes exports dearer.\nMemory tip: Devalue = Exports cheaper, imports dearer (\"sell more, buy less\").",
 "|Which of the following is an example of structural unemployment?|A worker laid off because the economy is in recession|A worker between jobs looking for a better position|A coal miner who is unemployed because coal mines have closed permanently due to shift to renewable energy|A seasonal farm worker out of work in winter": "Structural unemployment arises from a lasting change in the structure of the economy, such as new technology, a change in demand or the decline of an industry, leaving workers whose skills are no longer needed: C. In Nigeria, the decline of coal mining at Enugu after oil became the main fuel is a classic example. Cyclical unemployment comes from a recession (A), frictional unemployment from people moving between jobs (B), and seasonal unemployment from the season (D; in Nigeria, farm workers in the dry season).\nMemory tip: Structural = the job itself disappears; frictional = between jobs.",
 "|The demand curve for a Giffen good:|Slopes downward like normal goods|Slopes upward — as price rises, quantity demanded also rises|Is perfectly elastic|Is perfectly inelastic": "A Giffen good is a very inferior staple on which poor households spend a large share of income (the classic example is bread or potatoes for the very poor). When its price rises, they can no longer afford better foods, so they buy MORE of the staple. Quantity demanded rises with price, so the demand curve slopes upward: B. The negative income effect is stronger than the substitution effect. Giffen goods are an exception to the law of demand, along with goods of ostentation and goods bought in expectation of further price rises.\nMemory tip: Giffen = \"Goes up together\" (price and quantity).",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a function of money?|Medium of exchange|Store of value|Unit of account|Source of unlimited wealth": "Money performs four functions: medium of exchange (it removes the need for a double coincidence of wants under barter), unit of account (measure of value), store of value, and standard of deferred payment (for debts and future payments). \"Source of unlimited wealth\" is not a function; money itself is limited and loses value with inflation: D.\nMemory tip: \"Many Useful Services Daily\": Medium, Unit, Store, Deferred payment.",
 "|A monopsony in the labour market refers to:|A single seller of labour (trade union)|A single buyer of labour (employer)|A labour market with many employers|Government control of wages": "In a market, \"mono\" means one and \"-poly\" means seller, while \"-psony\" means buyer. A monopsony is a market with a single buyer; in the labour market, a single employer, for example a large mine or company that is the only employer in a town: B. A monopsonist can pay a wage below what workers would get in a competitive market. A single seller of labour, such as a strong trade union, is a monopoly seller of labour.\nThe trap: A (single seller of labour) is the mirror image; monopsony is the BUYER side.\nMemory tip: monoPSony = one PurchaSer.",
 "|When marginal cost (MC) equals marginal revenue (MR), a firm is:|Making maximum losses|At the profit-maximising level of output|At the shutdown point|At the break-even point only": "Any firm maximises profit (or minimises loss) at the output where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), with MC rising through MR: B. If MR > MC, an extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so the firm should produce more; if MC > MR it should produce less. The shutdown point is where price equals minimum average variable cost, and break-even is where total revenue equals total cost (P = AC), so C and D are different conditions.\nMemory tip: Keep producing while the next unit adds more to revenue than to cost; stop where MC = MR.",
 "|The multiplier in Keynesian economics is calculated as:|1 / (1 − MPC)|MPC / (1 − MPS)|MPS × investment|1 / MPS × income": "The multiplier (k) shows how many times national income rises after an initial rise in spending. k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/MPS: A. Example: MPC = 0.75, so MPS = 0.25 and k = 1/0.25 = 4; a ₦20bn rise in investment raises income by ₦80bn. Option B simplifies to MPC/MPC = 1, and D multiplies the multiplier by income, which is not a formula for k.\nThe trap: D contains 1/MPS, which is correct by itself, but \"× income\" turns it into a wrong formula.\nMemory tip: The bigger the MPC (spending), the bigger the multiplier.",
 "|Comparative advantage in international trade means:|A country should produce what it produces better than all others (absolute advantage)|A country should specialise in producing goods in which it has a lower opportunity cost than other countries|Larger countries always have an advantage in trade|Free trade is always beneficial to all countries": "David Ricardo's principle of comparative advantage says a country should specialise in the good it can produce at a lower opportunity cost than others, even if another country is better at producing everything (absolute advantage): B. Example: if Nigeria gives up 2 units of cloth for 1 tonne of cocoa while Britain gives up 4, Nigeria has the comparative advantage in cocoa and should export it. Trade then lets both countries consume more.\nThe trap: A describes absolute advantage (Adam Smith), the closely related idea.\nMemory tip: Comparative = Compare opportunity costs; lowest cost wins.",
 "|Which of the following is an automatic stabiliser in an economy?|Discretionary government spending on infrastructure|Progressive income taxes and unemployment benefits|Central bank's decision to change interest rates|Government's decision to nationalise industries": "Automatic (built-in) stabilisers are parts of the budget that change by themselves with the economy, without any new decision. In a boom, incomes rise, people move into higher tax bands under progressive taxation and fewer people claim unemployment benefits, so spending is cooled; in a recession the reverse happens: B. Discretionary spending on infrastructure and nationalisation require deliberate government decisions, and interest rate changes are monetary policy.\nThe trap: A (discretionary spending) is fiscal policy too, but it needs a decision, so it is not automatic.\nMemory tip: Automatic = \"on autopilot\": taxes and benefits adjust without a vote.",
 "|Stagflation refers to:|Low inflation and economic stagnation|High unemployment, high inflation, and slow or negative economic growth occurring simultaneously|Rapid economic growth with deflation|A budget surplus during recession": "Stagflation = stagnation + inflation: rising prices at the same time as slow or negative growth and high unemployment: B. It is hard to cure because policies that reduce inflation tend to deepen unemployment. It was seen worldwide after the 1970s oil price shocks, and Nigeria experienced it in 2016–2017 and again in recent years, with double-digit inflation alongside weak growth and high unemployment.\nThe trap: A says LOW inflation; stagflation needs HIGH inflation.\nMemory tip: STAG (no growth) + FLATION (rising prices).",
 "|The World Bank and IMF were established at the:|Yalta Conference (1945)|Bretton Woods Conference (1944)|San Francisco Conference (1945)|Paris Peace Conference (1919)": "In July 1944, delegates from 44 countries met at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA, to plan the post-war economy. They created the International Monetary Fund (IMF), to help countries with balance of payments problems and keep exchange rates stable, and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD, part of the World Bank Group), to finance reconstruction and development: B. These are called the Bretton Woods institutions. Nigeria joined both in 1961.\nThe trap: A (Yalta 1945) and C (San Francisco 1945, which founded the UN) are close in date and both about the post-war order.\nMemory tip: Bretton Woods 1944 = \"B for Banks\" (IMF and World Bank).",
 "|If supply is perfectly inelastic, the supply curve is:|Horizontal|Upward sloping at 45°|Vertical|Downward sloping": "Price elasticity of supply (PES) = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price. If supply is perfectly inelastic (PES = 0), quantity supplied stays the same whatever the price, so the supply curve is vertical: C. Examples are land in a city centre, seats in a stadium on match day, or an antique. A horizontal curve is perfectly elastic (PES = infinity).\nThe trap: A (horizontal) is perfectly ELASTIC; students often swap the two.\nMemory tip: Inelastic = I (a vertical line, like the letter I); Elastic = flat like the horizon.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of an external cost (negative externality)?|A factory that trains workers who then work elsewhere|Air pollution from a factory that harms local residents|A beekeeper whose bees pollinate neighbouring farms|Education benefits that increase social productivity": "An externality is a cost or benefit that falls on a third party who is not part of the transaction. A negative externality (external cost) harms others: smoke and noise from a factory, gas flaring and oil spills in the Niger Delta, or a generator next door: B. The other options are positive externalities (external benefits): workers trained by one firm, bees pollinating a neighbour's farm, and the social gains from education. Social cost = private cost + external cost.\nMemory tip: Externality = a \"side effect\" on outsiders; negative = harm, positive = gain.",
 "|The Phillips Curve illustrates the relationship between:|Inflation and output|Unemployment and inflation — showing an inverse trade-off|Interest rates and investment|Money supply and price levels": "A. W. Phillips (1958) found, using British data, that when unemployment was low, wages (and so prices) rose fast, and when unemployment was high, they rose slowly. The Phillips curve therefore shows an inverse relationship, a trade-off, between unemployment and inflation: B. A government could buy lower unemployment at the cost of higher inflation. Stagflation in the 1970s showed that this trade-off can break down in the long run.\nThe trap: A (inflation and output) is close because output and unemployment are linked, but the curve is drawn with unemployment.\nMemory tip: Phillips: Prices up, unemployment down (and vice versa).",
 "|In economics, the term 'ceteris paribus' means:|With all things changed|Holding all other factors constant (all other things being equal)|In the long run|Under monopoly conditions": "\"Ceteris paribus\" is Latin for \"other things being equal\". Economists use it to study the effect of one variable while holding all other influences constant: B. For example, the law of demand says that, ceteris paribus, a rise in the price of garri reduces the quantity demanded; we hold income, tastes and the prices of rice and yam constant so we can see the price effect alone.\nMemory tip: Ceteris paribus = \"keep everything else still\".",
 "|Which of the following is the most liquid asset?|Real estate property|Shares in a company|Government bonds|Cash (currency in circulation)": "Liquidity is how quickly and easily an asset can be turned into cash without loss of value. Cash is money itself, so it is perfectly liquid: D. Government bonds and Treasury bills can be sold quickly but may lose value; shares depend on the stock market price; land and buildings take a long time to sell and are the least liquid. Keynes's liquidity preference theory is about why people want to hold cash.\nMemory tip: Liquidity ladder: cash → deposits → Treasury bills/bonds → shares → land.",
 "|A merit good is one that the government believes:|The market will overproduce|Individuals undervalue and underconsume relative to the socially optimal level, justifying government provision or subsidy|Should be taxed heavily|Only the rich should consume": "A merit good is one the government believes people undervalue and so consume too little of if left to the market, often because they lack information about its benefits; such goods also bring wider benefits to society. Examples are education, health care and immunisation, so government provides or subsidises them (for example Universal Basic Education in Nigeria): B. The opposite is a demerit good, such as cigarettes or alcohol, which is overconsumed and often taxed heavily.\nMemory tip: Merit = Most people should have More (underconsumed); Demerit = too much.",
 "|The 'terms of trade' of a country refer to:|The legal conditions governing international trade agreements|The ratio of export prices to import prices — indicating purchasing power of exports in terms of imports|The total value of exports|The tariff rates applied to imports": "The terms of trade compare the prices of a country's exports with the prices of its imports. Terms of trade = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100: B. Example: export price index 120, import price index 100: (120 ÷ 100) × 100 = 120, a favourable movement, since each unit of exports now buys more imports. When the price of crude oil falls, Nigeria's terms of trade usually worsen.\nMemory tip: Terms of trade = Export prices OVER import prices (E over I).",
 "|Dumping in international trade occurs when:|A country bans all imports|A country exports goods at a price below the cost of production or below the domestic price|A country imposes high import tariffs|A multinational corporation repatriates profits": "Dumping is selling goods in a foreign market at a price below their cost of production or below the price charged at home: B. Firms may do it to get rid of surplus output or to drive out local competitors. Dumped imports such as cheap textiles can harm Nigerian industries, so governments impose anti-dumping duties, and the WTO allows such duties when dumping causes injury.\nMemory tip: Dumping = \"dumping\" goods abroad cheaply.",
 "|If the government imposes a price ceiling BELOW the equilibrium price, the result is:|A surplus (excess supply)|A shortage (excess demand)|No change in the market|Higher prices": "A price ceiling (maximum price) is the highest price sellers are legally allowed to charge. Set below the equilibrium price, it makes quantity demanded greater than quantity supplied, so there is a shortage (excess demand): B. This leads to queues, rationing, hoarding and black markets, as was seen with controlled petrol prices in Nigeria. A minimum price (price floor), set above equilibrium, causes a surplus.\nThe trap: A (surplus) is the effect of a minimum price set above equilibrium.\nMemory tip: Ceiling below → Shortage; Floor above → Surplus.",
 "|The Lorenz curve is used to measure:|Economic growth over time|The distribution of income or wealth — its deviation from perfect equality|Inflation rates|Government budget deficits": "The Lorenz curve shows how income (or wealth) is shared in a country. It plots the cumulative percentage of households (poorest first) against the cumulative percentage of income they receive. The 45° line is perfect equality; the further the curve bends away from it, the greater the inequality: B. The Gini coefficient measures that gap from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality).\nMemory tip: Lorenz = Line of equality vs the bend of inequality.",
 "|The concept of 'consumer sovereignty' means:|The government determines what consumers buy|Consumers, through their purchasing decisions, ultimately determine what is produced in a free market|Consumers have no choice in the market|Producers dictate prices to consumers": "Consumer sovereignty means that in a free market the consumer is \"king\": by spending money on some goods and not others, consumers signal what firms should produce. Goods people buy earn profit and expand; goods they reject disappear: B. It is a feature of the capitalist (market) system. It is limited by advertising, monopoly, government controls and low incomes.\nMemory tip: Consumers \"vote\" with their naira.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a natural monopoly?|A supermarket chain|A taxi company|A water supply or electricity distribution network|A pharmaceutical company": "A natural monopoly arises when the fixed cost of the network is so large, and economies of scale so great, that one firm can supply the whole market at lower average cost than two or more firms could. Examples are water supply pipes, electricity transmission lines and railways: C. In Nigeria, the Transmission Company of Nigeria is still government owned, while electricity distribution is now done by privatised DisCos after NEPA/PHCN was unbundled in 2013.\nMemory tip: Natural monopoly = one set of pipes or wires is enough.",
 "|The concept of diminishing marginal utility states that:|Total utility always decreases as consumption increases|As a person consumes more units of a good, the additional satisfaction from each extra unit eventually declines|A good becomes cheaper as more is consumed|Marginal utility is always zero": "Utility is the satisfaction from consuming a good. Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. The law of diminishing marginal utility says that, other things equal, as a person consumes more units, the MU of each extra unit eventually falls: B. Example: the first sachet of water on a hot day gives great satisfaction, the fourth much less. Total utility keeps rising while MU is positive; it is highest when MU = 0.\nThe trap: A says TOTAL utility falls; it is MARGINAL utility that diminishes.\nMemory tip: Each extra bite is less exciting than the last.",
 "|In which market structure does a firm act as a price-taker?|Monopoly|Oligopoly|Monopolistic competition|Perfect competition": "A price-taker is a firm that must accept the market price because it is too small to influence it. Under perfect competition there are many firms selling an identical product, so if one firm raised its price it would lose all its customers: D. Its demand curve is horizontal, so price = AR = MR. Monopolists, oligopolists and firms in monopolistic competition are price-makers to some extent.\nMemory tip: Perfect competition: Price is Given.",
 "|The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures:|The total output of an economy|The average change in prices paid by consumers for a basket of goods and services over time|Corporate profit margins|Government expenditure": "The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures the average change over time in the prices of a fixed basket of goods and services bought by households, compared with a base year (= 100). In Nigeria, the NBS publishes it monthly, and the inflation rate is the percentage change in the CPI: B. Example: CPI rises from 200 to 230: inflation = (30 ÷ 200) × 100 = 15%.\nMemory tip: CPI = the price \"thermometer\" of a household's shopping basket.",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of a public good?|It is rival in consumption and excludable|It is non-rival and non-excludable|It generates no externalities|It is only provided by the government": "Public goods are non-rival (one person's use does not reduce what is available to others) and non-excludable (nobody can be prevented from using them): B. Examples are street lights, national defence and lighthouses. Because people can enjoy them without paying (the free-rider problem), the market will not provide them, so government does. Option D is a result, not a feature: not everything provided by government is a public good, and some public goods could in principle be provided privately.\nThe trap: D (only provided by the government) sounds right but is not the defining characteristic.\nMemory tip: Public good: non-rival + non-excludable.",
 "|The formula for calculating GDP using the income approach is:|GDP = C + I + G + (X−M)|GDP = Wages + Rent + Interest + Profit|GDP = Total Output × Price Level|GDP = Government Spending + Private Consumption": "National income can be measured three ways: output (value added by each sector), expenditure (C + I + G + (X − M)) and income. The income method adds the rewards to the factors of production: wages and salaries (labour), rent (land), interest (capital) and profit (entrepreneur): B. This gives GDP at factor cost; add indirect taxes and subtract subsidies to get GDP at market prices. Transfer payments such as pensions and gifts are excluded because they are not payments for production.\nThe trap: A (C + I + G + (X − M)) is a correct formula but for the EXPENDITURE method.\nMemory tip: Income method = factor rewards: Wages, Rent, Interest, Profit (\"WRIP\").",
 "|Price elasticity of supply measures:|How consumer income affects quantity demanded|The responsiveness of quantity supplied to a change in price|How price affects profits|The relationship between supply and demand": "Price elasticity of supply (PES) measures how much the quantity supplied responds to a change in price: PES = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price: B. Example: price rises from ₦100 to ₦120 (20%) and quantity supplied rises from 50 to 60 units (20%): PES = 20 ÷ 20 = 1 (unitary). Supply is more elastic in the long run, when goods can be stored, and when spare capacity exists. Option A describes income elasticity of demand.\nMemory tip: Elasticity always = % change in quantity ÷ % change in the cause.",
 "|Devaluation of a country's currency means:|The official reduction in the exchange rate of the currency against other currencies in a fixed exchange rate system|The currency becomes stronger|Inflation automatically falls|Exports become more expensive": "Devaluation is an official decision by the government or central bank to lower the value of its currency in a fixed exchange rate system: A. In a floating system, a fall in value caused by market forces is called depreciation. Devaluation makes exports cheaper and imports dearer. Nigeria devalued the naira under SAP in 1986 (through SFEM); since 2023 the naira has been allowed to float, so recent falls are depreciation.\nThe trap: D (exports become more expensive) is the reverse of the effect.\nMemory tip: DEvaluation = DEcision (fixed rate); DEpreciation = market drift (floating rate).",
 "|The 'invisible hand' concept introduced by Adam Smith suggests:|Government should control the economy|Individuals pursuing self-interest in a free market inadvertently promote the social good|Markets always fail without regulation|Wages should be set by unions": "Adam Smith in \"The Wealth of Nations\" (1776) argued that people serving their own interest, for example a baker baking bread to earn profit, are led \"as if by an invisible hand\" to serve the needs of society, without government direction: B. This is the basis of laissez-faire capitalism, where the price mechanism allocates resources. Critics point to market failures such as monopoly, externalities and public goods, which justify some government action.\nMemory tip: Invisible hand = the price mechanism working on its own.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a fiscal policy tool?|Setting the repo rate|Adjusting the reserve ratio|Changing the corporation tax rate|Open market operations": "Fiscal policy is the use of government revenue (taxation) and expenditure to influence the economy. Raising or lowering the companies income tax rate changes firms' after-tax profits and so their investment, so it is a fiscal tool: C. The monetary policy rate (repo rate in some countries), the reserve ratio and open market operations are monetary tools used by the CBN. In Nigeria, fiscal policy is set by the Federal Ministry of Finance through the annual budget passed by the National Assembly.\nMemory tip: Fiscal = Federal budget (tax and spending); Monetary = CBN.",
 "|The term 'balance of payments' refers to:|A government's budget balance|A systematic record of all economic transactions between a country's residents and the rest of the world over a period|The trade deficit only|The amount of foreign currency reserves held by the central bank": "The balance of payments (BOP) is a systematic record of all economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world over a period, usually a year: B. It has the current account (visible trade, invisibles such as services, income and transfers), the capital and financial account (investment and loans), and official reserves. In accounting terms it always balances; a \"deficit\" means a deficit before official financing. The CBN compiles Nigeria's BOP.\nThe trap: C (the trade deficit only) confuses the BOP with the balance of trade, which is only one part of it.\nMemory tip: Balance of TRADE = goods only; balance of PAYMENTS = everything.",
 "|Which of the following best describes the concept of 'crowding out' in macroeconomics?|Increased government borrowing raises interest rates, reducing private sector investment|Private firms crowd out government companies from the market|Inflation crowds out savings|High taxes crowd out consumption": "When the government borrows heavily to finance a budget deficit, it competes with private firms for the limited loanable funds. This pushes interest rates up, and firms then borrow and invest less. Government spending has \"crowded out\" private investment: A. In Nigeria, high-yield government bonds and Treasury bills can draw bank funds away from lending to businesses. Crowding out weakens the multiplier effect of government spending.\nMemory tip: Crowding out = government borrows, interest rises, private investment falls.",
 "|A negative income elasticity of demand (YED < 0) indicates:|A luxury good|A normal good|An inferior good|A Giffen good": "Income elasticity of demand (YED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income. If YED is negative, quantity demanded falls as income rises: the good is an inferior good, such as cheap local substitutes people abandon as they get richer: C. Normal goods have positive YED; luxuries have YED greater than 1.\nThe trap: D (Giffen good) is close because every Giffen good is inferior, but a negative YED alone does not make a good Giffen; a Giffen good also needs an upward-sloping demand curve.\nMemory tip: YED sign: + normal, > 1 luxury, − inferior.",
 "|The equilibrium wage in a perfectly competitive labour market is determined by:|The government setting a minimum wage|The intersection of labour demand (MRP of labour) and labour supply|Trade union negotiations only|The cost of living index": "In a perfectly competitive labour market, many firms hire many workers and no one can set the wage. The demand for labour is a derived demand, given by the marginal revenue product of labour (MRP = MP × MR). The supply of labour comes from workers. The wage settles where the demand and supply curves intersect: B. Minimum wages (such as Nigeria's national minimum wage of ₦70,000 set in 2024) and trade union bargaining are interventions that move wages away from this market wage.\nMemory tip: Wage = price of labour, set like any price: demand meets supply.",
 "|Which of the following is a measure of economic development (not just growth)?|Real GDP growth rate|Human Development Index (HDI)|Total exports|Government budget surplus": "Economic growth is a rise in real GDP; economic development is wider: better living standards, less poverty, better health and education. The Human Development Index (HDI), published by the UNDP since 1990, combines life expectancy (health), years of schooling (education) and GNI per capita (income), giving a value from 0 to 1: B. Nigeria's HDI is low (about 0.55), even though its GDP is one of the largest in Africa. Real GDP growth measures growth, not development.\nThe trap: A (real GDP growth rate) is the standard measure of GROWTH, which the question excludes.\nMemory tip: HDI = Health, Degrees (education), Income.",
 "|The law of demand states:|As price rises, quantity demanded rises (other things equal)|As price falls, quantity demanded rises (other things equal)|Supply always equals demand|Demand is unaffected by price changes": "The law of demand states that, other things being equal, the lower the price of a good, the higher the quantity demanded, and the higher the price, the lower the quantity demanded: B. This inverse relationship gives a demand curve that slopes downward from left to right. A change in price moves the buyer along the curve; a change in income, tastes or prices of related goods shifts the whole curve. Exceptions include Giffen goods and goods of ostentation.\nThe trap: A states the opposite (a direct relationship), which describes the law of supply.\nMemory tip: Demand: price and quantity move in opposite directions (a \"see-saw\").",
 "|If the price elasticity of demand for a good is 0.43, an increase in the price of the good will result in|an increase in profit by 43%|a net gain|a decrease in profit by 43%|a net loss": "Price elasticity of demand measures how strongly quantity demanded responds to a price change. A value of 0.43 is less than 1, so demand is inelastic: if price rises by 10%, quantity falls by only about 4.3%. Because the price rise outweighs the fall in sales, total revenue (price × quantity) goes up, so the seller makes a net gain: B. Options A and C misread 0.43 as a 43% change in profit, but elasticity is a ratio of percentage changes, not a profit figure. Memory tip: Inelastic demand: raise the price, raise the revenue; elastic demand: cut the price to raise revenue.",
 "|In a free market economy, the price system allocates resources|under government's directives|to ensure general welfare|to their best alternative uses|to reduce poverty": "In a free market (capitalist) economy there is no central planner. Prices act as signals: when consumers want more of a good its price and profits rise, attracting land, labour and capital into producing it, and resources leave goods people want less. In this way the price mechanism (the \"invisible hand\" described by Adam Smith) moves resources to their best alternative uses, where they earn the highest reward: C. Government directives (A) describe a command economy, while general welfare and poverty reduction (B, D) are social aims that the market does not guarantee. Memory tip: Prices are the market's traffic lights, directing resources where they are most wanted.",
 "|At consumer equilibrium, the slope of the indifference curve is|half the slope of the budget constraint line|equal to the slope of the budget constraint line|greater than the slope of the budget constraint line|less than the slope of the budget constraint line": "An indifference curve shows combinations of two goods giving equal satisfaction; its slope is the marginal rate of substitution (MRS). The budget line shows what the consumer can afford; its slope is the ratio of the two prices. The consumer reaches the highest possible satisfaction where the budget line just touches (is tangent to) an indifference curve. At a point of tangency the two lines have the same slope, so MRS = Px/Py: B. If the slopes differed, the consumer could reshuffle spending and move to a higher indifference curve. Memory tip: Equilibrium = Tangency = Equal slopes (MRS = price ratio).",
 "|For a supply curve, an increase in the price of a commodity will result in|a decrease in supply|a decrease in the quantity supplied|an increase in supply|an increase in the quantity supplied": "Economists separate two ideas. A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the supply curve, called a change in quantity supplied. A change in anything else (costs, technology, weather, taxes) shifts the whole curve, called a change in supply. Since the supply curve slopes upward, a higher price moves the firm up the curve to a larger quantity: an increase in the quantity supplied: D. The trap: C, \"an increase in supply\", sounds almost the same but means a rightward shift of the whole curve, which a price change does not cause. Memory tip: Own price = movement (quantity supplied); other factors = shift (supply).",
 "|A normal good with close substitutes is likely to have price elasticity of demand|between zero and one|equal to unity|less than unity|greater than unity": "Elasticity of demand depends mainly on the availability of substitutes. If a good has close substitutes, a rise in its price makes buyers switch quickly to the alternatives, so quantity demanded falls by a larger percentage than the price rose. That is elastic demand, with a coefficient greater than one: D. Examples are different brands of soap or soft drinks. Options A and C (between zero and one, less than unity) describe inelastic demand, typical of necessities with few substitutes such as salt; B (unity) is the special case where percentage changes are equal. Memory tip: More Substitutes = More Sensitive (elastic, greater than 1).",
 "|Economics is regarded as a social science because it|adopts the scientific method in production|is an agent of socialization|adopts the scientific method in the study of human behaviour|deals with social problems": "A social science studies people in society using a systematic, scientific approach. Economics studies how people behave in using scarce resources to satisfy wants, and it does this by the scientific method: observing facts, forming hypotheses, and testing them to build laws such as the law of demand. Because its subject is human behaviour and its method is scientific, it is a social science: C. Option A wrongly places the scientific method \"in production\"; B describes institutions like the family or school; D is too vague, since many subjects deal with social problems. Memory tip: Social (human behaviour) + Science (scientific method) = social science.",
 "|The diagram shows the total utility curve. At the point M, marginal utility|increases|is unity|diminishes|is zero": "Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit. As long as total utility (TU) is rising, MU is positive; when TU is at its maximum, MU is zero; when TU falls, MU is negative. In the diagram, M is the peak of the total utility curve, so the next unit adds nothing: marginal utility is zero: D. Beyond M the curve falls, showing negative marginal utility. The trap: C, \"diminishes\", describes MU on the rising part of the curve before M, not at the peak itself. Memory tip: Top of TU = Zero MU.",
 "|A change in supply is best described as a|movement along the supply curve|shift of the supply curve to the left or to the right|shift of the supply curve to the left|shift of the supply curve to the right": "A change in supply means the whole supply schedule changes because of a non-price factor such as cost of production, technology, weather or taxation. On a graph this is a shift of the supply curve: to the right when supply increases and to the left when supply decreases. Since the question asks generally about a change, the best description includes both directions: B. A movement along the curve (A) is a change in quantity supplied caused by the good's own price. The trap: C and D are each half true, because each names only one direction of shift. Memory tip: \"Change in supply\" = the curve moves house, left or right.",
 "|When a generalization is made based on observed facts, it is known as|deductive reasoning|inductive reasoning|theoretical reasoning|normative reasoning": "Economists use two kinds of reasoning. Inductive reasoning starts from particular observed facts and draws a general conclusion; for example, after observing many markets where buyers bought less at higher prices, economists generalised this into the law of demand. Deductive reasoning works the other way, from general assumptions to particular conclusions. A generalisation built from observed facts is therefore inductive reasoning: B. Normative reasoning (D) involves value judgements about what ought to be. The trap: A, deductive reasoning, is the closely related opposite method and is often mixed up with induction. Memory tip: INduction brings facts IN to build a rule; DEduction DErives facts from a rule.",
 "|A major problem facing all economies is how to|increase consumption of imported goods|improve trade among nations|transform from a developing to a developed economy|allocate scarce resources": "The basic economic problem arises because human wants are unlimited while the resources to satisfy them (land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) are scarce. Every economy, whether capitalist, socialist or mixed, developed or developing, must therefore decide what to produce, how to produce it and for whom, which together mean deciding how to allocate scarce resources among competing uses: D. Increasing imports, improving trade or becoming developed (A, B, C) are goals of particular countries at particular times, not problems facing all economies. Memory tip: Scarcity forces Choice, and choice is about Allocation.",
 "|A vertical supply curve indicates that|the quantity supplied is responsive to price|the same fixed quantity will be supplied no matter the price|an increase in price will result in an increase in the quantity supplied|there is a fixed price for the commodity below which no supply will be made": "A vertical supply curve means that whatever the price, the same quantity is offered for sale. Price elasticity of supply is zero (perfectly inelastic). Examples are land in a fixed area, seats in a stadium on match day, or perishable goods already brought to market that must be sold. So the curve shows that the same fixed quantity is supplied no matter the price: B. Options A and C describe a normal upward-sloping supply curve, and D describes a supply price below which nothing is offered, which a vertical line does not show. Memory tip: Vertical = Very fixed (elasticity zero); horizontal = infinitely elastic.",
 "|In a demand curve the relationship between price and quantity is|indeterminate|direct|nil|inverse": "The law of demand states that, other things being equal, the higher the price of a good the smaller the quantity demanded, and the lower the price the greater the quantity demanded. Price and quantity move in opposite directions, which is an inverse relationship, so the demand curve slopes downward from left to right: D. A direct (positive) relationship (B) is what we find on a supply curve. Exceptions such as Giffen goods or goods of ostentation are rare and do not change the general rule. Memory tip: Demand Down-slopes: price up, quantity down.",
 "|If the arithmetic mean of 1, 2, 5, 6, x, 16 and 18 is 8.0, find the value of x.|7.0|6.0|8.5|8.0": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of the values divided by the number of values, so the sum = mean × number.\nStep 1: There are 7 values (1, 2, 5, 6, x, 16, 18), so the total must be 8.0 × 7 = 56.\nStep 2: Add the known values: 1 + 2 + 5 + 6 + 16 + 18 = 48.\nStep 3: x = 56 − 48 = 8.0: D.\nCheck: (48 + 8) ÷ 7 = 56 ÷ 7 = 8. Memory tip: when a mean is given, rebuild the total first (mean × n), then subtract what you know.",
 "|The fixing of the price of an item above or below the equilibrium price is most likely to take place in a|centrally planned economy|free market economy|developed economy|mixed economy": "In a free market, prices are set by demand and supply and settle at equilibrium. In a centrally planned (command) economy, a central authority decides what to produce and also fixes prices administratively. These official prices are not set by the market, so they are usually above or below the equilibrium price, leading to surpluses or shortages, queues and rationing, as happened in the former Soviet Union: A. Mixed economies also use occasional price controls, but fixing prices is the normal practice only under central planning. A free market economy (B) by definition lets prices find equilibrium. Memory tip: Command economy = Commanded prices.",
 "|A consumer's scale of preference is an arrangement of his|scarce resources in order of importance|needs in order of importance|sources of income and their importance|requirement and how to satisfy them": "Because income is limited, a consumer cannot satisfy all his wants at once. A scale of preference is a list of his needs (wants) arranged in order of importance or urgency, so that he satisfies the most pressing first and leaves the less urgent for later. For a student it might be food, rent, textbooks, then new clothes. So it is an arrangement of his needs in order of importance: B. The wants left out when he chooses the first item on the list are his opportunity cost. Option A confuses wants with resources. Memory tip: Scale of preference = needs ranked from Most to Least urgent.",
 "|A firm's shut-down point is reached when the average revenue fails to cover the|average variable cost|marginal cost|average total cost|average fixed cost": "In the short run a firm has fixed costs that it must pay whether it produces or not. It should keep producing as long as price (average revenue) covers its average variable cost, because any revenue above AVC helps to pay part of the fixed costs. If AR falls below AVC, producing adds to the loss, so the firm shuts down: A. The trap: C, average total cost, gives the break-even point (normal profit), not the shut-down point; a firm can keep running for a while while covering only AVC. Memory tip: AVC is the \"stay open\" line; ATC is the \"break even\" line.",
 "|Internal economies of scale are expected to bring about|an increase in short-run average cost|an increase in long-run average cost|a decrease in long-run average cost|a decrease in short-run average cost": "Internal economies of scale are cost savings a firm enjoys as it grows larger, such as technical (bigger, better machines), managerial (specialised managers), financial (cheaper loans), marketing (bulk buying) and risk-bearing economies. Growing in size means changing all factors, including plant and buildings, which happens only in the long run. These economies therefore make the long-run average cost (LRAC) curve fall as output expands: C. The trap: D, \"a decrease in short-run average cost\", sounds right because costs do fall, but in the short run the firm's scale is fixed, so scale economies cannot apply. Memory tip: Scale changes in the Long run, so economies of scale lower Long-run average cost.",
 "|In the long-run a monopolist maximizes his profit when the marginal cost equals|total revenue|marginal revenue|average cost|price": "Every firm maximises profit where marginal cost (MC) equals marginal revenue (MR): if MR is above MC, producing more adds to profit; if MC is above MR, producing less does. For a monopolist the demand curve slopes downward, so MR is below price, and the monopolist charges a price above MC. The equilibrium condition is still MC = MR: B. The trap: D, price, is the rule P = MC that holds only for a perfectly competitive firm, where price and MR are the same. Memory tip: MC = MR for all firms; P = MC only under perfect competition.",
 "|The long run is a period during which a firm|sells input to purchase fixed asset|varies all its inputs|sources all its inputs from within|replaces all its inputs": "Economists divide time by how far a firm can change its inputs. In the short run some factors (such as buildings and machinery) are fixed and only variable inputs like labour and raw materials can be changed. The long run is a period long enough for the firm to vary all its inputs, including plant size, so there are no fixed costs: B. The very long run also allows technology to change. Options A, C and D describe particular business actions, not a time period in economic analysis. Memory tip: Short run: Some fixed; Long run: aLL variable.",
 "|An important feature of perfect competition is that|the movement of goods and services is restricted|there is adequate knowledge of existing prices|prices are centrally administered|individual economic units can influence prices": "Perfect competition has these features: many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous product, free entry and exit, free mobility of factors, and perfect knowledge of market conditions, especially prices. Because every buyer and seller knows the ruling price, no seller can charge more without losing all customers. So adequate knowledge of existing prices is a feature: B. Restricted movement (A) and centrally administered prices (C) are the opposite of a free, perfect market, and in perfect competition each firm is a price taker that cannot influence price (D). Memory tip: Perfect competition = Perfect knowledge, Price takers.",
 "|If government fixes price below the equilibrium price, what effect will it have on demand?|Quantity demanded and supplied will be equal.|Quantity supplied will be greater than quantity demanded.|Quantity demanded will increase.|Quantity demanded will decrease.": "A maximum (ceiling) price fixed below equilibrium makes the good cheaper than the market price. By the law of demand, a lower price raises the quantity demanded, while producers offer less because the price is less rewarding. Demand therefore exceeds supply and a shortage appears, often leading to queues, rationing and black markets. The effect on demand is that quantity demanded increases: C. Quantity supplied greater than quantity demanded (B) is the surplus that follows a minimum price above equilibrium, and equality (A) occurs only at equilibrium. Memory tip: Ceiling below equilibrium = Shortage; floor above equilibrium = Surplus.",
 "|An imperfect market exists where|the product is homogenous|there is perfect information among the few buyers and sellers|both buyers and sellers have free entry into and free exit from the market|the location of some sellers gives them an advantage over others": "A perfect market requires a homogeneous product, perfect information, many buyers and sellers, and free entry and exit, so that one price rules everywhere. An imperfect market exists when any of these conditions fails. If some sellers are better located, for example a shop near a bus park or a filling station on a busy road, customers prefer them and they can charge a little more, so different prices exist for the same good: D. Options A and C are features of perfect competition, and B describes perfect information. Memory tip: Location, branding and ignorance make markets imperfect.",
 "|A production function relates|cost to output|wage to profit|cost to input|output to input": "A production function shows the technical relationship between the quantity of output a firm produces and the quantities of inputs (land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) it uses, written Q = f(L, K, ...). It tells how much output a given combination of inputs can produce, and it underlies the laws of returns. So it relates output to input: D. The trap: A, \"cost to output\", describes a cost function, a closely related idea that comes from the production function once input prices are known. Memory tip: Production function = Physical quantities (inputs in, output out); cost function = money.",
 "|The table below shows demand schedule for eggs: \nPrice per dozen (₦) ... Qty demanded (in dozen) ... Qty. supplied (in dozen)\n100 ... 410 ... 570\n90 ... 450 ... 530\n80 ... 490 ... 490\n70 ... 530 ... 450\n60 ... 570 ... 410\n\nWhat is the equilibrium price?|₦100|₦110|₦60|₦80": "Equilibrium price is the price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, so the market clears.\nStep 1: Compare the two columns row by row: at ₦100, 410 vs 570 (surplus); at ₦90, 450 vs 530 (surplus); at ₦80, 490 vs 490 (equal); at ₦70, 530 vs 450 (shortage); at ₦60, 570 vs 410 (shortage).\nStep 2: The quantities match only at ₦80, with 490 dozen bought and sold: D.\nAbove ₦80 there is a surplus and below it a shortage, which pushes price back to ₦80. ₦110 (B) is not in the table at all. Memory tip: equilibrium is the row where the two quantity columns read the same.",
 "|A major advantage of specialization and division of labour is that|time wastage will be minimized|trade unions will be encouraged|there will be a ready market for labour|workers will be adequately rewarded": "Division of labour breaks production into small tasks, with each worker specialising in one task. Its advantages include greater skill through repetition, use of machines, increased output, and saving of time, because workers no longer move from one job or tool to another. So a major advantage is that time wastage is minimised: A. Encouraging trade unions (B) is not a purpose of specialisation, and specialisation does not guarantee a ready market for labour or adequate pay (C, D); in fact over-specialised workers may find it hard to change jobs. Memory tip: Specialisation Saves time, Sharpens skill, Swells output.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a fixed cost?|Fuel cost.|Electricity bill.|Rent on building.|Transportation cost.": "Fixed costs do not change with the level of output in the short run; they must be paid even when output is zero. Examples are rent on buildings, insurance premiums, interest on loans and salaries of permanent staff. Rent on a factory building is paid whether the firm produces nothing or works at full capacity: C. Fuel, electricity and transport costs rise as the firm produces more, so they are variable costs. The trap: B, the electricity bill, is often thought fixed because it arrives regularly, but the amount rises with the machines and hours used. Memory tip: if you would still pay it with the factory shut, it is a fixed cost.",
 "|In the agricultural sector, improved extension services facilitate|acquisition of techniques|arable crop production|mobility of labour|livestock production": "Agricultural extension services link research institutes and farmers. Extension officers visit farmers, run demonstration plots and give advice so that farmers learn and adopt improved techniques such as better seeds, fertiliser use, pest control and storage methods. Their central role is therefore to help farmers acquire modern techniques: A. These techniques may then raise arable or livestock output (B, D), but those are results in particular branches, not what extension services themselves provide. In Nigeria, the Agricultural Development Programmes (ADPs) were the main channel for extension work. Memory tip: Extension = Education of farmers in new techniques.",
 "|A disadvantage of concentrating industries in an area is that it could|lead to collusion among the firms|result in environmental pollution|bring about diseconomies of scale|increase the cost of production": "Localisation of industry is the concentration of many firms in one area, such as industrial estates in Lagos or Kano. It brings benefits (skilled labour, ancillary firms, banking facilities) but also problems: congestion, housing shortages, rising land costs, regional imbalance and, especially, environmental pollution from smoke, noise, chemicals and waste discharged by many factories in one place: B. Concentration does not by itself cause collusion (A), and costs often fall rather than rise because of external economies (D). Memory tip: Many factories in one place = Much pollution in one place.",
 "|The losses suffered by a sole proprietor are|usually equal to the amount invested|usually less than the amount invested|limited to the amount invested|not limited to the amount invested": "A sole proprietorship is owned and controlled by one person and is not a separate legal entity from its owner. The owner has unlimited liability: if the business cannot pay its debts, creditors can claim the owner's personal property, such as his house or car. His losses are therefore not limited to the amount he invested: D. Limited liability (C) belongs to shareholders of a limited company, who can lose only what they paid for their shares. Partners in an ordinary partnership also have unlimited liability. Memory tip: Sole trader = Sole risk, with no limit.",
 "|To facilitate the exportation of crude oil from Nigeria special|airstrips were constructed for jets to evacuate the oil|roads were built to connect the major oil wells to the ports|pipelines were laid to connect the major oil wells to the ports|railway lines were constructed to connect the major oil wells to the ports": "Nigeria's crude oil is produced mostly in the Niger Delta, onshore and offshore. To export it, oil companies laid a network of pipelines carrying the crude from the oil fields to export terminals and ports such as Bonny, Forcados, Brass and Escravos, where it is loaded into tankers: C. Pipelines are the cheapest and safest way of moving large volumes of oil continuously. Oil is not carried by jets (A), and roads and railways (B, D) are used only for small quantities of refined products. Pipelines also link refineries and depots, though vandalism remains a problem. Memory tip: Crude flows by Pipe to Port, then by tanker abroad.",
 "|An industry is a|company producing unrelated commodities|group of firms producing related commodities|group of firms producing distinct commodities|firm producing differentiated commodities": "A firm is a single business unit, while an industry is the group of all firms producing the same or similar (closely related) products. For example, all firms making cement form the cement industry, and all textile mills form the textile industry. So an industry is a group of firms producing related commodities: B. A single company or firm (A, D) is not an industry, and firms producing distinct, unrelated goods (C) belong to different industries. Memory tip: Firm = one tree; industry = the whole forest of similar trees.",
 "|The raising of funds by selling stocks to the public is called|equity financing|stock financing|debt financing|loan financing": "A company can raise long-term finance in two broad ways. Equity financing means selling shares (stocks) to the public, usually through the stock exchange; buyers become part-owners and receive dividends, and the money need not be repaid. Debt financing means borrowing, through loans or debentures, which must be repaid with interest. Raising funds by selling stocks is therefore equity financing: A. The trap: B, \"stock financing\", sounds natural because stocks are sold, but it is not the recognised name; the term is equity financing. Memory tip: Shares = Equity = Ownership; Loans = Debt = Repayment.",
 "|A tax that increases at a higher percentage as income increases is called|a proportional tax|a regressive tax|a progressive tax|an income tax": "Taxes are classified by how the rate changes with income. A progressive tax takes a higher percentage of income as income rises; Nigeria's personal income tax bands work this way, so the rich pay a larger share. A proportional tax takes the same percentage from everyone, and a regressive tax takes a higher percentage from the poor (most indirect taxes act this way). So a tax whose rate increases as income increases is progressive: C. The trap: D, \"an income tax\", is tempting because the tax depends on income, but an income tax can be progressive, proportional or regressive; it names the base, not the rate structure. Memory tip: Progressive rates Progress upward with income.",
 "|The price paid for labour services is the|commission rate|wage rate|income rate|salary rate": "Each factor of production earns a reward, which is its price: land earns rent, labour earns wages, capital earns interest and the entrepreneur earns profit. The price paid for labour services is therefore the wage rate: B. The wage rate is set by the demand for and supply of labour, collective bargaining and government minimum wage laws. The trap: D, \"salary rate\", is a closely related everyday word for monthly pay, but in economic theory every payment for labour is called wages. Commission (A) is a payment for a particular sales service. Memory tip: Land-Rent, Labour-Wages, Capital-Interest, Entrepreneur-Profit.",
 "|The capital market is a market for trading of financial assets such as|bankers acceptances|treasury bills|long-term securities|commercial papers": "Financial markets are divided by the length of the funds traded. The money market deals in short-term funds (up to one year) with instruments such as treasury bills, bankers' acceptances, commercial papers and call money. The capital market deals in long-term funds through long-term securities: shares (stocks), government development stocks and bonds, and debentures, traded mainly on the stock exchange (in Nigeria, the Nigerian Exchange) and regulated by the SEC: C. The trap: B, treasury bills, are government securities too, but they mature within a year, so they belong to the money market. Memory tip: Money market = Months; Capital market = years.",
 "|In national income accounting, NNP is derived by subtracting from the GNP|transfer payments|indirect taxes|allowance for wastage|capital consumption": "Gross National Product (GNP) is the total value of final goods and services produced by a country's citizens in a year. In producing this output, machines, buildings and equipment wear out. The value of this wear and tear is called depreciation or capital consumption allowance. Subtracting it gives Net National Product: NNP = GNP − capital consumption: D. Subtracting indirect taxes (B) is the step from NNP at market prices to NNP at factor cost (national income). The trap: C, \"allowance for wastage\", sounds like the \"capital consumption allowance\", but wastage is not the accounting term. Memory tip: Gross minus depreciation = Net.",
 "|Economic growth can be accelerated through|balancing of savings and consumption|postponement of current consumption|planned current consumption|increased current consumption": "Economic growth means a sustained increase in a country's real output. Growth needs capital formation (more machines, factories, roads), and capital formation needs saving. When people postpone (put off) current consumption, they save more, and these savings can be channelled through banks into investment. The larger stock of capital raises future production. So growth is accelerated through postponement of current consumption: B. Increasing current consumption (D) reduces saving and investment, and simply \"balancing\" or \"planning\" consumption (A, C) does not by itself free more resources for investment. Memory tip: Consume less today, Save, Invest, Grow tomorrow.",
 "|An increase in the discount rate is an indication of a Central Bank's intention to pursue|an expansionary monetary policy|a disciplined monetary policy|a dynamic monetary policy|a contractionary monetary policy": "The discount rate (also called the bank rate, or in Nigeria the Monetary Policy Rate) is the rate at which the central bank lends to commercial banks. When the central bank raises it, banks pay more to borrow and in turn raise their lending rates. Borrowing becomes dearer, credit and money supply fall, and spending slows. This is a tight or contractionary monetary policy, usually used to fight inflation: D. Lowering the rate would be an expansionary policy (A). \"Disciplined\" and \"dynamic\" (B, C) are not technical names of policy types. Memory tip: Rate Up, money Tight; rate down, money loose.",
 "|The efficiency of labour can be increased by|constant training of workers|retirement of workers on a regular basis|engaging expatriates only|placing an embargo on recruitment": "Efficiency of labour is the ability of workers to produce more and better output with the same effort and time. It depends on education, health, working conditions, motivation, wages and, above all, training. Constant (regular) training keeps workers' skills up to date with new methods and machines, so their productivity rises: A. Regular retirement of workers (B) loses experience, engaging only expatriates (C) does not improve the existing labour force, and an embargo on recruitment (D) only limits the number of workers. Memory tip: Train to Gain: skills raise efficiency.",
 "|In Nigeria, revenue sharing from the Federation account is between the|executive the judiciary and the legislature|federal and state governments and the parastatals|federal and state governments and the Senate|federal, state and local governments": "All federally collected revenue in Nigeria (oil revenue, company income tax, customs duties and so on) is paid into the Federation Account. It is then shared vertically among the three tiers of government, federal, state and local governments, according to a formula recommended by the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) and approved by the National Assembly: D. The arms of government (A) and parastatals or the Senate (B, C) are not tiers of government that receive a share. The Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC) meets monthly to share the funds. Memory tip: Three tiers share the pot: Federal, State, Local.",
 "|The reduction of high fertility rate is a measure in population control designed to make the|female population higher than the male|female and male population equal|male population higher than the female|growth in population more sustainable": "Fertility rate is the average number of children born to a woman. A high fertility rate causes rapid population growth that can outrun food supply, jobs, schools and health facilities. Population control measures such as family planning, education of women and later marriage aim to reduce fertility so that population growth is slower and can be supported by the country's resources, that is, more sustainable: D. Reducing fertility does not aim to change the balance between males and females (A, B, C), because births of boys and girls are roughly equal. Memory tip: Fewer births per woman = growth the economy can Sustain.",
 "|National debt is an expected outcome of a|deficit budget|consolidated budget|balanced budget|surplus budget": "A budget is the government's plan of revenue and expenditure for a year. In a deficit budget expenditure exceeds revenue, and the gap is financed mainly by borrowing at home (treasury bills, bonds, central bank loans) or abroad. Borrowing adds to the national (public) debt, so national debt is the expected outcome of a deficit budget: A. A surplus budget (D) has revenue above spending and can be used to repay debt, while a balanced budget (C) has no gap to borrow for. A consolidated budget (B) simply combines several accounts. Memory tip: Deficit leads to Debt.",
 "|The distribution channel that contributes most to the cost of production is|producer-wholesaler-consumer|producer-retailer-consumer|producer-retailer-wholesaler-consumer|producer-wholesaler-retailer-consumer": "A channel of distribution is the route goods take from producer to consumer. Each middleman (wholesaler, retailer) adds a profit margin and costs of transport, storage and handling, so the longer the channel, the higher the final price. The common long channel producer-wholesaler-retailer-consumer uses the most middlemen and therefore adds most to the cost of goods to the consumer: D. The short channels in A and B have only one middleman each. Option C lists the same number of stages but in a reversed order that is not a normal trade channel, since wholesalers buy from producers and sell to retailers. Memory tip: More middlemen, more margins, more cost.",
 "|Money may not be accepted as a store of value if|its value is unstable|it is easily transferable|its value is stable|it is easily divisible": "Money performs four main functions: medium of exchange, measure (unit) of value, store of value and standard of deferred payment. To serve as a store of value, money must keep its purchasing power over time. If its value is unstable, for example during high inflation when prices rise quickly, people lose by holding it and prefer to store wealth in land, buildings, gold or foreign currency: A. A stable value (C) is exactly what makes money a good store of value, while transferability and divisibility (B, D) are useful qualities, not reasons for rejection. Memory tip: Inflation eats savings, so unstable money is a poor store.",
 "|In order to accelerate economic development a country must|stimulate her population growth rate|pursue a high population growth rate|pursue a rational population policy|pursue an incremental population policy": "Economic development means improved living standards, not just more output. Rapid population growth can swallow the gains of development by raising the dependency ratio and the demand for food, schools, health care and jobs. A country should therefore pursue a rational population policy, one that balances population growth with available resources through education, family planning and good data from censuses: C. Stimulating or pursuing high population growth (A, B) works against development in most developing countries. \"Incremental population policy\" (D) is not a recognised policy. Nigeria's National Population Policy (1988, revised 2004) follows this approach. Memory tip: Plan the People to Plan the Progress.",
 "|If the labour force in a given community is 2,000,000, those in the wage employment 200,000 and the unemployed are 80,000, what is the unemployment rate in the community?|0.4%|40.0%|10.0%|4.0%": "The unemployment rate measures the share of the labour force that is willing and able to work but has no job.\nStep 1: Formula: unemployment rate = (unemployed ÷ labour force) × 100.\nStep 2: 80,000 ÷ 2,000,000 = 0.04.\nStep 3: 0.04 × 100 = 4.0%: D.\nThe figure for wage employment (200,000) is not needed. The trap: B (40.0%) comes from dividing 80,000 by the 200,000 in wage employment instead of by the whole labour force; A (0.4%) is a slip in the decimal point. Memory tip: always divide by the whole labour force.",
 "|GNP is not a good measure of social welfare because there are unrecorded|indirect taxes|social costs|government subsidies|transfer payments": "GNP measures the market value of output, so it is a weak guide to social welfare (people's well-being). It ignores leisure, the distribution of income, non-market output such as housework, and especially social costs: pollution, congestion, accidents, health damage and environmental destruction that come with production. These costs are borne by society but not recorded or subtracted in GNP, so a rising GNP can overstate welfare: B. The trap: D, transfer payments such as pensions, are also left out of GNP, but on purpose, because they are not payments for current production; leaving them out is correct accounting, not a weakness. Memory tip: GNP counts the factory's output, not the smoke.",
 "|The loans from the World Bank to developing countries are mainly to support|the establishment of more universities|capital-intensive technology|infrastructural investments|their low level of consumption": "The World Bank (International Bank for Reconstruction and Development), set up at Bretton Woods in 1944, together with its soft-loan arm the International Development Association (IDA), lends to developing countries for long-term development. Its loans mainly finance infrastructure and development projects: roads, power stations, water supply, irrigation, ports, and also agriculture, health and education programmes: C. It does not lend to raise consumption (D), and it does not mainly fund new universities or favour capital-intensive technology (A, B). Short-term balance of payments help comes from the IMF, not the World Bank. Memory tip: World Bank builds (projects); IMF balances (payments).",
 "|The major reason why nations engage in international trade is that|it allows free movement of factors of production|it makes possible the use of capital intensive method of production|nations are substantially different in their endowments of economic resources|it affords the trading nations the opportunity to earn high profit": "Countries differ greatly in their resources: Nigeria has crude oil and a tropical climate, Japan has advanced technology and skilled labour, Saudi Arabia has oil but little farmland. Because of these differences in endowments, each country can produce some goods more cheaply than others. By specialising according to comparative advantage and trading, all countries gain: C. International trade moves goods, not mainly factors of production (A); capital-intensive methods (B) are not its basis; and while traders seek profit (D), the underlying reason nations trade is the difference in resources. Memory tip: Different gifts make trade worthwhile.",
 "|A country with over-valued currency will|expect balance of payments surplus|have increased demand for her exports|increase her foreign reserve|decrease her foreign reserve": "A currency is over-valued when its official exchange rate is higher than its true market value. Foreigners then need more of their own money to buy the country's goods, so exports become expensive and fall, while imports become cheap and rise. The balance of payments moves into deficit, and the central bank must use foreign reserves to pay for the excess imports and defend the rate, so its foreign reserves decrease: D. A surplus, higher export demand and rising reserves (A, B, C) are the results of an under-valued currency. Nigeria's naira was over-valued before SAP (1986), when it was devalued. Memory tip: Over-valued = Over-importing = reserves Out.",
 "|If goods P and Q are purchased by a consumer, a fall in the price of P with the price of Q unchanged will cause the budget line to|rotate outwards away from the origin|shift parallel inwards|rotate inwards towards the origin|shift parallel outwards": "A budget line shows every combination of P and Q a consumer can buy with a fixed income. Its end points are income ÷ price of P on the P axis and income ÷ price of Q on the Q axis. When the price of P falls, the P intercept moves outwards (more P can be bought), but the Q intercept does not move because the price of Q and income are unchanged. So the line pivots outwards from the Q axis, away from the origin: A. The trap: D (shift parallel outwards) is what happens when income rises or both prices fall in the same proportion, not when one price falls. Memory tip: one price changes, the line Rotates; income changes, the line shifts in Parallel.",
 "|One of the reasons for an exceptional demand curve is the|availability of credit facilities|availability of substitutes|change in the price of the commodity|expectation of a future change in price": "The normal demand curve slopes downwards: as price rises, people buy less. Exceptional (abnormal) demand curves slope upwards, and the textbook causes are Giffen goods, goods of ostentation (snob or Veblen goods), ignorance, and the expectation of a future change in price. If buyers believe prices will keep rising, for example during a fuel scarcity or before a budget, they rush to buy more now even though the price is already higher: D. A change in the commodity's own price (C) only causes a movement along a normal curve, and availability of substitutes or credit does not make demand slope upwards. Memory tip: GIVE: Giffen, Ignorance, Veblen, Expectation.",
 "|The elasticity of supply of perishable goods is|elastic|zero|inelastic|unitary": "Elasticity of supply measures how much the quantity supplied responds to a change in price. Sellers of perishable goods such as tomatoes, fresh fish and vegetables cannot store them to wait for a better price, and cannot quickly grow more when price rises. Whatever is harvested must be sold quickly, so the quantity supplied changes little when price changes: supply is inelastic, C. Zero elasticity (B) would mean supply does not change at all, which is the extreme case of fixed supply such as land or a single day's catch already landed, not the general rule for perishables. Durable, storable goods have more elastic supply. Memory tip: if it cannot wait, its supply cannot change much.",
 "|In the pie chart above, the percentage for children is equivalent to|288°|144°|90°|72°": "In a pie chart the whole circle, 360°, stands for 100%, so each 1% is 3.6°.\nStep 1: Children are 40% of the total.\nStep 2: Angle = 40/100 × 360° = 0.4 × 360° = 144°: B.\nCheck: women 35% = 126°, men 25% = 90°; 144° + 126° + 90° = 360°.\nThe trap: C (90°) is the angle for men, the 25% sector, and D (72°) is a fifth of the circle, so read the label carefully. Memory tip: degrees = percentage × 3.6.",
 "|In the diagram above, the marginal rate of substitution of X for Y for a movement from S to T is|5:1|1:5|18:4|13:3": "The marginal rate of substitution (MRS) of X for Y measures how many units of Y a consumer is willing to give up to get one more unit of X while staying on the same indifference curve (the same satisfaction).\nStep 1: Read the two points. S is at 2 units of X and 18 units of Y; T is at 4 units of X and 15 units of Y.\nStep 2: Y given up = 18 − 15 = 3; X gained = 4 − 2 = 2.\nStep 3: MRS of X for Y = ΔY ÷ ΔX = 3 ÷ 2 = 1.5, that is 3:2.\nNone of the options gives 3:2, so this question has no correct answer. Had the consumer given up 5 units of Y (18 to 13) for one more unit of X, the MRS would be 5:1, option A. MRS falls as you move down an indifference curve, which is why the curve is convex to the origin. Memory tip: MRS of X for Y = Y given up ÷ X gained.",
 "|A scientific approach in economics entails|a normative method only|both inductive and deductive methods|a deductive method only|both inductive and normative methods": "Economics is called a social science because it studies human behaviour using scientific methods. The deductive method starts from assumptions (for example, that people seek maximum satisfaction) and reasons logically to conclusions such as the law of demand. The inductive method starts from observed facts and data and builds general laws from them. Economists use both: theories are deduced and then tested against real-world data: B. Normative economics deals with what ought to be (value judgements), so it is not part of the scientific method; positive economics deals with what is. Memory tip: Deduction goes Down from theory to facts; Induction goes Into theory from facts.",
 "|In a centrally planned economy, the economic problem of deciding what to produce is dependent on|state command|relative prices|average cost|average profit": "Every economy must answer what to produce, how, for whom and how to use resources efficiently. In a free-market (capitalist) economy, prices and profits answer these questions. In a centrally planned (command or socialist) economy, a central planning authority owned by the state decides what goods are produced, in what quantities, and how they are shared, and producers follow its directives: A. Relative prices (B) are the guide in a market economy, and average cost or profit (C, D) are signals that private firms use. The former Soviet Union was the classic example of a command economy. Memory tip: Command economy = Central planners Choose.",
 "|A shift in supply curve indicates that a different quantity will be supplied at each possible price because|supply is facing competition|consumers are willing to pay higher prices|price has changed|other factors than price have changed": "The supply curve is drawn for a given set of conditions: costs of production, technology, prices of other goods, weather, taxes and subsidies, and the goals of firms. If only the good's own price changes, sellers move along the existing curve (a change in quantity supplied). When one of those other conditions changes, a different quantity is offered at every price, so the whole curve shifts (a change in supply): D. For example, a fall in the cost of fertiliser shifts the supply of maize to the right. The trap: C (price has changed) describes a movement along the curve, not a shift, and is the classic confusion. Memory tip: own price Moves you along; anything else Shifts the curve.",
 "|The demand for factors of production is an example of|competitive demand|derived demand|composite demand|joint demand": "Firms do not want land, labour, capital or entrepreneurs for their own sake. They want them because consumers want the bread, cars or cloth those factors help to produce. The demand for a factor therefore depends on, or is derived from, the demand for the final product: B. If demand for houses rises, demand for bricklayers and cement rises too. Joint demand (D) is for goods used together, like cars and petrol; composite demand (C) is one good wanted for several uses; competitive demand (A) is for substitutes. Memory tip: Derived = Depends on the demand for the final good.",
 "|A student has ₦30.00 with which to buy a ruler costing ₦18.00 and an exercise book costing ₦25.00. If he buys the exercise book, his opportunity cost is|the exercise book|₦25.00|₦18.00|the ruler": "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. It is measured in the real alternative given up, not in money.\nStep 1: The ruler and exercise book together cost ₦18.00 + ₦25.00 = ₦43.00, more than his ₦30.00, so he can buy only one.\nStep 2: He buys the exercise book, so the alternative he gives up is the ruler: D.\nThe trap: C (₦18.00) is the price of the ruler; opportunity cost is the ruler itself, not its money price. A and B describe the item he actually chose. Memory tip: opportunity cost = what you could have had instead.",
 "|Import substitution aims at|substituting agricultural products with industrial ones|substituting imports with agricultural exports|improving the balance of payments|the diversification of industries": "Import substitution industrialisation means setting up local factories to produce goods that a country used to import, for example assembling vehicles or making textiles at home, usually protected by tariffs and import bans. Its aims include saving foreign exchange, creating jobs, and reducing dependence on foreign goods. By reducing the import bill, it is meant to improve the balance of payments: C. It does not mean replacing farm products with industrial ones (A) or paying for imports with farm exports (B), and diversification (D) is a possible side effect rather than its main aim. Nigeria followed this strategy in the 1960s and 1970s. Memory tip: make it here so you do not have to import it.",
 "|Some of the key indicators of underdevelopment in a country are|poverty, low level of literacy and low income|poverty, low income and low unemployment level|poverty, high level of literacy and low income|low level of illiteracy, high income and poverty": "Economists judge development by indicators such as per capita income, literacy, life expectancy, health and the level of poverty. An underdeveloped country typically has widespread poverty, a low level of literacy (many people cannot read and write) and a low income per head: A. Low unemployment (B), high literacy (C) and high income (D) are signs of a developed country, so each of the other options mixes in a feature that does not fit. These indicators are combined in the Human Development Index (HDI) used by the United Nations. Memory tip: the three P's of underdevelopment: Poverty, Poor education, Poor income.",
 "|ECOWAS has taken a giant step towards economic integration by|introducing a common currency for travelers|presenting a united front against foreign economic exploiters|holding frequent meetings at various state capitals|increasing economic stability among member states": "ECOWAS, the Economic Community of West African States, was formed in Lagos in 1975 to promote economic integration: free movement of people, goods and services, a customs union and eventually a single market and currency. Integration is meant to make member economies stronger and more stable together. So increasing economic stability among member states (D) is right, and so is A: in 1999 ECOWAS introduced the ECOWAS travellers' cheque, a common means of payment for travellers in all member states and a real step towards a common currency. Both are accepted: D or A. Presenting a united front against exploiters (B) and holding meetings (C) are not integration measures in themselves. Memory tip: ECOWAS = Economic Community; integration aims at a stronger, more stable region.",
 "|One of the major factors militating against industrialization in Nigeria is the|frequent breakdown of equipment|failure to get foreign partners and supporters|inadequacy of infrastructural facilities|absence of government participation": "Industries need reliable electricity, good roads and railways, water, and communication networks. In Nigeria, the most often cited obstacle to industrialisation is inadequate infrastructure: erratic power supply forces factories to run costly generators, and poor roads raise transport costs, so many firms close or operate below capacity: C. Equipment breakdown (A) is largely a result of poor maintenance and power surges rather than the root cause, and government has always participated heavily in industry, so D is false. Other factors include shortage of capital, skilled labour and raw materials. Memory tip: no power, no roads, no factories.",
 "|The types, sources and uses of government income are mainly concerned with|public budget|public finance|public expenditure|public revenue": "Public finance is the branch of economics that deals with the income and expenditure of government: the types of revenue (taxes, fees, royalties, loans), where it comes from, and how it is spent on defence, education, health and so on. Because the question covers types, sources and uses of government income together, the whole subject, public finance, is the answer: B. Public revenue (D) covers only sources of income, public expenditure (C) covers only spending, and a budget (A) is a yearly statement of planned revenue and spending. Memory tip: Revenue + Expenditure = Public Finance.",
 "|The present privatization policy in Nigeria is aimed at|reducing the prices of goods and services|increasing efficiency in production|poverty alleviation|reducing income inequality": "Privatization is the sale of all or part of government-owned enterprises to private investors. Nigeria's privatization programme began under the Structural Adjustment Programme in 1988 and was expanded by the Bureau of Public Enterprises from 1999. Its main aim is to make these enterprises more efficient: private owners have a profit motive to cut waste, improve management and serve customers, and government is relieved of subsidies to loss-making corporations: B. Lower prices (A) do not always follow, and poverty alleviation and income equality (C, D) are not its main purpose. Memory tip: Privatize to Perform better.",
 "|One of the roles of the NNPC in the production and refining of crude oil is to|secure maximum government revenue and regulate price|protect the local operators in the petroleum industry|prevent the smuggling of petroleum products|satisfy the domestic consumption of petroleum products": "The Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation (NNPC) was formed in 1977 to manage the country's oil interests. It explores for and produces crude oil through joint ventures, and it owns the refineries at Port Harcourt, Warri and Kaduna, which were built to refine crude oil into petrol, kerosene and diesel for the home market. One of its roles is therefore to satisfy domestic demand for petroleum products: D. Today the company is NNPC Limited, a commercial company under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021, and the Dangote refinery also supplies the local market. Preventing smuggling (C) is a job for customs, and regulation is done by separate regulators. Memory tip: NNPC refines to supply Nigeria first.",
 "|One way of correcting the balance of payments problem of a country is to|devalue the currency|introduce import-promotion measures|deemphasize import|buy investments abroad": "A balance of payments deficit means a country pays out more foreign exchange than it earns. Devaluation lowers the value of the home currency against foreign currencies, so exports become cheaper for foreigners and imports become dearer at home. If demand for exports and imports is elastic enough, exports rise, imports fall and the deficit narrows: A. Promoting imports (B) worsens the deficit, and buying investments abroad (D) is an outflow of funds. Other remedies include tariffs, import quotas, exchange control and export promotion; \"deemphasize import\" (C) is too vague to count as a named policy. Memory tip: devalue to make exports cheap and imports dear.",
 "|One of the problems arising from the localization of industries is|high prices of output|structural unemployment|the exportation of output|the scarcity of foreign exchange": "Localization of industry means the concentration of a particular industry in one area, such as textiles in Kaduna or the oil industry around Port Harcourt. It brings external economies, but it has dangers. If demand for that industry's product falls, or the industry is hit by competition or a change in technology, many workers with the same specialised skills lose their jobs at once and cannot easily find work in other industries. This mismatch is structural unemployment: B. High prices (A) and foreign exchange scarcity (D) are not caused by localization, and exporting output (C) is not a problem. Memory tip: all eggs in one basket: when the industry falls, the whole area is out of work.",
 "|The major purpose of the African Development Bank is to|educate peasant farmers in new techniques|provide loans for development banks in distress|provide loans for infrastructural development|provide loans for trade development": "The African Development Bank (AfDB) was set up in 1964 with headquarters in Abidjan, Côte d'Ivoire. Its main purpose is to contribute to the economic and social development of its regional members by lending for development projects, especially infrastructure such as roads, power, water supply and telecommunications, and by providing technical assistance: C. It does not mainly rescue distressed banks (B) or train peasant farmers (A), and financing trade (D) is not its central aim. Nigeria is one of its largest shareholders. Memory tip: AfDB builds Africa's roads, power and water.",
 "|As a country gets more developed, the percentage of labour engaged in agriculture tends to|switch over to trading|remain constant|increase steadily|decrease steadily": "In a poor, less developed country most people work on farms with simple tools. As development proceeds, farming becomes mechanised, so fewer workers are needed to produce the food, while manufacturing and services grow and attract labour to towns. The share of the labour force in agriculture therefore falls steadily: D. In many developed countries less than 5% of workers are farmers, compared with well over half in many poor countries. Total farm output can still rise, because each farmer produces more. Memory tip: the richer the country, the fewer the farmers.",
 "|A deficit budget can be used to|starve the economy of funds for economic development|protect the economy from inflation|stimulate recovery from trade depression|provide measures to remedy the balance of payments": "A deficit budget is one in which planned government expenditure is greater than expected revenue, the gap being financed by borrowing. Following Keynes, it is used in a depression or recession: the extra government spending raises aggregate demand, creates jobs and income, and through the multiplier helps the economy recover: C. A deficit budget pumps funds into the economy, so it does not starve it of funds (A), and it tends to cause rather than prevent inflation (B); a surplus budget is used to fight inflation. Memory tip: Deficit in Depression, Surplus against inflation.",
 "|In the diagrams above, the opportunity cost of a unit of cotton in terms of cocoa is|20 for Ghana, 60 for Nigeria|1/4 for Ghana, 1/2 for Nigeria|5 for Ghana, 30 for Nigeria|4 for Ghana, 2 for Nigeria": "On a production possibility line, the opportunity cost of one unit of cotton is the cocoa given up, which is the cocoa intercept divided by the cotton intercept.\nStep 1: Ghana: 20 tonnes of cocoa or 5 tonnes of cotton, so 1 tonne of cotton costs 20 ÷ 5 = 4 tonnes of cocoa.\nStep 2: Nigeria: 60 tonnes of cocoa or 30 tonnes of cotton, so 1 tonne of cotton costs 60 ÷ 30 = 2 tonnes of cocoa: D.\nNigeria has the lower opportunity cost, so it has the comparative advantage in cotton.\nThe trap: B (1/4 and 1/2) is the opportunity cost of cocoa in terms of cotton, the ratio turned upside down. Memory tip: cost of X = what you give up ÷ what you get.",
 "|External finance for a limited liability company is mainly sourced through|the issuing of shares|the leasing of equipment|trade credits|loans": "A limited liability company can raise funds internally, from retained (ploughed-back) profits and depreciation provisions, or externally, from outside sources. The main external sources are borrowing: bank loans, overdrafts and debentures. These loans bring in money without changing who owns the company: D. Issuing shares (A) is often listed as a source of capital in its own right, which makes this item debatable, while leasing (B) and trade credit (C) supply equipment and goods rather than finance in the main. Memory tip: inside money = retained profit; outside money = loans.",
 "|One of the major criticisms of the 1962-1968 National Development Plan was that|its execution was based largely on foreign-sourced financial resources|it failed to incorporate lessons gained from earlier plans to enhance efficiency|planned expenditure based too heavily on expected earnings from crude oil|it came too quick after the country's independence": "Nigeria's First National Development Plan (1962–1968) aimed at growth through investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure. Its major weakness was its dependence on foreign finance: about half of the planned public investment was expected to come from foreign aid and loans, but much of it never came, so many projects stalled: A. The civil war (1967–1970) also disrupted it. Oil was not yet the main revenue source in the early 1960s, so C does not fit; that criticism applies to later plans. Memory tip: First plan, Foreign money.",
 "|Which of the following yields more revenue to Nigeria?|Value added tax|Royalties|Direct tax|Indirect tax": "Government revenue in Nigeria comes from direct taxes (on income and profits), indirect taxes (customs and excise duties, VAT), royalties and rents, and other sources. The petroleum profits tax paid by oil companies is a direct tax, and together with company income tax and personal income tax it gives more revenue than any of the other options: C. VAT (A), introduced in 1993, is one indirect tax, and royalties (B), though large, are smaller than the petroleum profits tax. Memory tip: oil profits are taxed directly, and that is where the big money comes from.",
 "|In the short run a firm's marginal cost curve above the point of shut-down is its|supply curve|revenue curve|demand curve|cost curve": "In the short run a firm will keep producing as long as price covers average variable cost (AVC); the lowest point of AVC is the shut-down point. Above it, a profit-maximising firm produces where price = marginal cost. So for each price, the quantity supplied is read off the marginal cost curve, making the MC curve above the shut-down point the firm's short-run supply curve: A. A firm's demand curve (C) and revenue curve (B) come from buyers, not from costs. Memory tip: above AVC, MC = Supply.",
 "|The benefits that accrue to a firm as a result of an improvement in the industry it belongs to are called|internal economies of scale|market economies|external economies of scale|economies of scale": "Economies of scale are cost advantages from large-scale production. Internal economies arise inside the firm as it grows: bulk buying, specialisation, better machinery, easier borrowing. External economies arise outside the firm, from the growth or improvement of the whole industry: a pool of skilled labour, ancillary firms supplying parts, research institutes, better roads and specialised banks in the area. Every firm in the industry enjoys them whatever its own size, so the benefits described are external economies of scale: C. The trap: A (internal economies) sounds close, but internal means from the firm's own expansion. Memory tip: Internal = In the firm; External = Every firm in the industry.",
 "|As a firm increases its output the average fixed cost|tends to decrease continuously|rises and then falls|remains constant|tends to rise continuously": "Average fixed cost is total fixed cost divided by output. Fixed costs, such as rent and the salary of a manager, stay the same whatever is produced. For example, with fixed cost of ₦1,000: at 10 units AFC is ₦100, at 100 units it is ₦10, at 1,000 units it is ₦1. As output rises, the same fixed cost is spread over more units, so AFC keeps falling, approaching zero without ever reaching it: A. The trap: B (rises and then falls) describes no cost curve; AVC, ATC and MC fall and then rise (U-shaped), and that U shape is easy to apply wrongly to AFC. Memory tip: fixed cost spread thinner and thinner.",
 "|The economic policy of deregulation is aimed at encouraging|a competitive market structure|an oligopolistic market structure|a duopolistic market structure|a monopolistic market structure": "Deregulation means removing government controls, licences, price controls and legal barriers that restrict who can enter an industry and how they operate. With barriers removed, new firms can come in and compete, prices are set by the market, and efficiency improves. Its aim is therefore a competitive market structure: A. Oligopoly, duopoly and monopoly (B, C, D) all involve few sellers, which is what deregulation tries to move away from. Examples in Nigeria include the deregulation of telecommunications, which brought GSM operators in 2001, and of the downstream petroleum sector. Memory tip: Deregulate to let more players in.",
 "|The effect of an increase in demand for a commodity accompanied by a decrease in supply will be to|decrease the equilibrium quantity and affect the price in an indeterminate way|raise the price of the commodity and affect the quantity in an indeterminate way|lower its price while affecting the equilibrium quantity in an indeterminate way|raise its price as well as the equilibrium quantity": "Work out each change separately. An increase in demand (demand curve shifts right) raises both price and quantity. A decrease in supply (supply curve shifts left) raises price but lowers quantity. Together, both push price up, so price certainly rises. But for quantity, one change pushes it up and the other pushes it down; the net effect depends on which shift is larger, so it is indeterminate: B. The trap: D (raise price as well as quantity) is true only if the rise in demand is larger than the fall in supply, so it cannot be the general answer. Memory tip: when the two shifts agree on one variable, that one is certain; the other is uncertain.",
 "|Under conditions of perfect competition a firm's supply curve is determined by its|fixed cost curve|variable cost curve|total cost curve|marginal cost curve": "A firm in perfect competition is a price taker: it sells as much as it wants at the market price. To maximise profit it produces where price (which equals marginal revenue) equals marginal cost. So at each possible price the firm's output is read off its MC curve, as long as price covers average variable cost. The firm's supply curve is therefore determined by its marginal cost curve: D. Fixed costs (A) do not affect the output decision at all, and variable and total cost curves (B, C) are used only to find the shut-down and break-even points. Memory tip: competitive firm: P = MR = MC.",
 "|The short-run equilibrium output for a monopolist is determined by the|intersection of the marginal cost and marginal revenue curves|highest point on the total revenue curve|intersection of the average revenue and average cost curves|minimum point on the average cost curve": "All profit-maximising firms, whatever the market structure, produce where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), with MC cutting MR from below. Beyond that point an extra unit adds more to cost than to revenue. The monopolist then charges the price read off its demand (AR) curve at that output: A. The highest point on the total revenue curve (B) maximises revenue, not profit; AR = AC (C) gives only normal profit, the long-run result in monopolistic competition; and minimum AC (D) is the efficient output reached in long-run perfect competition, not by a monopolist. Memory tip: MC = MR is the golden rule for every firm.",
 "|In the diagram above, what is the excess demand at the price of ₦60?|300|100|600|200": "Excess demand is the amount by which quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a price below equilibrium.\nStep 1: Read the diagram at ₦60: the demand curve gives 300 units.\nStep 2: The supply curve at ₦60 gives 100 units.\nStep 3: Excess demand = 300 − 100 = 200: D.\nEquilibrium is at about 200 units, where the two curves cross above ₦60. The trap: A (300) is only the quantity demanded, not the shortage. Memory tip: excess demand = demanded minus supplied, measured across at the same price.",
 "|The equilibrium wage in an economy is determined by the|supply and demand for labour|rate of inflation|workers' union|public service": "Wages are the price of labour, and in a free labour market they are set like any other price. Employers' demand for labour (which falls as wages rise) and workers' supply of labour (which rises as wages rise) meet at the equilibrium wage, where the number of workers firms want equals the number willing to work: A. Trade unions (C) and government (D) can influence wages through bargaining or minimum wage laws, and inflation (B) affects the real wage, but the equilibrium itself is where supply and demand meet. Memory tip: wage = price of labour, set by supply and demand.",
 "|If the growth rate of available resources continuously outpaces that of the population, a country will eventually experience|under population|maximum population|optimal population|over population": "The optimum population is the size at which, given the available resources and technology, output per head is highest. If population is below the optimum, there are not enough people to make full use of the resources: underpopulation. If resources keep growing faster than the population, the population becomes ever smaller relative to what the resources could support, so the country experiences underpopulation: A. Overpopulation (D) is the opposite case, where population outgrows resources, the situation Malthus feared. Memory tip: more resources than people = under; more people than resources = over.",
 "|The middlemen in the chain of distribution are|wholesalers and retailers|manufacturers and consumers|consumers and wholesalers|retailers and consumers": "The chain of distribution links the producer to the consumer: manufacturer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer. The middlemen are those who stand between the producer at one end and the consumer at the other, buying and reselling goods. These are the wholesalers, who buy in bulk from producers, and the retailers, who sell in small quantities to consumers: A. Manufacturers and consumers (B, C, D) are the two ends of the chain, not the middle. Other middlemen include agents, brokers and commission agents. Memory tip: Producer – Wholesaler – Retailer – Consumer: the middle two are the middlemen.",
 "|The Malthusian theory of population growth is often said to be|oversimplified|ambiguous|optimistic|pessimistic": "Thomas Malthus, in his Essay on the Principle of Population (1798), argued that population tends to grow in a geometric progression (1, 2, 4, 8 …) while food supply grows only in an arithmetic progression (1, 2, 3, 4 …). Population would therefore outrun food, and be held down by positive checks such as famine, disease and war unless people practised moral restraint. Because it predicts misery for mankind, the theory is described as pessimistic: D. Critics note that technology and the green revolution raised food output far more than he expected. Memory tip: Malthus = Misery: geometric people, arithmetic food.",
 "|Improved labour efficiency can be measured by|the constancy of input-output ratio|an increase in input-output ratio|an increase in output-input ratio|a decrease in output-input ratio": "Efficiency, or productivity, is measured as output per unit of input: output ÷ input. If the same number of workers produce more goods, or the same goods are produced by fewer workers, the output-input ratio rises, and labour has become more efficient: C. An increase in the input-output ratio (B) means more input for each unit of output, which is a fall in efficiency, and a decrease in the output-input ratio (D) is the same thing. The trap: B uses the right words in the wrong order, the ratio turned upside down. Memory tip: productivity = Output over Input; up is good.",
 "|National income estimates can be used to|compare a country's growth rate with that of another over a period of time|differentiate between the rich and the poor in a country|prepare a country's annual budget|project the level of a country's economic development": "National income statistics measure the total value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. Their uses include measuring the standard of living (income per head), measuring growth over time, comparing one country's performance with another's, and guiding economic planning. Comparing a country's growth rate with another's over a period is a standard use: A. National income totals do not show how income is shared, so they cannot separate rich from poor within a country (B); budgets (C) rely on revenue and expenditure estimates, and projections (D) are a planning exercise rather than what the estimates themselves show. Memory tip: national income figures compare across time and across countries.",
 "|In Nigeria, the bank that can correctly be described as a unit bank is|the Central Bank|Community Bank|People's Bank|Mortgage Bank": "A unit bank operates from one office only, with no branches, and serves its local area; branch banking is the opposite, with a head office and many branches. In Nigeria, community banks, set up from 1990, were each licensed to operate as a single unit bank owned by and serving one community: B. The Central Bank of Nigeria (A) has branches in many state capitals and is the banker to government and banks, not a unit bank. People's Bank and mortgage banks also operated branches. Since 2007 community banks have been converted into microfinance banks, some of which are still unit banks. Memory tip: one Community, one office = unit bank.",
 "|Creeping inflation implies that there is a|rapid but not permanent increase in the general price level|gentle but not permanent increase in the general price level|rapid and persistent increase in the general price level|gentle and persistent increase in the general price level": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. It is classified by speed. Creeping (mild) inflation is a slow, gentle rise, typically a few per cent a year, that goes on persistently over a long period: D. Galloping inflation is a rapid rise, and hyperinflation is an extremely rapid rise in which money loses its value. A rise that is not persistent (A, B) is not inflation at all but a once-for-all price change. The trap: B uses \"gentle\" but adds \"not permanent\", which contradicts the meaning of inflation. Memory tip: Creeping = Crawling slowly but Continuously.",
 "|In a closed economy, the marginal propensity to consume is 0.6 and the average propensity to consume 0.8. The value of the multiplier is|2.7|2.6|2.4|2.5": "The multiplier depends on the marginal propensity to consume (MPC), the share of each extra naira spent, not on the average propensity.\nStep 1: MPS = 1 − MPC = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4.\nStep 2: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ MPS = 1 ÷ 0.4 = 2.5: D.\nThe APC of 0.8 is extra information put in to distract; using it would give 1 ÷ (1 − 0.8) = 5, which is not an option. Memory tip: k = 1 / (1 − MPC) = 1 / MPS.",
 "|Excess demand inflation can be controlled through|contractionary trade policy|expansionary monetary policy|contractionary fiscal policy|expansionary fiscal policy": "Excess demand (demand-pull) inflation occurs when total spending is greater than the economy can produce at current prices: too much money chasing too few goods. The cure is to reduce total demand. Contractionary fiscal policy does this: government cuts its own spending and raises taxes, leaving households and firms less to spend: C. Expansionary monetary or fiscal policy (B, D) adds to demand and makes inflation worse. Contractionary monetary policy (higher interest rates, open market sales) would also work, but it is not offered. Memory tip: too much demand? Contract it.",
 "|The primary objective of the NDIC is to|provide regulations guiding the finance houses|give protection to banks against fraud|give protection to bank depositors|give protection to the CBN against fraud": "The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) was established in 1988 to insure the deposits of banks. Insured banks pay premiums to it, and if an insured bank fails, NDIC pays depositors up to a set limit. It also supervises banks to reduce the risk of failure and acts as liquidator of failed banks. Its primary objective is therefore the protection of bank depositors: C. Regulation of finance houses (A) is mainly a CBN function, and protecting banks or the CBN against fraud (B, D) is not its purpose. Today it works under the NDIC Act 2023. Memory tip: NDIC = Depositors' Insurance Cover.",
 "|Liquidity preference refers to the|wish to hold more funds for precautionary purposes|need to increase the money supply in order to lower the interest rate|demand to hold money as assets rather than as stocks|need to borrow money for short periods to meet some temporary crises": "John Maynard Keynes used \"liquidity preference\" for the demand for money: the desire of people to hold their wealth in the form of cash (the most liquid asset) rather than in interest-earning assets such as bonds and stocks. He gave three motives: transactions, precautionary and speculative. The question's definition is the demand to hold money as an asset rather than as stocks: C. Option A gives only one of the three motives, so it is too narrow, and B and D describe policy and borrowing, not preference for money. Memory tip: liquidity preference = preferring cash to securities, for Transactions, Precaution and Speculation.",
 "|If the country's GNP in the current year is ₦65 billion and the GNP in the base year is ₦50 billion, the GNP deflator is|30.0|130.0|15.0|1.3": "A GNP deflator, as used here, compares the current-year GNP with the base-year GNP, with the base year set at 100.\nStep 1: Deflator = current-year GNP ÷ base-year GNP × 100.\nStep 2: = ₦65 billion ÷ ₦50 billion × 100 = 1.3 × 100 = 130.0: B.\nThis means prices are 30% higher than in the base year.\nThe trap: D (1.3) forgets to multiply by 100, and A (30.0) is the percentage rise, not the index. Memory tip: an index always starts at 100 in the base year.",
 "|Insurance companies invest mainly in instruments traded on the|commodity market|open market|capital market|money market": "Insurance companies collect premiums now and pay claims later, often many years later in life assurance. They hold large long-term funds, so they invest mainly in long-term securities: shares, government bonds, debentures and stocks, which are traded on the capital market: C. The money market (D) deals in short-term instruments such as treasury bills, which insurers hold only for liquidity. The commodity market (A) deals in goods, and \"open market\" (B) refers to central bank operations. Memory tip: long-term funds go to the long-term market.",
 "|The solution to the problem of double coincidence of wants requires a buyer and a seller whose demands are precisely|complementary|supplementary|composite|competitive": "Under barter, goods are exchanged directly for goods. A trade can take place only if each party has exactly what the other wants and wants exactly what the other has: the farmer with yams who wants cloth must find a weaver who has cloth and wants yams. This \"double coincidence of wants\" means the two traders' wants must match or complement each other precisely: A. The difficulty of finding such a match is the main reason money was invented as a medium of exchange. Supplementary, composite and competitive (B, C, D) are not terms for matching wants. Memory tip: barter needs two matching wants; money needs only one.",
 "|The stock exchange is a market for the buying and selling of|existing shares|monetary instruments|new shares|treasury bills": "A stock exchange, such as the Nigerian Exchange (formerly the Nigerian Stock Exchange, Lagos, 1960), is a market where securities already issued by companies and government are bought and sold. It is chiefly a secondary market: when you buy shares there, you buy them from another investor, not from the company. So it is mainly a market for existing shares: A. New shares (C) are sold to the public in the primary market, though they are then listed on the exchange. Treasury bills (D) and other short-term monetary instruments (B) belong to the money market. Memory tip: Stock Exchange = second-hand market for shares.",
 "|The price of a good rises from ₦5 to ₦8 and the quantity demanded falls from 200 to 190 units. Over this price range, the demand curve is|fairly inelastic|fairly elastic|perfectly inelastic|perfectly elastic": "Price elasticity of demand (PED) compares how strongly quantity responds to a price change.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 190 − 200 = −10, so %ΔQ = 10 ÷ 200 × 100 = 5%.\nStep 2: Change in price = ₦8 − ₦5 = ₦3, so %ΔP = 3 ÷ 5 × 100 = 60%.\nStep 3: PED = 5% ÷ 60% ≈ 0.08.\nA value between 0 and 1 means demand is inelastic. It is not perfectly inelastic (C), because quantity did fall a little: A.\nThe trap: C catches students who see a tiny change in quantity and forget that perfectly inelastic means no change at all (PED = 0).\nMemory tip: PED below 1 = Inelastic (Insensitive); exactly 0 = perfectly inelastic.",
 "|In economic analysis, a statement is said to be normative if it|can be tested scientifically|relates to value judgment|is contradictory|is incorrect": "Economists separate two kinds of statements. A positive statement describes what is, was or will be, and can be tested against facts (\"raising the price of fuel reduces its consumption\"). A normative statement says what ought to be (\"government should subsidise fuel\"); it depends on opinions and values, so evidence alone cannot settle it. That is why a normative statement relates to value judgment: B. Being testable (A) is the mark of a positive statement, and a normative statement need not be contradictory (C) or incorrect (D); it simply cannot be proved either way.\nThe trap: A is the definition of the opposite kind of statement, positive economics, which students often mix up.\nMemory tip: Normative has \"ought\" in it; Positive can be Proved.",
 "|Utility is the satisfaction derived from|production|distribution|consumption|demand": "Utility is the want-satisfying power of a good, or the satisfaction a person gets when he or she consumes it. Eating a plate of rice, using a phone or riding in a bus all yield utility to the consumer. Utility is the basis of consumer behaviour theory: total utility, marginal utility and the law of diminishing marginal utility all describe how satisfaction changes as more units are consumed. So utility is derived from consumption: C. Production (A) and distribution (B) create and deliver goods, while demand (D) is the desire backed by ability to pay, which follows from expected utility.\nMemory tip: Utility is Used up in consumption; you get it when you Use the good.",
 "|If there is an increase in demand without a corresponding increase in supply, there will be|fall in price|rise in price|shift in supply curve to the right|shift in demand curve to the left": "Price is set where demand equals supply. If demand increases (the demand curve shifts to the right) and supply does not increase as well, buyers want more than sellers offer at the old price. This shortage, or excess demand, makes buyers compete for the limited goods and sellers raise their prices, until a new, higher equilibrium price is reached with a somewhat larger quantity: B. A fall in price (A) would follow a rise in supply or a fall in demand. A shift of supply to the right (C) is exactly what the question rules out, and a leftward demand shift (D) is a fall, not a rise, in demand.\nMemory tip: More Demand, same supply = More Dearness.",
 "|Use the production possibility curve of a country represented in the diagram to answer the question.\n\nAn improvement in technology will enable the country to produce at|V|W|X|Z": "A production possibility curve (PPC) shows the largest combinations of two goods a country can produce with its present resources and technology. Points on the curve (V, Y, W) are efficient, a point inside it (X) means idle or wasted resources, and a point outside it (Z) cannot be reached for now. Better technology lets the same resources produce more of both goods, so the whole curve shifts outwards. A point such as Z, which was once unattainable, then becomes possible: D. V and W are already attainable on the present curve, and X is inside it.\nMemory tip: Inside = Idle, On = Optimal, Outside = Out of reach (until growth or technology shifts the curve).",
 "|Use the production possibility curve of a country represented in the diagram to answer the question.\n\nIf the country is currently producing at point Y, it can increase production of producer goods by moving to the point|V|W|X|Z": "In the diagram, producer goods are measured along the horizontal axis and consumer goods up the vertical axis. Y is on the curve, so the economy is fully employed; it cannot get more of one good without giving up some of the other (opportunity cost). Moving down the curve to the right, from Y to W, increases producer goods and reduces consumer goods: B. Moving to V (A) would give more consumer goods and fewer producer goods. X (C) is inside the curve, with less of both, and Z (D) is outside the curve and cannot be reached with present resources.\nMemory tip: along a PPC, \"more of one means less of the other\": that sacrifice is the opportunity cost.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nQ ... 1 ... 2 ... 3 ... 4 ... 5 ... 6 ... 7 ... 8\nMUy ... 16 ... 14 ... 12 ... 10 ... 8 ... 6 ... 4 ... 2\nMUx ... 11 ... 10 ... 9 ... 8 ... 7 ... 6 ... 5 ... 4\n\nIn the table above, the price of commodity y is ₦2 and that of x is ₦1 while the individual has an income of ₦12. Determine the combination of the two commodities the individual should consume to maximize his utility|3y and 3x|6y and 4x|3y and 6x|5y and 5x": "A consumer maximises utility when the marginal utility per naira is the same for both goods and the whole income is spent: MUy/Py = MUx/Px and 2y + 1x = ₦12.\nStep 1: MUy per naira (MUy ÷ ₦2) is 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1; MUx per naira is MUx itself (price ₦1).\nStep 2: Find matching values: 6 occurs at y = 3 and x = 6.\nStep 3: Spending = 3 × ₦2 + 6 × ₦1 = ₦12, exactly the income: C.\nThe trap: A (3y and 3x) uses the right y but spends only ₦9; D (5y and 5x) costs ₦15, more than the income.\nMemory tip: equal \"MU per naira\" AND spend all the money: both conditions must hold.",
 "|When two variables are positively related, the graph of the relationship|is a straight line|is a downward-sloping curve|is an upward sloping curve|has a negative intercept": "Two variables are positively (directly) related when they move in the same direction: as one rises, the other rises too, as with the price of a good and the quantity supplied. Plotted on a graph, such a relationship climbs from the bottom left to the top right, so the curve slopes upwards: C. A downward-sloping curve (B) shows a negative (inverse) relationship, such as price and quantity demanded. A straight line (A) may slope either way, so it does not tell us the relationship is positive, and the intercept (D) says nothing about the direction of the relationship.\nMemory tip: Positive = uPward; Negative = dowNward.",
 "|For an inferior good, a decrease in real income will lead to|an outward shift of the demand curve|an inward shift of the demand curve|a lowered equilibrium price|a change in quantity demanded": "For a normal good, demand rises when income rises. An inferior good behaves the opposite way: as real income falls, people can no longer afford better substitutes, so they buy more of the inferior good. A change in income is a non-price determinant, so it shifts the whole demand curve. More demand at every price means the curve shifts outwards to the right: A. An inward shift (B) would happen if income rose. A movement along the curve, a change in quantity demanded (D), is caused only by a change in the good's own price.\nThe trap: B is what happens to a normal good, which is the case students usually learn first.\nMemory tip: Inferior goods go In the Opposite direction to income.",
 "|In a planned economy, the emphasis is on|individual choice and decisions|public ownership and control|private ownership and control|prices and competition": "A planned (command or socialist) economy is one in which the state owns the means of production (land, factories, mines) and a central planning authority decides what to produce, how to produce and for whom. The emphasis is therefore on public ownership and control: B. Individual choice (A), private ownership (C) and prices and competition (D) are the features of a capitalist (free market) economy, in which the price mechanism allocates resources. A mixed economy, like Nigeria's, combines both public and private sectors.\nMemory tip: Planned = Public; Capitalist = private Choice.",
 "|Price elasticity of supply is a ratio of the change in|quantity supplied to the change in demand|original quantity to a change in new quantity|quantity supplied to the change in price|price to the change in quantity supplied": "Price elasticity of supply measures how responsive the quantity supplied is to a change in the price of the good.\nFormula: Es = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price.\nSo it is the ratio of the change in quantity supplied to the change in price: C. Option D turns the ratio upside down, and A uses demand instead of price. If Es is greater than 1, supply is elastic (producers can easily expand output, as with manufactured goods); if it is less than 1, supply is inelastic (as with cocoa in the short run).\nThe trap: D has the same two items as C but the wrong way round; the quantity always goes on top.\nMemory tip: elasticity is always \"Quantity over Price\": Q on top.",
 "|The long-run average cost curve is called a planning curve because it shows what happens to costs when|variable inputs are increased|fixed factors are increased|different sizes of plants are built|a bigger size of plants is built": "In the long run all factors are variable, so a firm can choose any size of plant. For each plant size there is a short-run average cost (SAC) curve. The long-run average cost (LRAC) curve touches the lowest-cost part of all these short-run curves (it is their \"envelope\"). It therefore shows the lowest cost of producing each output when different sizes of plant can be built, and a firm uses it before investing to decide which plant to build. That is why it is called a planning curve: C. Changing only variable inputs (A) is the short run, and D refers to just one bigger plant, not the full range of plant sizes.\nMemory tip: LRAC = Long-run Range of All plant sizes = the Planning envelope.",
 "|Imperfect market is characterized by|many buyers and few sellers|a large number of buyers and sellers|non-preferential treatment|perfect mobility of factors of production": "A perfect market has many buyers and many sellers, a homogeneous product, perfect knowledge, free entry and exit, perfect mobility of factors and no preferential treatment of buyers. An imperfect market lacks one or more of these features. Its typical sign is a small number of sellers (as in monopoly or oligopoly) facing many buyers, so each seller has some control over price: A. A large number of buyers and sellers (B), non-preferential treatment (C) and perfect mobility of factors (D) are all features of a perfect market.\nMemory tip: Few sellers = Price-makers (imperfect); Many sellers = Price-takers (perfect).",
 "|Short-run period in production is a period too short for a firm to be able to change its|variable inputs|total outputs|total revenue|scale of operation": "The short run is a period during which at least one factor of production, such as the factory building, machinery or land, is fixed. It is too short for the firm to build a bigger or smaller plant, so it cannot change its scale of operation: D. In the short run the firm changes output only by varying inputs such as labour and raw materials, so variable inputs (A) are exactly what it CAN change. Total output (B) and total revenue (C) are results of production, not what defines the period. In the long run all factors are variable and the scale can change.\nThe trap: A is what the firm can change in the short run; the question asks what it is too short to change.\nMemory tip: Short run = Some factors fixed; Long run = aLL factors variable.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput produced per day (unit) ... Fixed cost per day (₦) ... Total cost per day (₦)\n20 ... 60 ... 100\n30 ... 60 ... 120\n40 ... 60 ... 130\n50 ... 60 ... 135\n60 ... 60 ... 150\n70 ... 60 ... 170\n80 ... 60 ... 190\n\nThe ATC at 30 units of output is|₦120.00|₦60.00|₦4.00|₦3.00": "Average total cost (ATC) is total cost per unit of output.\nStep 1: From the table, at 30 units the total cost is ₦120.\nStep 2: ATC = TC ÷ Q = ₦120 ÷ 30 = ₦4.00: C.\nCheck: ₦4 × 30 = ₦120.\nThe trap: A (₦120.00) is the total cost itself, the figure students copy from the table when they forget to divide by output. B (₦60.00) is the fixed cost.\nMemory tip: \"Average\" always means divide by output: ATC = TC/Q, AFC = FC/Q, AVC = VC/Q.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput produced per day (unit) ... Fixed cost per day (₦) ... Total cost per day (₦)\n20 ... 60 ... 100\n30 ... 60 ... 120\n40 ... 60 ... 130\n50 ... 60 ... 135\n60 ... 60 ... 150\n70 ... 60 ... 170\n80 ... 60 ... 190\n\nAt 60 units of output, the AVC is|₦150.00|₦90.00|₦2.50|₦1.50": "Average variable cost (AVC) is variable cost per unit of output, and variable cost is total cost minus fixed cost.\nStep 1: At 60 units, TC = ₦150 and FC = ₦60.\nStep 2: VC = ₦150 − ₦60 = ₦90.\nStep 3: AVC = ₦90 ÷ 60 = ₦1.50: D.\nThe trap: C (₦2.50) is the average total cost (₦150 ÷ 60), the slip of forgetting to remove fixed cost; B (₦90.00) is total variable cost, not per unit.\nMemory tip: TC = FC + VC, so VC = TC − FC; then divide by Q.",
 "|If the demand curve facing a firm is sharply downward sloping, the firm is likely to be|a perfect competitor|a monopolistic competitor as it can have a limited influence on price|an oligopolist as it can collude with other firms to have some influence on price|a monopolist as it can have a great influence on price": "The slope of a firm's demand curve shows how much power it has over price. A perfect competitor (A) faces a horizontal (perfectly elastic) demand curve, because it is a price taker. A monopolistic competitor (B) faces a gently sloping, fairly elastic curve, because there are many close substitutes. A monopolist is the only seller of a product with no close substitutes, so buyers cannot easily switch; its demand curve is the whole market demand curve and slopes sharply downwards (inelastic). The monopolist can therefore have a great influence on price: D.\nThe trap: B also says the firm influences price, but only a little, which matches a gentle slope, not a sharp one.\nMemory tip: the steeper the demand curve, the stronger the seller's grip on price.",
 "|A normal supply curve is usually positively sloped because the relationship between|demand and price is positive|supply and price is negative|price and demand is negative|price and supply is positive": "The law of supply states that, other things being equal, the higher the price, the greater the quantity supplied, and the lower the price, the less is supplied. Higher prices make production more profitable and draw in more resources and producers. Because price and quantity supplied move in the same direction, the relationship is positive (direct), and a normal supply curve slopes upwards from left to right: D. Options A and C talk about demand, which is not what shapes a supply curve, and B wrongly says the supply relationship is negative.\nThe trap: B names the right pair of variables (supply and price) but gives the wrong sign.\nMemory tip: Supply goes up the Slope: higher price, more supplied.",
 "|The best method of production in an under-populated country is|labour-extensive|land-intensive|capital-intensive|labour-intensive": "A country should use most of the factor it has in plenty and economise on the factor that is scarce. An under-populated country has too few people to make the best use of its land and other resources, so labour is scarce and dear. The sensible choice is to substitute machines for workers, that is, capital-intensive methods, as in heavily mechanised farming: C. Labour-intensive methods (D) suit densely populated countries with abundant cheap labour. \"Labour-extensive\" (A) and \"land-intensive\" (B) do not solve the problem of too few workers.\nThe trap: D is the method a crowded country with cheap labour would choose, the opposite case.\nMemory tip: Few people, more Capital; Many people, more labour.",
 "|Insurance companies, pension and provident funds and unit trusts are all examples of|government financial agencies|non-governmental organizations|non-bank financial institutions|rural-based revenue mobilisers": "Financial institutions are grouped into banks and non-bank financial institutions. Commercial banks accept deposits that can be withdrawn by cheque and can create credit. Insurance companies, pension and provident funds, unit trusts, building societies and finance houses also collect savings and invest them, but they do not accept chequeable deposits or create money. They are therefore non-bank financial institutions: C. They are not all government agencies (A), most are private businesses, and they are not NGOs (B) or rural revenue collectors (D).\nMemory tip: if it pools savings but issues no cheques, it is a Non-Bank Financial Institution.",
 "|For a firm, value added can be defined as the difference between the|value of its output and the cost of production|total revenue and total cost|input prices and product prices|value of its output and inputs purchased from other firms": "Value added is the extra value a firm creates at its own stage of production. It equals the value of the firm's output minus the value of the inputs (raw materials, fuel, components and services) it bought from other firms: D. Example: a baker sells bread worth ₦100,000 made from flour and other inputs costing ₦60,000; its value added is ₦40,000, which pays its workers, rent, interest and profit. Adding the value added at every stage gives national output without double counting. A and B describe profit, not value added, because cost of production includes the firm's own wages.\nThe trap: A looks similar but subtracts all costs, including wages, which leaves profit rather than value added.\nMemory tip: Value Added = Output − Bought-in inputs.",
 "|Securities are described as listed when they are|bought and sold purely for development purposes|mobilized as short-term debt instruments|mobilized as long-term debt instrument|traded on a recognized stock market": "A security (share, bond or debenture) is \"listed\" or \"quoted\" when it has been admitted to the official list of a recognised stock exchange, so that it can be bought and sold there, and its price is published daily: D. In Nigeria this is the Nigerian Exchange Limited (formerly the Nigerian Stock Exchange, demutualised in 2021). Listing requires the company to meet the exchange's rules and publish its accounts. Being long-term or short-term (B, C) describes the kind of instrument, not whether it is listed, and A is not a definition.\nMemory tip: Listed = on the List of a stock exchange.",
 "|The commercial banks differ from non-bank financial institutions because they|accept deposits withdrawable by cheque|mobilize savings|invest surplus funds|contribute to economic development": "Commercial banks and non-bank financial institutions both mobilise savings (B), invest funds (C) and contribute to development (D), so these do not separate them. What is special about commercial banks is that they accept current-account (demand) deposits that can be withdrawn by cheque. Cheques drawn on these deposits are used as money, which is why commercial banks can create credit and why the Central Bank watches them closely: A. Insurance companies, pension funds and unit trusts cannot issue chequebooks against your savings.\nMemory tip: Cheques = Commercial banks.",
 "|Inflation that is usually associated with periods of trade boom is|cost-push inflation|demand-pull inflation|creeping inflation|stagnation": "In a trade boom, incomes, employment, investment and spending all rise. Aggregate demand grows faster than the economy can increase output, so \"too much money chases too few goods\" and prices are pulled up: demand-pull inflation, B. Cost-push inflation (A) starts from rising costs of production, such as wages or imported inputs, and can happen even in a slump. Creeping inflation (C) describes only a slow rate of price increase, not its cause, and stagnation (D) is the opposite of a boom.\nThe trap: A is the other main cause of inflation; link \"boom\" to demand and \"rising costs\" to cost-push.\nMemory tip: Boom = Buyers pull prices up.",
 "|The term \"Near Money\" is best described as|a financial asset that is convertible to cash|a financial instrument that is readily convertible to cash|government financial instrument that is convertible to cash|time deposits with low interest rates": "Near money (quasi-money) consists of assets that are not generally accepted in payment but can be converted into cash quickly and with little or no loss of value: savings and time deposits, treasury bills, bills of exchange and certificates of deposit. The key idea is that conversion is ready (easy and quick), so the best description is a financial instrument readily convertible to cash: B. Option A leaves out \"readily\", and almost any asset is convertible to cash in the end. C wrongly limits near money to government instruments, and D picks one example only.\nThe trap: A is almost the same as B but drops the word \"readily\", which is the heart of near money.\nMemory tip: Near money is Nearly cash: one quick step away.",
 "|The distinguishing characteristic between the money market and the capital market lies in whether the|funds mobilized are private or public|securities are primary or secondary|debt instruments provided are long-term or short-term|securities are in debentures or ordinary shares": "Both markets bring together lenders and borrowers; what separates them is the period of the funds. The money market deals in short-term funds and instruments, usually up to one year, such as treasury bills, call money, commercial papers and certificates of deposit. The capital market deals in long-term funds, such as shares, government development stocks and debentures, traded on the stock exchange. So the distinction is whether the instruments are short-term or long-term: C. Primary and secondary (B) is a division within the capital market, and both markets handle private and public funds (A).\nMemory tip: Money market = Months; Capital market = years.",
 "|If Nigeria's composite price index in 1999 was 140.03% and 144.05% in 2000, the rate of inflation in 2000 was|4.02%|2.10%|2.06%|1.03%": "The rate of inflation is the percentage change in the price index.\nStep 1: Change in index = 144.05 − 140.03 = 4.02 points.\nStep 2: Rate = 4.02 ÷ 140.03 × 100 ≈ 2.87%.\nSo strictly the inflation rate in 2000 was about 2.87%, which is not among the options. The answer the examiners intended is A (4.02%), the rise in the index in points, as if it were measured from a base of 100. This question is marked as not fully correct, because A is a point change, not a percentage change: A.\nMemory tip: inflation rate = (new index − old index) ÷ old index × 100; always divide by the old index.",
 "|The monetary control instrument most effectively used by the Central Bank of Nigeria is the|discount rate|reserve ratio|margin requirements|open market operations": "The CBN controls money supply with instruments such as the bank rate (discount rate, now the Monetary Policy Rate), the cash reserve ratio, liquidity ratio and open market operations (OMO). OMO is the buying and selling of government securities, mainly treasury bills, to take money out of or put money into the banking system. After Nigeria moved from direct controls (credit ceilings) to indirect, market-based control in 1993, OMO became the CBN's principal and most effectively used tool: D. Margin requirements (C) are a minor tool for stock-market lending. Today the CBN still relies heavily on OMO, alongside the MPR and the cash reserve ratio.\nMemory tip: OMO = Open Market, Often used.",
 "|The major objectives of a revenue allocation formula in a country is to|share revenue between the public and private sectors|ensure the financial viability of the country|share revenue between the different tiers of government|divert revenue from areas of surplus to areas of need": "In a federation such as Nigeria, most revenue (especially oil revenue) is collected centrally into the Federation Account. A revenue allocation formula sets out how this money is shared vertically among the federal, state and local governments, and horizontally among the states using principles like population, equality of states and derivation. Its major objective is therefore to share revenue between the different tiers of government: C. Sharing with the private sector (A) is not its job. D describes one horizontal principle (need), not the main purpose.\nMemory tip: Revenue allocation = sharing the national \"cake\" among Tiers (federal, state, local).",
 "|The effectiveness of devaluation as a solution to a balance of payments problems depends on the|relative elasticities of demand and supply of imports and exports|foreign investments of business|value of visible and invisible goods|value of invisible items of both domestic and foreign trades": "Devaluation lowers the value of a country's currency, making its exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home. Whether this improves the balance of payments depends on how buyers and sellers respond: foreign demand for exports must rise enough, and local demand for imports must fall enough. If demand for exports and imports is elastic (the Marshall–Lerner condition: the elasticities add up to more than 1) and export supply can expand, devaluation works; if demand is inelastic, foreign exchange earnings may even fall. So its effectiveness depends on the relative elasticities of demand and supply of imports and exports: A.\nMemory tip: Devaluation needs Elastic trade to Deliver.",
 "|IBRD as an international monetary institution is concerned with the|balance of payments problems of member nations|financing of private business in member nations|development of infrastructure in member nations|improvement of trade among member nations": "The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD), the main arm of the World Bank, was set up at Bretton Woods in 1944 with the IMF. Its original aim was to rebuild war-damaged Europe; today it gives long-term loans and technical help to member countries for development projects such as roads, power, water, agriculture and education, that is, development of infrastructure: C. Balance of payments problems (A) are the IMF's area. Lending to private business (B) is the role of its sister body, the International Finance Corporation (IFC).\nMemory tip: IMF = Money problems (balance of payments); IBRD = Building Roads and Development.",
 "|An advantage of the sole proprietorship over the partnership form of business organization is that|it enjoys limited liability for debt in the event of failure|its existence is limited to an individual's lifespan|it relies on the decision of friends to succeed|the possibility of conflict in management is virtually non-existent": "A sole proprietorship has one owner, who provides the capital, takes all decisions and keeps all the profit. In a partnership, two or more partners share decisions, and they can disagree, sometimes causing the business to break up. Because the sole trader alone decides, there is virtually no possibility of conflict in management: D. A is false, because the sole trader has unlimited liability. B (limited lifespan) and C (relying on friends) are disadvantages, not advantages.\nThe trap: A sounds like an advantage, but unlimited liability is exactly what sole traders and ordinary partners share.\nMemory tip: Sole = Single boss = no quarrels, but no limit on liability.",
 "|The distinction between capital and recurrent expenditure lies in the|amount of expenditure involved|source of the revenue generated|nature of the goods and services to be provided|time frame of the expenditure": "Government spending is divided into recurrent and capital expenditure. Recurrent expenditure covers items used up within the financial year that recur year after year, such as salaries, pensions, maintenance and stationery. Capital expenditure is on projects and assets that last for many years, such as roads, schools, hospitals and dams. So the line between them is drawn by how long the spending lasts, the time frame of the expenditure: D. The amount (A) or the source of revenue (B) does not decide the class. C is close, because capital items are durable assets, but the classification turns on their lasting nature over time.\nMemory tip: Recurrent = Repeats every year; Capital = lasts for years.",
 "|Under floating exchange rate regime, the determinant of the exchange rate is|the system of government|demand for and supply of foreign goods|the highest denomination of the currency|an act of the national assembly": "Under a floating (flexible) exchange rate system, the value of a currency is not fixed by government but is determined by market forces, the demand for and supply of foreign exchange. Demand for foreign currency comes mainly from importers of foreign goods and services, and supply comes from exporters selling to foreigners. So the exchange rate rises or falls with the demand for and supply of foreign goods: B. The system of government (A), the highest currency note (C) and an act of the National Assembly (D) do not set a floating rate; a law setting the rate would mean a fixed system.\nMemory tip: Floating = Free market forces of demand and supply.",
 "|To compare the standard of living among nations, the most widely used economic indicator is the|per capita nominal income|real domestic output|real gross national income|real per capita income": "To compare living standards, economists divide national income by population to get income per head (per capita income), and use real figures to remove the effect of inflation. Real per capita income shows the goods and services the average person can actually buy, so it is the most widely used indicator: D. Nominal per capita income (A) is distorted by price levels. Real domestic output (B) and real gross national income (C) ignore population size, so a large, poor country could look richer than a small, rich one. \nThe trap: C is \"real\" too, but it is a total for the whole country, not per person.\nMemory tip: Real + Per head = true living standard.",
 "|Statutory organizations usually established by Acts of parliament are called|public enterprises|public corporations|cooperative societies|joint-stock companies": "A public corporation is a government-owned enterprise set up by a special Act of parliament (or decree), which gives it its name, powers, duties and management board. Examples in Nigeria included NEPA, NITEL, the Nigerian Railway Corporation and NNPC. They are statutory organisations because a statute creates them: B. \"Public enterprises\" (A) is a wider term that also covers government-owned companies registered under company law. Cooperatives (C) and joint-stock companies (D) are registered under ordinary law. Note that NEPA was replaced by PHCN in 2005 and the power companies were privatised in 2013.\nThe trap: A is the broad family name, while B is the specific statutory form.\nMemory tip: Statute = Special Act = State corporation.",
 "|A greater burden of the taxes on essential goods is borne by the|higher income group|newly recruited workers|lower income group|contract workers": "Taxes on essential goods (food, kerosene, soap) are indirect taxes. Rich and poor pay the same tax on each unit, but poor households spend a much larger share of their income on essentials. So the tax takes a bigger proportion of a low income than of a high one: it is regressive, and the lower income group bears the greater burden: C. The higher income group (A) pays the same amount per unit but it is a smaller part of their income. Newly recruited workers (B) and contract workers (D) are not a basis for the tax burden.\nMemory tip: taxes on necessities hit the Needy hardest.",
 "|Privatization and commercialization of public enterprises in Nigeria is necessitated by|the IMF conditions|the IBRD conditions|the government's loss of interest in them|their operational inefficiency": "In the 1980s Nigeria's public enterprises (NEPA, NITEL, Nigeria Airways and others) were widely seen as inefficient: they made losses, gave poor service, had too many staff and depended on government subsidies. The Privatisation and Commercialisation Decree of 1988, under the Structural Adjustment Programme, aimed to make them efficient and reduce the burden on the treasury. So their operational inefficiency was the main reason: D. IMF and World Bank conditions (A, B) encouraged reform, but the root cause was inefficiency. Since 1999 the Bureau of Public Enterprises has continued privatisation, including the 2013 sale of the PHCN power companies.\nMemory tip: Privatise because public firms Performed Poorly.",
 "|The theory of comparative advantage states that a commodity should be produced in that nation where the|absolute cost is least|absolute money cost is least|opportunity cost is least|production possibility curve increases": "David Ricardo's theory of comparative advantage says that even if one country is better at producing everything (absolute advantage), trade still benefits both countries if each specialises in the good in which it is relatively more efficient, that is, the good it can produce at the lowest opportunity cost (the least amount of the other good given up): C. Absolute cost (A) or absolute money cost (B) is Adam Smith's absolute advantage. An increase in the production possibility curve (D) is economic growth.\nThe trap: A and B describe absolute advantage, the older idea that comparative advantage improves on.\nMemory tip: Comparative = Cost in terms of what you Give up (opportunity cost).",
 "|The effect of an increase in the personal income tax is to|raise the absolute price level|distort the economy|reduce unemployment|reduce the disposable income": "Disposable income is the income a person has left to spend or save after paying direct taxes such as personal income tax: disposable income = income − direct taxes. If the rate of personal income tax goes up, more is deducted, so the taxpayer's disposable income falls: D. Lower disposable income tends to reduce consumer spending, which is why higher taxes are used to fight inflation, so raising the price level (A) is the opposite effect, and higher taxes do not reduce unemployment (C). B is too vague to be the direct effect.\nMemory tip: Tax up, take-home down.",
 "|An important factor hindering the rapid development of the industrial sector in Nigeria is|excessive demand for finished products|the dominance of the oil sector|rural-urban migration|inadequate infrastructure facilities": "Industries need reliable electricity, good roads and railways, water and communications. In Nigeria, poor power supply forces firms to run their own generators, bad roads raise transport costs, and these extra costs make local products dear and uncompetitive. Inadequate infrastructure facilities is therefore a major factor hindering rapid industrial development: D. Excessive demand for finished products (A) would encourage, not hinder, industry. Dominance of the oil sector (B) and rural-urban migration (C) are real issues but are not the direct, important hindrance named in the syllabus.\nMemory tip: no Power, no Roads, no Progress in industry.",
 "|The demand for labour is an example of|competitive demand|derived demand|composite demand|joint demand": "Demand for a factor of production is derived demand: it depends on the demand for the goods and services that the factor helps to produce. Employers hire bricklayers because people want houses, and bakers because people want bread; if demand for bread falls, demand for bakers falls too. So the demand for labour is derived demand: B. Joint demand (D) is when goods are demanded together (car and fuel), composite demand (C) is when one good has several uses (sugar for tea and for soft drinks), and competitive demand (A) is for substitutes.\nMemory tip: Derived = Depends on the product's demand.",
 "|The major role of multi-national companies in the petroleum industry in Nigeria is|oil prospecting only|oil marketing only|the establishment of refineries|oil marketing and prospecting": "Nigeria's oil industry has been run largely through joint ventures between NNPC and multinational oil companies such as Shell, ExxonMobil, Chevron, Total and Agip. These firms explore for (prospect) and produce crude oil, and several also market petroleum products through their filling stations. Their major role is therefore both prospecting and marketing: D. Options A and B say \"only\", which leaves out half of what they do. Refineries (C) in Nigeria were built and owned mainly by the government through NNPC. Since the Petroleum Industry Act 2021, NNPC has become NNPC Limited, a commercial company.\nMemory tip: Multinationals: Mine (prospect) and Market.",
 "|Fixing the prices of agricultural products can be a problem because of the|unpredictable output of farmers|instability of government policies|activities of marketing boards|size of agricultural exports": "Agricultural output depends on weather, rainfall, pests, diseases and the number of farmers who plant a crop, so harvests vary unpredictably from year to year. When output is large, prices tend to fall; when it is small, they rise. A price fixed in advance may therefore cause gluts or shortages, and maintaining it needs costly buying and storage. That is why fixing agricultural prices is a problem: the unpredictable output of farmers, A. Unstable government policy (B) and marketing boards (C) are about how prices were fixed, not why fixing them is difficult. The commodity marketing boards were abolished in 1986 under SAP.\nMemory tip: Uncertain harvest, Uncertain price.",
 "|Occupational mobility as applied to factors of production means the ease by which|factors can be transferred from one place to another|factors can be transferred from one form of use to another|resources can be transferred from one place to another|resources can be transformed from one form to another": "Factors of production can be mobile in two ways. Occupational mobility is the ease with which a factor can move from one use or job to another, for example a teacher becoming a banker, or land used for cassava being used for maize: B. Geographical mobility is the ease of moving from one place to another, as in options A and C. Option D talks about transforming resources, which is production, not mobility. Training and general skills raise occupational mobility.\nThe trap: A and C describe geographical mobility, the closely related twin concept.\nMemory tip: Occupational = Occupation (job) changes; Geographical = Geography (place) changes.",
 "|The labour force of a country can be determined by examining the|sex distribution of the population|occupational distribution of the population|age structure of the population|geographical distribution of the population": "The labour force (working population) is made up of people of working age, usually 15 to 64 years in Nigeria, who are working or are able and willing to work. To know how big it is, we look at the age structure (age distribution) of the population, which shows how many people fall in the young dependent, working-age and old dependent groups: C. Sex distribution (A) tells us male and female numbers, occupational distribution (B) shows how those already working are spread over jobs, and geographical distribution (D) shows where people live.\nThe trap: B sounds close because it is about jobs, but it shows how workers are spread, not how many there are.\nMemory tip: Labour force = working Age, so look at the Age structure.",
 "|Two basic features common to an industry are|similarity of products and production processes|similarity of products and source of raw materials|production processes and source of capital|source of capital and labour supply": "A firm is a single business unit; an industry is a group of firms producing the same or similar products, such as the cement industry or the textile industry. Firms in one industry usually use similar production processes as well, with similar raw materials and machines. So the two basic common features are similarity of products and of production processes: A. Option B includes the source of raw materials, which can differ (one textile mill may use local cotton, another imported yarn). The source of capital (C, D) and labour supply (D) differ from firm to firm.\nThe trap: B shares \"similarity of products\" with A, so it looks right at first glance.\nMemory tip: Industry = Identical products, Identical processes.",
 "|A country is said to be over populated when|there are too many able-bodied men and women in the country|the resources are more than enough to cater for the population|the size of the population is greater than the annual budget|the resources are inadequate to cater for the population": "Over-population, under-population and optimum population all compare the size of the population with the resources (land, capital, technology) available. A country is over-populated when its population is too large for its resources, so resources are inadequate and income per head is lower than it would be with fewer people: D. B describes under-population (resources more than enough). Many able-bodied people (A) can be an asset if resources match them, and comparing population with the annual budget (C) makes no sense.\nThe trap: B uses the same words but describes the opposite situation, under-population.\nMemory tip: Over-populated = Over-stretched resources.",
 "|When a union is composed of workers with same skill, it is called|an industrial union|a workers' union|a craft union|a technical union": "Trade unions are organised in different ways. A craft union is made up of workers who share the same skill or trade, wherever they work, for example electricians or carpenters: C. An industrial union (A) brings together all workers in one industry, whatever their skills, such as all workers in the petroleum industry. A general (workers') union (B) recruits workers of many skills from many industries. \"Technical union\" (D) is not a standard type.\nThe trap: A is the other main type of union, organised by industry instead of by skill.\nMemory tip: Craft = same Craft (skill); Industrial = same Industry.",
 "|An adverse environmental impact of the petroleum industry on the economy is|the vandalisation of oil equipment|oil spillage|communal riots|community development": "The petroleum industry earns most of Nigeria's foreign exchange, but it also has harmful effects on the environment. Oil spillage from pipelines, wells and tankers pollutes rivers, creeks and farmland in the Niger Delta, killing fish and crops and destroying the livelihoods of farmers and fishermen: B. Gas flaring is another serious environmental effect. Vandalisation of equipment (A) and communal riots (C) are social problems linked to the industry, not environmental impacts of it, and community development (D) is a benefit, not a harm.\nMemory tip: Environmental harm = what the oil Does to land, water and air (spills, flaring).",
 "|By advertising, a monopolistic competitive firm tries to shift its|supply curve to the right|demand curve to the right|supply curve to the left|demand curve to the left": "A firm in monopolistic competition sells a product that is slightly different from its rivals' products, so it can use advertising and branding to win customers. Successful advertising makes more people want the product at every price, so the whole demand curve for the firm's product moves to the right (and often becomes less elastic, because buyers become loyal to the brand). Advertising does not change the firm's costs of producing each unit in a way that shifts supply, and a firm would never pay to push its own demand to the left: B. Memory tip: Adverts Add buyers, so demand moves Right.",
 "|Given that Y = C + I and C = bY where b = 0.8, what is the multiplier?|5|10|4|2": "In this simple economy Y = C + I and consumption C = bY, so b is the marginal propensity to consume (MPC). The multiplier tells us how much income rises for each ₦1 of new investment.\nStep 1: MPC = b = 0.8, so MPS = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2.\nStep 2: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5.\nSo every ₦1 of extra investment raises national income by ₦5: A. The trap: D (2) is what students get if they divide by the MPC wrongly or mix up the formula; always divide 1 by the MPS. Memory tip: k = 1/MPS, and a high MPC means a big multiplier.",
 "|The role of the Central Bank in the economic development of Nigeria is to|give short-and medium-term loans to the public|give financial and technical advice to customers|use the cheques and bank drafts to facilitate business transactions|maintain a stable price system": "The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN), set up in 1958 and opened in 1959, is the bankers' bank and the government's bank. It does not lend to members of the public, advise individual customers or handle their cheques; those are jobs of commercial banks. Its contribution to development is through monetary policy: controlling money supply, interest rates and credit so that prices stay stable and the naira keeps its value, which encourages saving and investment: D. Options A, B and C describe what commercial banks do. Memory tip: CBN = Controls money, Banks for banks, No public customers.",
 "|The primary function of non-bank financial institutions is to|manage investment portfolios for government|mobilize saving for investment|grant loans to banks|receive deposits from banks": "Non-bank financial institutions include insurance companies, pension funds, building societies (primary mortgage institutions), finance houses and unit trusts. They do not take ordinary current-account deposits or create money as banks do. Their main job is to collect savings from people (premiums, pension contributions, savings plans) and channel them into long-term investment such as mortgages, shares and government stocks: B. They do not exist mainly to lend to banks or to manage government's portfolio. Memory tip: Non-banks are savings funnels: Savings in, Investment out.",
 "|At the maximum point of the total product curve of a firm, marginal product is|decreasing|increasing|constant|zero": "Total product (TP) is total output; marginal product (MP) is the extra output from one more unit of a variable factor such as labour. While MP is positive, TP keeps rising. When MP falls to zero, adding another worker adds nothing, so TP stops rising and is at its maximum. If more workers are added after that, MP becomes negative and TP falls. So at the peak of the TP curve, MP is zero: D. MP is decreasing on the way to the peak, but at the peak itself its value is zero. Memory tip: TP at the top, MP hits the floor (zero).",
 "|In an open economy, the GNP is measured as|C + I + G + X|C + I + G + (X − M)|C + I + G + M|C + I + G": "Expenditure on a country's output comes from households (C), firms (I) and government (G). In a closed economy that is all. In an open economy foreigners also buy our exports (X), while part of our spending goes on imports (M), so we add net exports: C + I + G + (X − M): B. Strictly, this expression gives GDP at market prices; adding net factor income from abroad turns it into GNP, but of the options only B correctly includes the foreign sector. A forgets imports, C adds them instead of subtracting, and D is a closed economy. Memory tip: Open economy = add X, take away M.",
 "|The diagram above represents the short-run position of a monopolist. The profit-maximizing output is|Q3|Q4|Q1|Q2": "Every firm, including a monopolist, maximises profit where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR), with MC cutting MR from below. A monopolist's MR line lies below its AR (demand) curve and falls twice as steeply. In the diagram, the steep straight line falling from the price axis is MR, and it crosses the MC curve directly above output Q2, so Q2 is the profit-maximising output: D. The other marked outputs are not where MC equals MR, so producing there would either leave extra profit unearned or add units that cost more than they bring in. Memory tip: Profit peaks where MC = MR.",
 "|The capital market provides business firms with the avenue to|lease capital equipment|obtain long-term funds|obtain short-term funds|purchase capital equipment": "Financial markets are split by the length of the loan. The money market (treasury bills, call money, commercial papers) provides short-term funds. The capital market, including the stock exchange, is where firms raise long-term funds by selling shares, debentures and bonds, and where governments sell development stocks: B. Option C describes the money market, so do not mix the two. Leasing or buying equipment (A and D) is what firms may do with the funds, not what the market provides. The trap: C (short-term funds) catches students who confuse the capital market with the money market. Memory tip: Capital market = Long life money; money market = short.",
 "|Which of the financial institutions is charged with the formulation of monetary policies in Nigeria?|The Central Bank of Nigeria|The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation|The Abuja Commodity Exchange|The Lagos Stock Exchange": "The Central Bank of Nigeria is responsible for formulating and implementing monetary policy, using tools such as the monetary policy rate, open market operations, cash reserve ratio and liquidity ratio. NDIC insures bank deposits and helps supervise banks, the Abuja Commodity Exchange was set up for trading farm commodities, and the Lagos Stock Exchange (today the Nigerian Exchange, NGX) is a market for shares and bonds: A. Memory tip: Monetary policy belongs to the Monetary authority, the CBN.",
 "|GDP differs from GNP because GDP|is inclusive of depreciation|emphasizes the final goods and services produced within the country at a particular period|emphasizes the goods and services produced within the country|emphasizes the market value of all final goods and services produced by nationals": "Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the value of all final goods and services produced within a country's borders in a period, whoever produces them. Gross National Product (GNP) is the value produced by the country's citizens and their firms, wherever they are: GNP = GDP + net factor income from abroad. So the key difference is \"within the country\" versus \"by nationals\": C. Option B says the same thing in fuller words, so it is also accepted; D describes GNP and A (depreciation) is true of both gross measures. Memory tip: Domestic = inside the Door; National = our Nationals anywhere.",
 "|The distinction between perfect competition and monopolistic competition is that the latter is characterized by|a single market price|many buyers and sellers|product differentiation|homogeneity of product": "Perfect competition and monopolistic competition both have many buyers and sellers and free entry. The key difference is the product: in perfect competition every firm sells an identical (homogeneous) product at a single market price, while in monopolistic competition each firm's product is differentiated by brand, packaging, quality or location: C. Options A, B and D are features of perfect competition or of both structures. Memory tip: Monopolistic competition = Many sellers, Many brands.",
 "|The downstream activities of oil companies in Nigeria involve|refining and distribution|prospecting and exploration|off-shore drilling|management of spillage and pollution": "The oil industry is divided into stages. Upstream covers exploration, prospecting and drilling to produce crude oil, including offshore drilling. Downstream covers what happens to the oil after it is produced: refining it into petrol, kerosene and diesel, then transporting, storing and selling these products through depots and filling stations. So downstream activities involve refining and distribution: A. Options B and C are upstream activities. Memory tip: Upstream finds the oil; Downstream delivers the products.",
 "|The movement of goods and services from the producers to the consumers is referred to as|external trade|the distribution chain|the circular flow|consumption": "Production is not complete until goods reach the people who use them. The route goods follow from the producer (manufacturer) through wholesalers and retailers to the final consumer is called the chain (or channel) of distribution: B. The circular flow describes payments and goods moving between households and firms in the whole economy, external trade is trade with other countries, and consumption is the final use of goods. Memory tip: Producer, Wholesaler, Retailer, Consumer: the links of the distribution chain.",
 "|The purpose of conducting a census is to enable a country to|plan her capital inflow and outflow|determine the growth rate of population|determine the optimum level of population|plan her growth and development": "A census counts the whole population and records age, sex, occupation, location and other details. Government uses these facts for planning: how many schools, hospitals, houses and jobs are needed, where to build roads, how to share revenue and seats. So the purpose is to help the country plan her growth and development: D. A census can show the growth rate (B), but that figure is itself only useful as an input to planning. Nigeria's last accepted census was in 2006. Memory tip: Count people to Plan for people.",
 "|A major cause of the slow pace of industrialization in Nigeria is inadequate|raw materials|landmass|skilled labour|communal labour": "Nigeria has many raw materials (oil, gas, tin, cotton, cocoa) and a large land area, so A and B are not the problem. A major obstacle to industrialisation has been the shortage of skilled labour: engineers, technicians and managers who can build, run and maintain modern factories. Other problems include poor power supply, weak infrastructure and inadequate capital. Communal labour (D) has nothing to do with factories: C. Memory tip: Machines need Minds: no skills, slow industry.",
 "|The maximum wage legislation seeks to|fix wages above the equilibrium wage rate|set wages below the equilibrium wage rate|deter employers from exploiting their employees|ensure an upper limit to the wages payable": "A maximum wage is a legal ceiling: employers may not pay more than the stated amount. It is an upper limit on wages payable (D). Like any maximum price, it only bites if it is set below the equilibrium wage that the market would otherwise reach (B); a ceiling above equilibrium would change nothing. Because the two options describe the same policy from two angles, both are accepted, and the answer shown is: B. A describes a minimum wage, and C is the aim of minimum wage laws. Memory tip: Maximum = ceiling, set below; Minimum = floor, set above.",
 "|The marketing of agricultural commodities in Nigeria is hampered by|lack of adequate storage facilities|the existence of farmers' associations|the existence of commodity exchange|the abolition of marketing boards": "Many Nigerian farm products, especially food crops like tomatoes, yams and vegetables, are perishable. Without good storage (silos, cold rooms, warehouses), farmers must sell immediately at harvest when prices are lowest, and much of the produce rots before reaching markets. This lack of adequate storage facilities is a major marketing problem: A. Farmers' associations and commodity exchanges help marketing rather than hamper it. Other problems include poor roads and too many middlemen. Memory tip: No Store, No Sale at a good price.",
 "|Import duty concessions on industrial inputs directly benefit the|consumers|external sector|commercial sector|manufacturers": "An import duty concession lowers or removes the tax on goods brought into the country. When the concession is on industrial inputs (machinery, spare parts, raw materials), the people who import and use those inputs, the manufacturers, pay less, so their production costs fall directly: D. Consumers may gain later if lower costs bring lower prices, but that benefit is indirect. The policy is meant to encourage local manufacturing. Memory tip: Inputs are for Industry, so the Manufacturer gains first.",
 "|The labour force in Nigeria falls between the ages of|18 and 45|18 and 55|18 and 60|18 and 30": "The labour force (working population) is the people of working age who are able and willing to work. In Nigerian economics textbooks of the time, working age was taken as from about 18, after secondary school, up to 60, the normal retirement age in the public service: C. Today the National Bureau of Statistics follows the international standard of 15 to 64 years. The trap: B (18 and 55) catches students who remember an older retirement age. Memory tip: Work from 18 to 60, then retire.",
 "|Government's involvement in agricultural business in Nigeria is most manifested in the|establishment of farm settlements in each state of the federation|establishment of credit guarantee schemes for small-scale farmers|formation of farmers' cooperative societies|procurement and distribution of fertilizers": "Government takes part in agriculture in many ways: research institutes, credit schemes, extension services, farm settlements and input supply. Of these, the most widespread and visible has been the procurement (bulk buying) and distribution of fertilizers to farmers, often at subsidised prices, through federal and state agencies: D. Farm settlements were limited mainly to the old regions in the 1960s, and cooperatives are formed by farmers themselves. Memory tip: Government Feeds the Farms with Fertilizer.",
 "|The difference between birth and death rates of a given population is referred to as the|mortality rate|fertility rate|natural growth rate|linear growth rate": "Birth rate is the number of live births per 1,000 people in a year; death (mortality) rate is the number of deaths per 1,000. The birth rate minus the death rate is the natural growth rate (natural increase) of the population, which ignores migration. For example, a birth rate of 40 per 1,000 and a death rate of 15 per 1,000 gives a natural growth rate of 25 per 1,000, or 2.5%: C. Mortality rate (A) is only the deaths side. Memory tip: Births minus Deaths = Natural growth.",
 "|Fiscal policy is the government's plan to control aggregate demand by manipulating|the demand and supply of money|revenue and expenditure|tastes and preferences of consumers|the structure of production and employment": "Government has two main tools for managing aggregate demand. Fiscal policy works through the budget: changing taxes and other revenue, and changing government spending. Raising spending or cutting taxes boosts demand; cutting spending or raising taxes reduces it: B. Monetary policy, run by the Central Bank, works through the supply of money and interest rates (A). The trap: A (the demand and supply of money) catches students who mix up fiscal and monetary policy. Memory tip: Fiscal = the Fisc, the government's purse: Taxes and Spending.",
 "|A country is said to have absolute advantage when she|has a higher opportunity cost in the production of a good|can sell to other countries without buying from them|can produce more of any good with fewer resources|has a lower opportunity cost in the production of a good": "A country has an absolute advantage in a good when it can produce more of it than another country with the same resources, or the same amount with fewer resources: C. This was Adam Smith's basis for trade. Having a lower opportunity cost (D) is comparative advantage, David Ricardo's idea, which explains why trade benefits both countries even when one is better at everything. The trap: D catches students because it is the definition of comparative, not absolute, advantage. Memory tip: Absolute = Amount (more output); Comparative = Cost (lower opportunity cost).",
 "|The European Union was formed to|promote free trade within member nations|promote economic integration in non-member countries|assist countries to achieve their economic goals|finance development projects in member nations": "The European Union grew from the European Economic Community (EEC), set up by the Treaty of Rome in 1957, and was formally created by the Maastricht Treaty in 1993. Its central aim was to remove tariffs and other barriers so goods, services, capital and people move freely among member states, forming a single market; it later added a common currency, the euro, for many members: A. It does not work for integration among non-member countries (B). Memory tip: EU = Easy movement within the Union.",
 "|If budget deficits are financed by borrowing, the crowding-out effects can be offset by an increase in|government expenditure|savings|interest rates|exchange rates": "When government borrows to finance a deficit, it competes with private firms for the available pool of loanable funds. This raises interest rates, and some private investment that would have happened is \"crowded out\". The effect is reduced if the pool of funds grows, that is, if savings increase, so government and private borrowers can both be served without interest rates rising as much: B. More government spending (A) or higher interest rates (C) would make crowding out worse. Memory tip: More Savings, more room for borrowers: Savings soften crowding out.",
 "|When the importation of a commodity is limited to a definite quantity, the trade control means used is known as|exchange control|tax relief|devaluation|quotas": "Governments restrict imports in several ways. A quota sets a fixed maximum quantity (or value) of a good that may be imported in a given period, for example 50,000 tonnes of rice a year: D. Exchange control rations foreign currency, devaluation lowers the value of the home currency to make imports dearer, and tax relief reduces taxes. Only a quota limits imports to a definite quantity. Memory tip: Quota = Quantity limit.",
 "|The excess of aggregate expenditure over full employment level of output is referred to as|GNP gap|deflationary gap|inflationary gap|recessionary gap": "An inflationary gap exists when aggregate expenditure (C + I + G + X − M) is greater than the value of output the economy can produce at full employment. Since output cannot rise further, the excess spending pushes up prices: C. The opposite, where spending falls short of the full-employment level, is a deflationary (recessionary) gap, so B and D describe the reverse situation. Memory tip: Too much spending, prices Inflate; too little, the economy Deflates.",
 "|The tax levied on goods and services at each stage of production is|surtax|VAT|ad valorem tax|PAYE": "Value Added Tax (VAT) is an indirect tax charged at each stage of production and distribution, on the value added at that stage; each seller claims back the VAT paid on inputs, and the final consumer bears the full tax. Nigeria introduced VAT in 1993 at 5%; the rate is now 7.5%: B. Ad valorem tax (C) is any tax charged as a percentage of value, not specifically at each stage. Surtax is an extra tax on high incomes, and PAYE is income tax deducted from wages. Memory tip: VAT = tax on the Value Added at every step.",
 "|The number of shareholders in a private limited liability company ranges from|two to seven|two to three|two to fifty|two to twenty": "Under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) 1990, which applied when this paper was set, a private limited company needed at least two members and could have no more than fifty (excluding employees and former employees). A public company had a minimum of seven and no maximum: C. Today CAMA 2020 allows a private company to be formed by just one person, with the maximum still fifty. The trap: A (two to seven) mixes up the private company with the public company's minimum of seven. Memory tip: Private: 2 to 50.",
 "|A major reason for having a national development plan is to|ensure efficient use of available resources|involve people in national programmes|make government spend more on production|ensure that more citizens are involved in planning": "Resources (land, labour, capital) are scarce, while needs are many. A national development plan sets out the government's priorities and targets for a period, such as five years, so that scarce resources are directed to the most important uses and duplication and waste are avoided: A. Involving people (B, D) can be a feature of good planning, but the main reason for planning is efficient use of resources. Nigeria had development plans from 1946 onwards, including four national plans from 1962 to 1985. Memory tip: Plan = Put resources where they matter most.",
 "|The difference between payments and receipts for visible trade is called|bilateral trade|current balance|balance of payment|balance of trade": "Visible trade is trade in physical goods, such as crude oil and cocoa exported, or machinery imported. The difference between the value of visible exports (receipts) and visible imports (payments) is the balance of trade: D. The current account balance (B) is broader, adding invisible items like services and transfers, and the balance of payments (C) covers all transactions, including capital flows. The trap: B (current balance) is closely related but includes invisibles, so it is not the right name. Memory tip: Balance of Trade = goods only.",
 "|One of the causes of the present high rate of inflation in Nigeria is|increasing budget surplus|increasing factors costs|exchange rate appreciation|high capacity utilization": "Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level. In Nigeria in the early 2000s a major cause was cost-push: the rising cost of factors of production, such as fuel, electricity (and generators), imported raw materials after the naira's fall, wages and transport. Firms passed these costs on as higher prices: B. A budget surplus (A) takes money out of circulation, exchange-rate appreciation (C) makes imports cheaper, and high capacity utilization (D) means more output; all of these would reduce inflation. Memory tip: Costs go up, prices go up: cost-push.",
 "|The final stage in the production process of any commodity involves its movement from the|retailer to the wholesaler|producer to the wholesaler|retailer to the consumer|wholesaler to the retailer": "In economics, production is not complete until goods reach the final consumer who needs them, because satisfying wants is the purpose of production. The usual chain is producer to wholesaler to retailer to consumer, so the last stage is the movement from the retailer to the consumer: C. Option A reverses the direction, and B and D are earlier links in the chain. Memory tip: The Retailer hands over the baton to the consumer at the finish line.",
 "|A change in demand for a normal good means|a shift in the demand curve|a change in the price elasticity|the demand changes as price changes|a movement along a given demand curve": "Economists distinguish a change in demand from a change in quantity demanded. A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the same demand curve (a change in quantity demanded). A change in demand is caused by other factors, such as income, tastes, prices of related goods or population, and it shifts the whole demand curve to the right (increase) or left (decrease): A. For a normal good, for example, a rise in income shifts demand to the right. Options C and D describe a change in quantity demanded. Memory tip: Price moves you Along; anything else Shifts the curve.",
 "|In the diagram above, ST is the budget line while I, II and III represent indifference curves. A rational consumer will choose to consume at point|M|T|K|L": "A rational consumer wants to reach the highest indifference curve possible, but must stay on or inside the budget line ST. The best point is where the budget line just touches (is tangent to) the highest attainable indifference curve; there the slope of the curve equals the price ratio. In the diagram this is point L on curve II: D. Point M is on the higher curve III but lies outside the budget line, so it cannot be afforded, and K is affordable but lies on the lower curve I, giving less satisfaction. Memory tip: Equilibrium is where the budget Line Lightly touches the curve.",
 "|In order to increase revenue, the seller of a commodity whose demand is fairly elastic is advised to|increase price|reduce output|reduce price|retain price": "When demand is elastic (elasticity greater than 1), the percentage change in quantity demanded is larger than the percentage change in price. So if the seller reduces the price, sales rise by a bigger proportion and total revenue (price × quantity) increases. For example, cutting price by 10% might raise sales by 20%. Raising the price would lose more in sales than it gains in price: C. If demand were inelastic, the opposite advice (raise price) would apply. Memory tip: Elastic: cut price, revenue rises; Inelastic: raise price, revenue rises.",
 "|In any set of data, the median represents the|value that is most representative of the data|mid-position when the values are arranged in ascending or descending order|mid-position when the values are arranged randomly|difference between the highest and the lowest values when arranged in ascending order": "The median is the middle value of a set of data after the values have been arranged in order, either ascending or descending. For 3, 5, 8, 10, 12 the median is 8. With an even number of values, it is the average of the two middle ones: B. The data must be ordered first, so C is wrong. The difference between the highest and lowest values (D) is the range, and A loosely describes an average in general. Memory tip: Median = Middle, but only after you Line them up.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nCombination ... Beans (Unit) ... Rice (Unit)\nK ... 15 ... 19\nL ... 20 ... 17\nM ... 24 ... 16\n\nThe table above shows the combinations of beans and rice that can be purchased by a consumer. The opportunity cost of moving from K to L is|2 units of rice|5 units of beans|17 units of rice|20 units of beans": "Opportunity cost is what you give up to get something else. Read the table carefully.\nStep 1: At K the consumer has 15 beans and 19 rice; at L, 20 beans and 17 rice.\nStep 2: Moving from K to L, beans rise by 20 − 15 = 5 units.\nStep 3: Rice falls by 19 − 17 = 2 units.\nThe extra 5 beans cost the consumer 2 units of rice, so the opportunity cost is 2 units of rice: A. The trap: B (5 units of beans) is what is gained, not what is given up. Memory tip: Opportunity cost is always the thing you Lose.",
 "|One of the assumptions of ordinalist utility in consumer behaviour is that|consumers are irrational|marginal utility rises continuously|utility is measurable|utility can only be ranked": "There are two approaches to utility. The cardinalist (marginal utility) approach assumes utility can be measured in units called utils. The ordinalist approach, used with indifference curves, assumes only that consumers can rank combinations of goods in order of preference (first, second, third) without measuring how much satisfaction each gives: D. It also assumes consumers are rational, so A is wrong, and C is the cardinalist assumption. The trap: C (utility is measurable) catches students who mix up the cardinalist and ordinalist assumptions. Memory tip: Ordinal = Order (rank); Cardinal = Count (measure).",
 "|A large standard deviation is an indication of|the size of the sample|a wide spread around the mean|a wide difference between the mean and the mode|the difference between the highest and the lowest values": "The standard deviation measures dispersion: how far, on average, the values in a set lie from their mean. A small standard deviation means the values are bunched closely around the mean; a large one means they are widely spread out around it: B. The difference between highest and lowest values (D) is the range, a cruder measure of spread, and standard deviation says nothing direct about sample size or the mode. Memory tip: Standard deviation = Spread around the mean.",
 "|A major characteristic of a mixed economy is that|economic decisions are made by both the rich and the poor|both primary and secondary production are undertaken|the federal and the state governments undertake joint projects|economic activities are undertaken by both the public and private sectors": "Economic systems are classified by who owns resources and makes economic decisions. In capitalism, private individuals and firms dominate; in socialism, the state does. A mixed economy combines both: the private sector runs most businesses for profit while government owns and runs some key enterprises and regulates the economy. So economic activities are undertaken by both the public and private sectors: D. Nigeria is an example of a mixed economy. Memory tip: Mixed = Public + Private.",
 "|Given the cost function C = 160 + 36Q, what is the average cost at 20 units of output?|₦720|₦216.00|₦44.00|₦880.00": "In the cost function C = 160 + 36Q, ₦160 is fixed cost and 36Q is variable cost.\nStep 1: At Q = 20, variable cost = 36 × 20 = ₦720.\nStep 2: Total cost = 160 + 720 = ₦880.\nStep 3: Average cost = total cost ÷ output = 880 ÷ 20 = ₦44.00.\nSo the answer is ₦44.00: C. The trap: D (₦880.00) is total cost, which students give when they forget to divide by output, and A (₦720) is only the variable cost. Memory tip: Average = Total ÷ Quantity.",
 "|If an increase in the price of good X leads to an increase in the supply of good Y, X and Y are said to be|jointly supplied|competitive|composite|jointly demanded": "Joint supply happens when producing one good automatically produces another, such as beef and hides from cattle, or petrol and kerosene from crude oil. If the price of X (beef) rises, more cattle are slaughtered to supply more beef, and so more hides (Y) are supplied too: A. With competitive supply (B), the goods compete for the same resources, so a higher price of X would reduce the supply of Y. Jointly demanded goods (D) are complements, like cars and petrol. The trap: B (competitive) is the closely related term where the effect goes the other way. Memory tip: Joint supply = by-product: more of one, more of the other.",
 "|The diagram above shows the shifts in both demand and supply curves. What is the new equilibrium point after the shifts?|E₃|E₁|E₄|E₂": "Equilibrium is where a demand curve crosses a supply curve. In the diagram the original curves are D₁ and S₁, meeting at E₄. Demand has increased to D₂ and supply has increased to S₂. The new equilibrium must be on both new curves, so it is where D₂ crosses S₂, labelled E₂: D. E₃ is D₂ with the old S₁, and E₁ is D₁ with the new S₂; each shows only one shift. The trap: E₃ (A) and E₁ (B) are points after just one of the two shifts. Memory tip: Both curves new? Use both new curves.",
 "|Use the diagram below to answer the question. S₁ and S₂ are supply curves; M and T lie on S₁ and N lies on S₂.\n\nWhen there is a change from T to N, it implies that|supply has increased|quantity supplied has increased|quantity demanded has increased|price has fallen": "In the diagram, point T lies on supply curve S₁ and point N lies on S₂, which is to the right of S₁. Moving from T to N means moving from one supply curve to a new one further right: at each price, more is now offered for sale. This is an increase in supply, caused by something other than the good's own price, such as better technology or lower costs: A. A change in quantity supplied (B) is a movement along one curve. The trap: B (quantity supplied has increased) catches students who do not check whether the two points are on the same curve. Memory tip: New curve = change in supply; same curve = change in quantity supplied.",
 "|Use the diagram below to answer the question. S₁ and S₂ are supply curves; M and T lie on S₁ and N lies on S₂.\n\nA movement from M to T implies that there has been|a decrease in supply|an increase in demand|a decrease in price|an increase in price": "Points M and T both lie on the same supply curve, S₁, with T higher up and to the right of M. A movement along a supply curve is caused only by a change in the good's own price: as price rises, producers offer more, so quantity supplied increases. Moving up from M to T therefore shows an increase in price: D. A decrease in supply (A) would be a shift of the whole curve to the left. Memory tip: Up the supply curve = price Up, quantity supplied Up.",
 "|A necessary condition for specialization in an economy is the existence of|a regulated market|a competitive market|a medium of exchange|adequate capital": "Specialisation means each person or region concentrates on producing one thing or doing one job. A specialist produces far more of one good than he needs and must exchange it for everything else he wants. Barter makes this very hard (double coincidence of wants), so the existence of money as a medium of exchange is a necessary condition for specialisation and division of labour to work: C. Markets and capital help, but without a medium of exchange specialists could not easily trade. Memory tip: No Money, No specialisation: you would eat only what you make.",
 "|A firm operating at full capacity will experience rising short-run total costs when|prices of its variable inputs rise|prices of its variable inputs fall|there is a change in management|labour productivity increases": "A firm at full capacity cannot raise output further in the short run, so its total cost can only rise if what it pays for inputs rises. If the prices of variable inputs, such as raw materials, fuel or wages, go up, the same output costs more to produce, and short-run total cost rises: A. Falling input prices (B) or higher labour productivity (D) would lower costs, and a change in management does not by itself raise costs. Memory tip: Same output, dearer inputs, higher total cost.",
 "|In a textile factory, the cost of cotton used is a typical example of|an average cost|a variable cost|a total cost|a fixed cost": "Costs are fixed or variable depending on whether they change with output. Fixed costs (rent, insurance, depreciation of machines) stay the same whatever the output. Variable costs change with output. In a textile factory, cotton is the main raw material: producing more cloth needs more cotton, and producing nothing needs none. So the cost of cotton is a typical variable cost: B. Average and total cost (A and C) are measures that include both fixed and variable costs. Memory tip: Raw materials Vary with output: Variable cost.",
 "|A black market can occur when|supply is in excess of demand|consumption of the commodity is restricted|prices are set by government above the equilibrium|prices are set by government below the equilibrium": "A black market is illegal trading at prices above the official price. It arises when the government fixes a maximum (controlled) price below the equilibrium price. At that low price, quantity demanded is greater than quantity supplied, creating a shortage; some buyers are willing to pay more than the official price, and sellers secretly sell to them: D. A price set above equilibrium (C) creates a surplus, not a black market, and supply in excess of demand (A) means a glut. The trap: C (above the equilibrium) is the minimum-price case, which causes a surplus instead. Memory tip: Price ceiling below equilibrium, Black market above it.",
 "|A profit-maximizing monopolist should produce within the range where his demand is|inelastic|elastic|infinitely elastic|unitarily elastic": "A monopolist maximises profit where marginal revenue (MR) equals marginal cost (MC). Marginal cost is always positive, so MR must be positive at the best output. On a straight-line demand curve, MR is positive only on the upper, elastic part; at unit elasticity MR is zero, and on the inelastic part MR is negative. If the monopolist were producing where demand is inelastic, cutting output would raise total revenue and also save cost, so profit would rise. Therefore a profit-maximising monopolist always produces where demand is elastic: B. The trap: D (unitarily elastic) is where total revenue is at its maximum, but profit is not maximised there because costs are positive. Memory tip: a monopolist never stays in the inelastic zone, because there MR is below zero.",
 "|In the circular flow diagram above, the arrow marked K indicates|the flow of capital goods|payments to factors of production|payments for goods and services|the flow of economic rent": "The simple circular flow has two sectors, households and firms, and two pairs of flows that run in opposite directions. In the factor market, households supply labour (labour inputs) to firms, and firms pay wages back to households. In the product market, firms send goods and services to households, and households pay money to firms in return. In the diagram, the inner bottom loop carries goods and services from the firms to the households, and the outer bottom loop, K, runs the other way, from households to firms. So K is the money households spend on goods and services: C. Payments to factors (B) are the wages on the top loop. Memory tip: every real flow has a money flow going the opposite way.",
 "|The savings deposit in a commercial bank is called|capital|fund|near money|call money": "Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment. Cash and current-account (demand) deposits, which can be spent by cheque, are money in the narrow sense. Savings and time deposits cannot be used directly to pay for goods, but they can be turned into cash quickly and with little or no loss, so they are called near money, or quasi-money: C. Other examples are treasury bills and bills of exchange. Call money (D) is money that banks lend to discount houses and other institutions, repayable at very short notice; it is not a description of a customer's savings deposit. Memory tip: near money is nearly cash, just one easy step away from spending.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nOutput (kg) ... 240 ... 450 ... 580 ... 630\nMR (₦) ... 75 ... 48 ... −40 ... −22\nMC (₦) ... 41 ... 48 ... 77 ... 20\n\nGiven the table above, what is the firm's short-run profit-maximizing output?|450kg|630kg|240kg|580kg": "A firm maximises profit where MR = MC, with MC cutting MR from below.\nStep 1: At 240kg, MR (₦75) is above MC (₦41), so producing more adds to profit.\nStep 2: At 450kg, MR = ₦48 and MC = ₦48: the last unit adds exactly as much to revenue as to cost, and MC has risen from ₦41 to ₦48.\nStep 3: At 580kg, MR is −₦40 and MC is ₦77, so those extra units lose money; at 630kg MR is still negative.\nSo profit is greatest at 450kg: A. Any output beyond the point where MR = MC adds more to cost than to revenue. Memory tip: produce until the extra revenue just equals the extra cost, MR = MC.",
 "|The regulatory authority of the capital market in Nigeria is the|Central Bank|Securities and Exchange Commission|Nigerian Deposit Insurance Corporation|Stock Exchange Market": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), set up in 1979, is the apex regulator of the Nigerian capital market. It registers securities and market operators, supervises the exchanges, and protects investors from fraud: B. The Central Bank of Nigeria (A) regulates banks and the money market, and the Nigerian Deposit Insurance Corporation (C) insures bank deposits. The stock exchange (D) is a market place that the SEC regulates, not the regulator itself. Today the SEC operates under the Investments and Securities Act, and the old Nigerian Stock Exchange is now Nigerian Exchange (NGX). Memory tip: Shares and Exchanges are policed by the SEC.",
 "|In the long run, the equilibrium point of a monopolistic firm is a point where the|marginal cost curve is tangential to the average fixed cost curve|demand curve is tangential to the average variable cost curve|supply curve is tangential to the marginal cost curve|demand curve is tangential to the average cost curve": "Under monopolistic competition, many firms sell similar but differentiated products, and entry is free. In the short run a firm can earn supernormal profit, but this attracts new firms with close substitutes. Each firm's demand curve then shifts to the left until price equals average cost, so only normal profit remains. Graphically, the downward-sloping demand (AR) curve just touches, or is tangential to, the average cost curve: D. At that point the firm is producing to the left of the lowest point of AC, which is why monopolistic competition is said to have excess capacity. Average fixed cost and average variable cost curves (A, B) do not decide long-run equilibrium. Memory tip: long run in monopolistic competition, AR kisses AC.",
 "|Given that Y = C + I, where C = 50 + 0.75Y and I = ₦45m, what is the equilibrium level of income?|₦380m|₦20m|₦180m|₦200m": "In a simple two-sector economy, income is in equilibrium where Y = C + I.\nStep 1: Substitute: Y = 50 + 0.75Y + 45.\nStep 2: Collect terms: Y − 0.75Y = 95, so 0.25Y = 95.\nStep 3: Y = 95 ÷ 0.25 = ₦380m: A.\nCheck: C = 50 + 0.75 × 380 = 50 + 285 = 335; C + I = 335 + 45 = 380. The multiplier is 1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 4, and (50 + 45) × 4 = 380 gives the same answer. Memory tip: equilibrium Y = autonomous spending × 1 ÷ (1 − MPC).",
 "|Above full employment level, an expansionary monetary policy will lead to a|fall in aggregate demand|decrease in aggregate supply|fall in the inflation rate|rise in the inflation rate": "Expansionary monetary policy (lower interest rates, more money supply) raises aggregate demand. Below full employment, idle labour and machines can be brought into use, so output rises. Above full employment, all resources are already in use, so output cannot rise much further. The extra spending then simply bids up prices: demand-pull inflation, a rise in the inflation rate: D. Aggregate demand rises, not falls (A), and the policy does not reduce aggregate supply (B). This is why central banks tighten money, not loosen it, when the economy is overheating. Memory tip: at full employment, more money chases the same goods, so prices rise.",
 "|A major assumption in a perfectly competitive market is that|the number of buyers and sellers is small|individuals cannot influence prices|the quality of products remains the same|prices will always remain constant": "Perfect competition rests on several assumptions: very many buyers and sellers, a homogeneous product, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge and perfect mobility of factors. Because each buyer or seller is tiny compared with the whole market, no individual can change the market price by buying or selling more or less; everyone is a price taker: B. The number of buyers and sellers is large, not small (A). Price is set by market demand and supply and can change when they change, so it does not always remain constant (D). Memory tip: in perfect competition the market sets the price and every firm simply takes it.",
 "|A discount house is a market where|various bills are exchanged|short-term and medium-term loans are obtained|shares are bought and sold|short-term loans are obtained": "A discount house is a money-market institution. It borrows short-term funds (call money) from commercial banks and uses them to buy, sell and discount short-term instruments such as treasury bills, treasury certificates and bills of exchange. In effect, it is a market where these bills change hands: A. Shares are bought and sold on the stock exchange, which is part of the capital market (C), and medium-term loans (B) come from banks and development finance institutions. Nigeria's first discount house began operating in 1993; discount houses were later converted into other types of financial institution. Memory tip: discount houses deal in Bills; the stock exchange deals in Shares.",
 "|Idle cash balances are held for|planned purchases|the purchase of bonds|the purchase of shares|unplanned purchases": "Keynes named three motives for holding money: transactions (everyday spending), precautionary (unforeseen needs) and speculative (waiting to buy bonds when their prices are favourable). Money held for transactions is active. Keynes called speculative money idle balances, held ready for the purchase of bonds: B. Many Nigerian textbooks, however, group precautionary money for unplanned purchases with speculative money as idle, because neither is spent in the normal course of business, so D is also accepted. Planned purchases (A) are the transactions motive, which uses active balances. Memory tip: Transactions = active; Speculation (and, in many texts, precaution) = idle.",
 "|In a particular year, the sum of the value added in each sector of the economy was ₦4,550 million. The amount represents the GNP measured by the|factor-price approach|output approach|income approach|expenditure approach": "National income can be measured in three ways. The output (product or value-added) approach adds up the value added by every sector, that is, the value of each firm's output minus the goods and services bought from other firms, to avoid double counting. The income approach adds up wages, rent, interest and profit, and the expenditure approach adds up C + I + G + (X − M). Since the question sums the value added in each sector, it is the output approach: B. The trap: C (income approach) also avoids double counting, but it sums factor incomes, not values added. Memory tip: Value Added = Output method.",
 "|Population density is described as the|average number of people per square kilometre|average number of people in a country|total population excluding migration|total population including net migration": "Population density measures how crowded a country or area is: the number of people living on each unit of land. It is calculated as total population ÷ land area, usually in persons per square kilometre: A. For example, a state of 6 million people on 30,000 km² has a density of 200 people per km². Areas such as Lagos and the South-East have high densities, while much of the North has lower densities. The average number of people in a country (B) is meaningless, and total population with or without migration (C, D) says nothing about land area. Memory tip: density = people ÷ land.",
 "|The short-run inelasticity of supply of agricultural produce causes|stability in prices|a steady rise in prices|a steady decline in prices|instability in prices": "Farm output depends on planting decisions made months earlier and on the weather, so in the short run farmers cannot increase or reduce supply quickly when prices change; supply is inelastic. With inelastic supply, any change in demand, or a good or poor harvest, causes large changes in price. A bumper harvest makes prices crash, and a poor one makes them soar. The result is instability in the prices and incomes of farmers: D. Governments use buffer stocks, guaranteed prices and storage schemes to reduce this instability. Memory tip: inelastic supply + shifting demand or harvest = big price swings.",
 "|The major contribution of the petroleum industry to the Nigerian economy is the|generation of revenue for the oil companies|provision of employment opportunities for communities|generation of revenue for government|promotion of industrial harmony": "Since the 1970s oil boom, petroleum has dominated Nigeria's economy. Through royalties, petroleum profits tax and the sale of the government's share of crude oil, it has provided most of the federal government's revenue and the bulk of Nigeria's foreign exchange earnings: C. The industry is capital-intensive and employs relatively few people (B), and the oil companies' own profits (A) are not its contribution to the national economy. Oil revenue is shared among the three tiers of government through the Federation Account. Today oil is still the largest single source of foreign exchange, though its share of government revenue has fallen. Memory tip: oil money is government money in Nigeria.",
 "|During a conflict between management and workers, the union's bargaining power is based on|work-to-rule|negotiation|picketing|management decision": "Collective bargaining is the process by which a trade union and the employers negotiate wages and conditions of service on behalf of all workers. When a conflict arises, the union's first and main tool is negotiation, backed by the strength of its membership and its ability to speak for all the workers: B. Work-to-rule (A), picketing (C), go-slows and strikes are forms of industrial action used to pressure management when negotiation breaks down. A management decision (D) is the employers' side, not the union's power. Memory tip: unions Negotiate first; they act (strike, picket, work-to-rule) only when talks fail.",
 "|Localisation of industries in Nigeria is mainly influenced by|market size|population groupings|raw materials|external economies": "Localisation of industry means the concentration of firms in one industry in a particular area. In Nigeria the most important influence on where industries settle is nearness to raw materials, especially bulky or perishable ones that are costly to transport: C. Examples are cement factories at Ewekoro, Obajana and Nkalagu (limestone), textile mills in Kaduna and Kano (cotton), and tin smelting on the Jos Plateau. Market size (A) and population (B) matter for some industries, such as bottling. External economies (D), such as a pool of skilled labour and ancillary firms, are benefits that come once an industry is already localised. Memory tip: in Nigeria, factories follow their raw materials.",
 "|If the death rate of a country is 52% and the growth rate is 12%, what is the birth rate of the country?|48%|64%|40%|52%": "The natural growth rate of a population is the birth rate minus the death rate (ignoring migration).\nStep 1: Growth rate = birth rate − death rate.\nStep 2: Rearrange: birth rate = growth rate + death rate.\nStep 3: Birth rate = 12% + 52% = 64%: B.\nThe trap: A (48%) comes from subtracting the growth rate from the death rate (52% − 12%), which would make the population shrink, not grow. Memory tip: Births − Deaths = Growth, so Births = Growth + Deaths.",
 "|Agricultural backward-linkage effect means the establishment of|subsidiary industries to increase the number of industries|main industries to increase output|industries to reduce imports|subsidiary industries to feed the main industry": "Linkages are the ways one industry stimulates others. A backward linkage arises when the growth of a main industry creates demand for its inputs, leading to new firms that supply it. For example, a large brewery or textile mill encourages the growth of farms and processing plants producing sorghum, maize or cotton to feed it. So an agricultural backward linkage means setting up subsidiary industries to supply the main industry: D. A forward linkage works the other way, when an industry's output becomes the input of new firms, such as cassava processed into starch for other factories. Reducing imports (C) is import substitution. Memory tip: Backward = Behind the factory, supplying its inputs.",
 "|The distinction between onshore and offshore operations in oil exploration lies in the|location of sites|output generated|size of production|techniques of production": "Oil exploration and production in Nigeria are divided by location. Onshore operations take place on dry land and in the swamps of the Niger Delta. Offshore operations take place at sea, in shallow or deep water, using platforms and floating production vessels: A. Output, size and techniques (B, C, D) may differ between them, but they are results of the location, not the basis of the distinction. Since the 1990s, Nigeria's deep-offshore fields such as Bonga and Agbami have become very important because they are less exposed to onshore problems like pipeline vandalism. Memory tip: on-shore = on the land; off-shore = off the land, at sea.",
 "|Long-run production is called|diminishing returns to scale|returns to scale|external economies of scale|economies of scale": "In the short run at least one factor, such as the size of the factory, is fixed, and adding more of a variable factor is governed by the law of diminishing returns. In the long run all factors can be changed. Studying how output responds when all inputs are increased in the same proportion is called returns to scale: B. Returns can be increasing (output rises by a larger proportion), constant, or decreasing. Economies of scale (D) refer to the fall in average cost as the scale grows, which is closely related but is a cost idea, not the name of long-run production. Memory tip: short run = returns to a factor; long run = returns to scale.",
 "|For a firm to break even in the long run, the marginal cost curve must cut the|average variable cost curve at its highest point|average cost curve at any two points|total cost curve at its lowest point|average cost curve at its lowest point": "The marginal cost curve always cuts the average cost curve at the lowest point of AC: when MC is below AC, AC falls, and when MC is above AC, AC rises. A firm breaks even, earning only normal profit, when price equals average cost. In the long run, competition pushes price down to the minimum of AC, which is exactly where MC cuts AC: D. MC cuts the average variable cost curve at its lowest point, not its highest (A), and it does not cut AC at two points (B). The total cost curve (C) has no lowest point that MC cuts. Memory tip: MC passes through the bottom of every average curve.",
 "|An increase in the quantity supplied of a commodity suggests|a leftward shift of the supply curve|a rightward shift of the supply curve|a movement along the supply curve|an increase in elasticity of supply": "A change in quantity supplied happens when the good's own price changes and all other things stay the same. The producer simply moves to another point on the same supply curve: a higher price leads to a movement up the curve and a larger quantity supplied: C. A change in supply, caused by costs, technology, weather or taxes, shifts the whole curve (A, B). The trap: B (a rightward shift) also means more is offered for sale, but that is an increase in supply, not in quantity supplied. Memory tip: quantity supplied changes = move along; supply changes = shift.",
 "|An example of a long-run cost of a firm is|fuel and maintenance cost|the planned size of plant and equipment|the existing size of plant and equipment|rent on buildings": "In the short run, a firm's plant and equipment are fixed, so costs such as rent on buildings (D) and the cost of the existing plant (C) are fixed costs, while fuel and maintenance (A) are short-run variable costs. In the long run, all factors are variable: the firm can choose a new size of factory and new machines. The cost of the planned size of plant and equipment is therefore a long-run cost: B. The long-run average cost curve is sometimes called the planning curve for this reason. The trap: C (the existing size of plant) is the fixed factor of the short run, not a long-run cost. Memory tip: long run = planning time, when even the plant can change.",
 "|In a car assembly plant, division of labour is demonstrated by|assigning each worker to a specific activity|assigning each worker a given number to produce|increasing the daily output|producing a specific model": "Division of labour means breaking a production process into many small tasks and giving each worker one task to repeat. In a car assembly plant, one worker fits engines, another fixes doors, another sprays paint and another tests the brakes: A. This raises skill and speed and allows the use of machines, but it can cause boredom and over-dependence. Giving each worker a quota (B) is a production target, raising daily output (C) is a result, and producing a specific model (D) is product specialisation by the firm, not division of labour among workers. Memory tip: division of labour = one worker, one task.",
 "|Under perfect competition, the short-run supply curve of a firm is determined by its|total cost curve|marginal cost curve|average fixed cost curve|average cost curve": "A firm in perfect competition is a price taker and maximises profit where price = marginal cost. At each possible price it produces the output where MC equals that price, so the quantity it supplies at each price is read off its marginal cost curve: B. The firm will not produce at all if price is below average variable cost, so its short-run supply curve is the part of the MC curve above the lowest point of AVC. The average cost curve (D) tells whether the firm makes profit or loss, not how much it supplies. Memory tip: under perfect competition, the MC curve above AVC is the supply curve.",
 "|The hoarding of goods is usually experienced when|supply is greater than demand|the market price is above the equilibrium price|demand is greater than supply|excess demand is greater than excess supply": "Hoarding means keeping goods off the market, usually to sell later at higher prices. It happens when goods are scarce, that is, when demand is greater than supply at the current price: C. Traders expect prices to keep rising, so they hold stocks back, which makes the shortage worse. Examples in Nigeria include fuel scarcity and hoarding of grains before the lean season. When supply exceeds demand (A) or price is above equilibrium (B), there is a surplus, and sellers want to sell, not hoard. Memory tip: shortage breeds hoarding; surplus breeds sales.",
 "|The technical relationship between input combination and maximum attainable output is called|a production function|an indifference curve|an isocost|an isoquant": "A production function is the technical (engineering) relationship between the quantities of inputs used and the maximum output that can be obtained from them, written Q = f(L, K, …): A. It tells the producer how much output any combination of labour and capital can yield with the existing technology. An isoquant (D) is one curve drawn from the production function, joining combinations that give the same output. An isocost (C) shows combinations that cost the same, and an indifference curve (B) is about consumer satisfaction. The trap: D (isoquant) is closely related, but it shows only one level of output, not the whole relationship. Memory tip: function = the whole input-to-output rule.",
 "|If the marginal utility of the last unit of commodity X at ₦2 is 16 and that of commodity Y at ₦4 is 24, the consumer will be at equilibrium when|the price-quantity ratios are equal|equal amounts of X and Y are consumed|less of Y and more of X are consumed|less of X and more of Y are consumed": "A consumer with two goods is in equilibrium when the last naira spent on each gives the same marginal utility: MUx/Px = MUy/Py.\nStep 1: MU per naira of X = 16 ÷ ₦2 = 8.\nStep 2: MU per naira of Y = 24 ÷ ₦4 = 6.\nStep 3: X gives more satisfaction per naira (8 > 6), so the consumer should buy more X, whose MU then falls, and less Y, whose MU then rises, until the ratios are equal: C.\nThe trap: D (less X, more Y) comes from comparing the raw utilities, 24 against 16, without dividing by price. Memory tip: always compare MU per naira, not MU alone.",
 "|A buyer who haggles in the market is applying the principle of|choice|price mechanism|opportunity cost|utility maximisation": "Haggling is bargaining over price, common in Nigerian open markets. A rational buyer has limited money and wants to get the greatest possible satisfaction from it. By pushing the price down, he can buy the item for less and have money left for other things, so he gets more total satisfaction from his income. This is the principle of utility maximisation: D. Haggling does take place in the market where prices are formed, but the question asks what principle the buyer is applying, which is to get the most satisfaction for his money. Memory tip: every naira saved by haggling buys extra satisfaction elsewhere.",
 "|An inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded implies that|the two variables change in opposite directions|the two variables change in the same direction|only one variable changes|the two variables remain unchanged": "Two variables have an inverse (negative) relationship when one rises as the other falls. The law of demand is the best-known example: when price rises, quantity demanded falls, and when price falls, quantity demanded rises, so the demand curve slopes downwards from left to right: A. A direct (positive) relationship, where both move in the same direction (B), is found between price and quantity supplied. Memory tip: inverse = opposite; direct = same direction.",
 "|When the marginal utility of a commodity is zero, the total utility is|at its minimum|upward-sloping|downward-sloping|at its maximum": "Total utility (TU) is the total satisfaction from all units consumed; marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. As consumption rises, MU falls (diminishing marginal utility). While MU is positive, each extra unit adds to TU, so TU rises. When MU reaches zero, the extra unit adds nothing, so TU stops rising and is at its peak. If consumption continues, MU becomes negative and TU falls. So when MU is zero, total utility is at its maximum: D. The trap: A (minimum) catches students who think zero marginal utility means zero satisfaction overall. Memory tip: MU = 0 at the top of the TU hill.",
 "|Economic freedom is a basic feature of|a market economy|an industrialised economy|a planned economy|a developing economy": "In a market (capitalist, free-enterprise) economy, the means of production are privately owned and decisions are made by individuals through the price mechanism. People are free to choose their occupation, to start and run businesses, to own property and to spend their income as they wish. This economic freedom, with consumer sovereignty and the profit motive, is its basic feature: A. In a planned economy (C), the state decides what to produce, so individual freedom is limited. Being industrialised or developing (B, D) describes the level of development, not the economic system. Memory tip: Market = freedom; Planned = state command.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (kg)\n40 ... 6\n48 ... 5\n55 ... 4\n60 ... 3\n70 ... 2\n\nWhat is the median quantity?|5kg|6kg|3kg|4kg": "The median is the middle value of a set of data arranged in order.\nStep 1: The quantities are 6, 5, 4, 3, 2. In ascending order: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\nStep 2: There are 5 values, so the median is the (5 + 1) ÷ 2 = 3rd value.\nStep 3: The 3rd value is 4kg: D.\nThe median is not affected by the prices beside them; only the quantity column is used. Memory tip: arrange first, then pick the middle.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nPrice (₦) ... Quantity (kg)\n40 ... 6\n48 ... 5\n55 ... 4\n60 ... 3\n70 ... 2\n\nFind the mean price of the distribution.|₦54|₦51|₦56|₦59": "The mean price of the distribution weights each price by the quantity bought at that price.\nStep 1: Multiply each price by its quantity: 40 × 6 = 240; 48 × 5 = 240; 55 × 4 = 220; 60 × 3 = 180; 70 × 2 = 140.\nStep 2: Σ(price × quantity) = 240 + 240 + 220 + 180 + 140 = 1,020.\nStep 3: Σquantity = 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 20.\nStep 4: Mean price = 1,020 ÷ 20 = ₦51: B.\nThe trap: A (₦54) is close to the simple average of the five prices, 273 ÷ 5 = ₦54.60, which ignores the quantities. Memory tip: for a distribution, mean = Σfx ÷ Σf.",
 "|If elasticity of demand is greater than 1 and less than infinity, demand is said to be|elastic|perfectly inelastic|inelastic|perfectly elastic": "Price elasticity of demand compares the percentage change in quantity demanded with the percentage change in price. When it is greater than 1, quantity changes by a larger percentage than price, and demand is elastic: A. When it equals infinity, buyers will buy any amount at one price but nothing above it: perfectly elastic (D), a horizontal demand curve. Between 0 and 1 demand is inelastic (C), and at exactly 0 it is perfectly inelastic (B). The trap: D (perfectly elastic) is the limit the question excludes by saying less than infinity. Memory tip: 0 perfectly inelastic, below 1 inelastic, 1 unitary, above 1 elastic, infinity perfectly elastic.",
 "|In economics, production takes place only when|producers sell to retailers|value has been added to a commodity|modern equipment is used|consumption has taken place": "In economics, production is the creation of utility, or the adding of value, to satisfy human wants; it is not only making things in a factory. Utility is added by changing a good's form, moving it to where it is needed, storing it until it is needed, and the services of traders, transporters and bankers. Production is therefore said to be complete only when the goods reach the final consumer, who consumes them: D. Because production is defined as creating utility, value has been added to a commodity (B) is also accepted. Selling to retailers (A) is only a stage, and modern equipment (C) is not needed. Memory tip: production ends at the consumer.",
 "|The monetization policy of the Nigerian government is aimed at|encouraging public servants to retire early and become self-employed|transferring government properties to retired public servants|reducing government's burden on the provision of fringe benefits to public servants|helping government recover properties held by public servants": "In 2003 the Federal Government introduced the monetisation of fringe benefits for public servants. Instead of providing official houses, cars, drivers, furniture, utilities and domestic staff, government pays officers cash allowances and lets them make their own arrangements. The aim was to cut the huge cost, waste and abuse involved in providing these benefits in kind, so it reduced government's burden: C. As part of the policy, many government houses in Abuja were later sold, often to the officers living in them, but that was a means, not the aim (B). Memory tip: monetise = turn benefits in kind into money.",
 "|The price index computed between two time periods is given as 128%. This implies that the|disposable incomes have increased by 28%|living standards have fallen by 128%|living standards have risen by 128%|living standards have fallen by 28%": "A price index compares prices in a current period with a base period, which is set at 100. An index of 128 means the general price level is 28% higher than in the base period. If money incomes do not change, the same income buys less, so living standards fall, which is the intended answer: D. Strictly, purchasing power falls to 100 ÷ 128 = 78.1% of its former level, a fall of about 22%, not 28%, and if incomes have also risen, living standards may not have fallen at all, so the option is not fully correct. Options B and C wrongly read 128 itself as the change. Memory tip: index − 100 = percentage change in prices.",
 "|The problem of planning in Nigeria is mainly in the|implementation of the plans|appraisal of the plans|allocation of scarce resources|articulation of the plans": "Nigeria has had several development plans: the First National Development Plan (1962–68), Second (1970–74), Third (1975–80) and Fourth (1981–85), then rolling plans and visions. The plans were generally well articulated on paper, but they failed mainly at the implementation stage: A. Causes included corruption, political instability, lack of reliable data, shortage of skilled manpower, poor monitoring, and over-dependence on oil revenue and foreign aid. Appraisal (B) and articulation (D) come before or after, and allocating scarce resources (C) is what planning itself tries to do. Memory tip: Nigerian plans were good on paper but weak in practice.",
 "|The raising of funds by selling stocks to the public is called|equity financing|deficit financing|loan financing|debt financing": "A company can raise long-term funds in two main ways. Equity financing is the sale of shares (stocks) to the public; the buyers become part-owners of the company and receive dividends, and the money need not be repaid: A. Debt financing (D) and loan financing (C) mean borrowing, such as bank loans or debentures, which must be repaid with interest. Deficit financing (B) is a government spending more than its revenue and borrowing to cover the gap. The trap: D (debt financing) is the other main method, often confused with equity, but buying stock gives ownership, not a loan. Memory tip: Equity = ownership; Debt = must be repaid.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nCountry ... Groundnuts (units) ... Cocoa (units)\nNigeria ... 8000 ... 1,000\nGhana ... 1000 ... 8000\nTotal production ... 9000 ... 9000\n\nApplying the law of comparative costs, how many units of groundnuts will Nigeria produce?|7000 units|16000 units|9000 units|8000 units": "Comparative cost: Nigeria is relatively better at groundnuts (8000 against Ghana's 1000), and Ghana at cocoa (8000 against Nigeria's 1000). In this kind of table, each country's figures are taken as the output of half its resources.\nStep 1: Nigeria devotes half its resources to groundnuts and gets 8000 units.\nStep 2: When it specialises, it moves the other half into groundnuts as well, giving 8000 + 8000 = 16000 units: B.\nGhana likewise produces 16000 units of cocoa. The trap: C (9000 units) is the world's total groundnut output before specialisation. Memory tip: specialise in what you do relatively best.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nCountry ... Groundnuts (units) ... Cocoa (units)\nNigeria ... 8000 ... 1,000\nGhana ... 1000 ... 8000\nTotal production ... 9000 ... 9000\n\nThe difference in each country's total output on specialization is|16000 units|9000 units|8000 units|7000 units": "Step 1: Before specialisation, each country produces 8000 units of one good and 1000 units of the other, so each country's total output is 9000 units.\nStep 2: After specialisation (with the same resources, as in the previous question), Nigeria produces 16000 units of groundnuts and Ghana 16000 units of cocoa.\nStep 3: The difference in each country's total output = 16000 − 9000 = 7000 units: D.\nThe world as a whole gains 7000 groundnuts and 7000 cocoa. The trap: A (16000 units) is the new output itself, not the difference. Memory tip: gain from trade = output after specialising − output before.",
 "|The loans from the World Bank to developing countries are mainly to support|capital-intensive technology|the establishment of universities|their infrastructural investment|their level of consumption": "The World Bank (the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development, set up in 1944, with the International Development Association) lends to developing countries for long-term development projects. Most of its lending has gone into infrastructure such as roads, power, water supply, irrigation and telecommunications, which are needed for growth but too costly for poor countries to finance alone: C. It does not lend to finance day-to-day consumption (D), and support for universities (B) is only a small part of its education lending. Today it also funds health, education and social programmes. Memory tip: World Bank = long-term development projects; IMF = short-term balance of payments support.",
 "|Economic growth is different from economic development because economic growth|describes expansion and change|is measurable but not objective|describes expansion and not change|is not measurable but objective": "Economic growth is a sustained rise in a country's real output, usually measured by real GDP or GDP per head. It is a quantitative idea: an expansion of output, without any necessary change in the structure of the economy or in people's welfare. Economic development is wider: it combines growth with change, such as less poverty and inequality, better health and education, and a shift from agriculture to industry and services. So growth describes expansion and not change: C. Growth is easier to measure than development, but both are studied objectively, which rules out B and D. Memory tip: growth is MORE output; development is BETTER lives.",
 "|A long-term capital outflow is recorded in the balance of payments as a|credit on the capital account|credit on the current account|debit on the current account|debit on the capital account": "In the balance of payments, transactions that bring money into the country are credits (+) and those that send money out are debits (−). The current account records trade in goods and services, income and transfers; the capital (and financial) account records investment and lending. A long-term capital outflow, such as a Nigerian firm investing in a factory abroad or lending to a foreign government, is a capital transaction and sends money out, so it is a debit on the capital account: D. The trap: A (credit on the capital account) is right for a capital inflow, such as foreign direct investment into Nigeria. Memory tip: money out = debit; money in = credit.",
 "|Business organizations in which the owner's personal property may be forfeited in the event of bankruptcy are|partnerships and limited liability companies|partnerships and sole proprietorships|cooperatives and Limited Liability Companies|Corporations and sole proprietorships": "Liability is the extent to which owners must pay a business's debts. Sole proprietors and partners in an ordinary partnership have unlimited liability: if the business fails, creditors can take their personal property, such as houses and cars, to recover debts: B. Shareholders of limited liability companies and corporations have limited liability, so they can lose only the money they invested; options A, C and D each include such a body. Cooperatives in Nigeria are registered with limited liability. Memory tip: one owner or partners = unlimited liability; company = limited liability.",
 "|The factor responsible for the current inflationary pressures in Nigeria is the|exchange rate depreciation|increase in exports|low per capita income|budget surplus": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. In the early 2000s, Nigeria imported much of its food, fuel, machinery and raw materials, so the steady depreciation of the naira raised the naira cost of imports and pushed up prices at home: cost-push or imported inflation: A. Rising exports (B) bring in foreign exchange and support the naira; low income per head (C) is a feature of poverty, not a cause of rising prices; and a budget surplus (D) reduces spending, which lowers inflation. The paper refers to 2005, but naira depreciation, especially after the 2023 move to a unified exchange rate, remains a major driver of inflation today. Memory tip: weak naira, dearer imports, higher prices.",
 "|If government increases its expenditure on public works, the desired effect is|a reduction in the use of capital equipment|an increase in the level of employment|an increase in the level of inflation|an increase in the use of capital equipment": "Government spending on public works, such as roads, bridges, schools and housing, is a tool of expansionary fiscal policy. It directly employs workers and contractors, and their spending of the extra income raises demand for other goods, creating still more jobs through the multiplier effect. The main desired effect is therefore an increase in employment: B. Inflation (C) is a possible side effect if the economy is near full employment, not the aim. The use of capital equipment (A, D) may change, but that is not the purpose of the policy. Keynes recommended public works to fight unemployment in a depression. Memory tip: public works put people to work.",
 "|An institution that regulates trade relations among members is the|ADB|OPEC|EU|IMF": "The European Union, which grew out of the European Economic Community set up in 1957, is a customs union and single market. It removes tariffs and quotas on trade among members, applies a common external tariff to outsiders, and negotiates trade agreements for all its members, so it regulates trade relations among them: C. OPEC (B) coordinates its members' oil production and pricing policies through output quotas, but it does not govern trade between the member countries. The IMF (D) deals with exchange rates and balance of payments problems, and the ADB (A) lends for development in Africa. Some readers argue for OPEC because its quotas shape members' oil exports, so the item is debatable, but the EU fits the question better. Memory tip: EU = trade union of nations; OPEC = oil cartel.",
 "|The oil-producing areas in Nigeria are agitating for special compensation owing mainly to|the high level of poverty|constant flooding|environmental pollution|the high level of unemployment": "Crude oil has been produced in the Niger Delta since 1958. Oil spills from pipelines, gas flaring and waste dumping have poisoned rivers, creeks and farmland, killing fish and crops, the main livelihoods of the people. Communities such as Ogoni have therefore demanded special compensation, more revenue (the derivation principle) and clean-up of their land. Poverty and unemployment (A, D) are real in the region, but they are largely consequences of the environmental damage; the main ground for compensation is environmental pollution: C. Bodies such as the Niger Delta Development Commission (2000) and HYPREP for the Ogoni clean-up were set up in response. Memory tip: the oil came out, the pollution stayed in, so compensation is for the damage.",
 "|Minimum wage legislation will result in|reduced income|reduced labour productivity|greater demand for labour|greater supply of labour": "A minimum wage is a legal floor below which employers may not pay workers. To have any effect it is fixed above the free-market wage. At that higher wage more people are willing to offer their labour (the quantity of labour supplied rises), while employers, facing higher costs, hire fewer workers (the quantity demanded falls). The gap is unemployment. So the law brings forth a greater supply of labour: D. It does not raise the demand for labour (C), which falls, and it raises rather than reduces the income of those who keep their jobs (A). The trap: C sounds right because workers gain, but a higher wage makes employers demand less labour, not more. Memory tip: price floor on labour = more sellers of labour, fewer buyers.",
 "|If children and the aged dominate the population of a country, this results in|an increase in the national income|a reduction in the importation of capital goods|an improvement in the standard of living|a fall in the standard of living": "A population dominated by children (under 15) and the aged (over 64) has a high dependency ratio: many dependants who consume but produce little, supported by a small working population. Income per head falls, and government must spend heavily on schools, health care and pensions instead of productive investment. The result is a fall in the standard of living: D. National income does not rise (A), because fewer people are working, and living standards do not improve (C). Option B has no direct link with the age structure of the population. Nigeria, with about 43% of its people under 15, faces this burden. Memory tip: more mouths, fewer hands = lower living standards.",
 "|The term 'oil shock' is used to describe a situation in which|oil prices have fallen drastically|the demand for oil is equal to its supply|the demand for oil exceeds its supply|the supply of oil exceeds its demand": "An oil shock is a sudden, drastic change in the price of crude oil that disturbs economies. For oil-importing countries the famous shocks were the price jumps of 1973 and 1979. For an oil-exporting country like Nigeria, whose government revenue and foreign exchange depend on oil, the shock that hit hardest was the collapse of oil prices in the early 1980s (the oil glut), which led to austerity and SAP in 1986; prices crashed again in 2014–2016 and 2020. Here the answer is that oil prices have fallen drastically: A. The term can also describe a sudden price rise, so the item is debatable, but none of the other options describes a shock at all. Memory tip: shock = sudden swing in oil price; for Nigeria, think price crash.",
 "|A major factor militating against rapid industrialization in Nigeria is|poor market facilities|poor transport facilities|epileptic power supply|inadequate skilled manpower": "Industries in Nigeria face many obstacles: inadequate capital, shortage of skilled manpower, poor roads and small markets. The one most often cited is epileptic (irregular, unreliable) power supply. Without steady electricity, factories must buy diesel or gas for their own generators, which sharply raises costs; many firms have closed or moved to neighbouring countries for this reason. So the major factor is C. Poor transport (B) and inadequate skilled manpower (D) are real problems but come after power in most surveys. Even after the privatisation of PHCN's companies in 2013 into GenCos and DisCos, power supply remains a key constraint today. Memory tip: no light, no factory.",
 "|The marginal productivity theory applies in a|unionised labour market|monopolistic market only|monopolistic super-market only|perfectly competitive market": "The marginal productivity theory of distribution says that a factor of production is paid the value of its marginal product: a firm hires workers until the wage equals the value of the extra output the last worker adds. The theory rests on the assumptions of perfect competition: many firms and workers, homogeneous labour, perfect knowledge, perfect mobility, and a given market price for the product. So it applies in a perfectly competitive market: D. In unionised markets (A) wages are set by bargaining, and under monopoly (B, C) the firm does not take price as given, so the simple theory does not hold. Memory tip: MP theory needs Perfect competition.",
 "|The major problem of conducting a census in Nigeria is|lack of political will|inadequate skilled personnel|urban congestion|financial constraint": "A census is a complete count of the people of a country at a given time. Nigeria's censuses (1962, 1963, 1973, 1991, 2006) have been controversial because population figures decide the sharing of federal revenue, the number of seats in the National Assembly and the creation of states and local governments. Groups therefore inflate their numbers, and governments have often been unwilling to conduct and accept an honest count; the 1962 and 1973 results were cancelled. The major problem is lack of political will: A. Shortage of skilled personnel (B) and funds (D) are also listed as problems. Memory tip: in Nigeria, headcount = headship, so politics spoils the count.",
 "|A major advantage of industrialisation is that it|curbs inflation|leads to growth and development|leads to self-reliance|improves the terms of trade": "Industrialisation is the setting up and expansion of manufacturing industries. It raises national output, creates jobs, adds value to raw materials before export, encourages new skills and technology, and widens the tax base. Together these bring economic growth (more output) and development (better living standards): B. Self-reliance (C) is one possible benefit, but it is part of the wider growth and development that industry brings. Industrialisation does not by itself curb inflation (A) or guarantee better terms of trade (D). Memory tip: factories turn raw materials into growth.",
 "|Mobility of labour is higher when there|are alternative jobs in the same location|is a monopoly of jobs in one location|is intense competition for jobs in the same location|are no alternative jobs in the same location": "Mobility of labour is the ease with which workers move from one job (occupational mobility) or place (geographical mobility) to another. Moving to another town is costly: transport, accommodation, leaving family and adjusting to a new culture. Workers therefore change jobs most easily when other jobs are available where they already live. So mobility is higher when there are alternative jobs in the same location: A. A monopoly of jobs (B) or no alternatives (D) tie workers down, and intense competition for jobs (C) makes it hard to find a new one. Memory tip: jobs next door make moving easy.",
 "|The organization whose aim is to solve the trade problems of less developed nations is|UNECA|UNCTAD|WTO|OECD": "UNCTAD, the United Nations Conference on Trade and Development, was set up in 1964 in Geneva to help developing countries trade on fairer terms. It pushed for better prices for primary products, preferential access for their manufactures to rich markets, and more aid: B. UNECA (A) is the UN Economic Commission for Africa in Addis Ababa, which deals with Africa's general economic development. The WTO (C), which replaced GATT in 1995, regulates world trade for all members. The OECD (D) is a club of rich industrial countries. The trap: C, the WTO, also deals with trade, but its role is rules for all countries, not the problems of poor ones. Memory tip: UNCTAD = UN Cares about Trade And Development.",
 "|Nigeria's exports usually comprise|consumer goods|primary goods|capital goods|intermediate goods": "Primary goods are products of the extractive industries: farming, fishing, forestry and mining. Nigeria's exports have always been mainly primary: cocoa, groundnuts, palm produce and cotton before the 1970s, and crude oil and gas since then, which make up about 80–90% of export earnings. So Nigeria's exports usually comprise primary goods: B. Nigeria imports most of its capital goods (C) such as machinery and many consumer goods (A). This heavy dependence on a single primary product exposes the economy to swings in world oil prices. Memory tip: Nigeria sells what comes from the ground and buys what comes from the factory.",
 "|An association formed by a group of individuals solely for the marketing of their product is a|voluntary organization|consumer cooperative society|credit and thrift society|producers cooperative society": "A cooperative society is a voluntary association of people who pool resources for their common benefit, run on the principle of one member, one vote. A producers' cooperative is formed by producers, often farmers, to process and market their output together, so they get better prices and avoid exploitation by middlemen: D. A consumer cooperative (B) is formed by buyers to purchase goods in bulk and sell to members cheaply. A credit and thrift society (C) collects members' savings and gives them loans. \"Voluntary organization\" (A) is too general. Examples: cocoa farmers' marketing cooperatives in Ondo State. Memory tip: producers sell together; consumers buy together; thrift saves together.",
 "|Economic growth can be accelerated through|increased current investment|excess consumption over investment|excess current consumption|increased current consumption": "Economic growth is a sustained rise in a country's real output (real GDP). Output grows when the economy's capacity grows, and capacity grows through investment: spending on machines, factories, roads, power stations and education. Money spent on current consumption is used up and does not add to future productive capacity. So growth is accelerated through increased current investment: A. Options B, C and D all favour consumption over investment, which slows the build-up of capital. To invest more, a country must save more, which is why savings are encouraged. Memory tip: consume today, enjoy today; invest today, grow tomorrow.",
 "|A characteristic common to partnership and sole proprietorship is|unlimited liability|limited liability|accessibility to loans|transferability of shares": "A sole proprietorship is owned by one person; an ordinary partnership by 2 to 20 people. In both, the business has no separate legal existence from its owners, so if the business cannot pay its debts, creditors can take the owners' personal property. This is unlimited liability: A. Limited liability (B) and transferable shares (D) belong to joint stock companies. Both small forms of business actually find it hard to get loans (C). A limited partner in a limited partnership is the only exception, and at least one partner must still have unlimited liability. Memory tip: small business, unlimited risk.",
 "|Import substitution policy is used to|check government expenditure|correct the balance of payments problem|protect local industries|encourage increased volume of trade": "Import substitution industrialisation means producing locally the goods a country used to import, such as textiles, beverages, cement and assembled vehicles. Its main purpose is to reduce the import bill and save scarce foreign exchange, so as to correct a balance of payments problem: B. To make the new local industries survive, governments protect them with tariffs and import bans, so protection of local industries (C) is the means used to make import substitution work rather than the purpose of the policy. Nigeria followed this strategy in its first and second national development plans (1962–1974). The trap: C is closely tied to import substitution, but it is the tool, while saving foreign exchange is the aim. Memory tip: substitute imports to save forex.",
 "|The exchange rate determined by market forces is known as|fixed exchange rate|floating exchange rate|pegged exchange rate|dual exchange rate": "The exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. Under a floating (flexible) exchange rate, the rate is determined by the demand for and supply of the currency in the foreign exchange market, without government fixing it: B. A fixed or pegged rate (A, C) is set by the government or central bank and defended at that level. A dual exchange rate (D) means two official rates for different transactions, as Nigeria had in the 1980s and 1990s. In June 2023 Nigeria unified its exchange rate windows and moved towards a market-determined rate for the naira. Memory tip: floating rate floats with the market tide.",
 "|A constraint on the expansion of a firm is the|rate of advertisement|level of the producers' income|tastes of the consumers|size of the market": "A firm expands to enjoy economies of scale, but it can only grow as large as the market for its product allows. If the market is small, because of low incomes, small population or poor transport, extra output cannot be sold, so the size of the market limits expansion: D. Adam Smith made the same point when he said that division of labour is limited by the extent of the market. Advertisement (A) and consumers' tastes (C) affect demand but are not the basic constraint, and the producer's own income (B) is not what limits the firm. Other constraints include finance and managerial ability. Memory tip: you cannot grow bigger than your market.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNumber of men ... Total product (units)\n1 ... 200\n2 ... 440\n3 ... 540\n4 ... 600\n5 ... 640\n6 ... 660\n7 ... 660\n8 ... 640\n\nIf the price per unit is ₦2, what is the average revenue when 6 men are employed?|₦220|₦120|₦110|₦330": "Read the table: 6 men produce a total product of 660 units. Here \"average revenue\" means the revenue per man employed (the average revenue product of labour).\nStep 1: Total revenue = total product × price = 660 × ₦2 = ₦1,320.\nStep 2: Revenue per man = ₦1,320 ÷ 6 = ₦220: A.\nStrictly, average revenue per unit of output is simply the price, ₦2, which is not an option, so the question is asking for revenue per worker. The trap: C (₦110) is the average product per man (660 ÷ 6) without multiplying by the price of ₦2. Memory tip: product × price = revenue; divide by men for revenue per man.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNumber of men ... Total product (units)\n1 ... 200\n2 ... 440\n3 ... 540\n4 ... 600\n5 ... 640\n6 ... 660\n7 ... 660\n8 ... 640\n\nWhat is the average product when 3 men are employed?|120 units|540 units|100 units|180 units": "Average product (AP) is the output per unit of the variable factor: AP = total product ÷ number of workers.\nStep 1: From the table, 3 men produce a total product of 540 units.\nStep 2: AP = 540 ÷ 3 = 180 units: D.\nThe trap: B (540 units) is the total product, not the average, and C (100 units) is the marginal product of the third man (540 − 440). Marginal product is the extra output from one more worker; average product is the output per worker. Memory tip: Average = total shared out; Marginal = the extra one adds.",
 "|A rising short-run average cost is a result of|falling marginal costs|diminishing returns|economies of scale|rising fixed costs": "In the short run some factors, such as the factory building and machines, are fixed. As a firm adds more variable factors such as labour to the fixed factors, output first rises fast, but beyond a point each extra worker adds less and less: this is the law of diminishing returns (variable proportions). When returns diminish, the cost of each extra unit rises, and average cost eventually rises too: B. Falling marginal costs (A) pull average cost down, economies of scale (C) are a long-run idea that lower costs, and fixed costs (D) do not change in the short run by definition. Memory tip: diminishing returns make the short-run AC curve turn up into a U.",
 "|The market structure in which there is interdependence of price-output policies is|an oligopoly|a pure monopoly|a pure competition|a monopolistic competition": "An oligopoly is a market dominated by a few large sellers, such as the major telecom operators (MTN, Airtel, Glo) or cement makers in Nigeria. Because each firm has a large share of the market, any price cut or new product by one firm affects the others, who will react. Each firm must therefore guess its rivals' responses before deciding price and output: this is interdependence, the hallmark of oligopoly: A. A pure monopolist (B) has no rivals, and firms in pure (C) or monopolistic (D) competition are too small to affect each other much. The kinked demand curve and price wars come from this interdependence. Memory tip: Oligopoly = a few firms watching One another.",
 "|The diagram above shows the effect of|excess demand over supply at q₃|excess supply over demand at q₂|minimum price legislation at P₂|maximum price legislation at P₂": "Look at the diagram. Demand DD and supply SS cross at the equilibrium price P₁ and quantity q₁. The price P₂ is fixed below equilibrium. A legal price set below the equilibrium price is a maximum (ceiling) price: sellers may not charge more than P₂. At P₂ suppliers offer only q₂ while buyers want q₃, so there is excess demand of q₂q₃, leading to shortages, queues, rationing and black markets. So the diagram shows maximum price legislation at P₂: D. A minimum price (C) would be set above P₁. The trap: A also mentions excess demand, but the shortage is the gap between q₂ and q₃, not something \"at q₃\". Memory tip: a maximum price is a ceiling held below equilibrium.",
 "|If the short-run cost curve of a firm is U-shaped, the marginal and average costs are equal where the|average fixed cost is falling|marginal cost is falling|average cost is minimum|average cost is maximum": "The short-run average cost (AC) curve is U-shaped: it falls at first, reaches a minimum, then rises because of diminishing returns. Marginal cost (MC) is the cost of one extra unit. When MC is below AC, it pulls the average down; when MC is above AC, it pushes the average up. So MC must cut AC exactly at its lowest point: C. Think of class scores: if a new score is below the class average, the average falls; if above, it rises. AC has no maximum point on a U-shaped curve (D), and MC equals AC when MC is rising, not falling (B). Memory tip: MC cuts AC at the bottom of the U.",
 "|Product differentiation in monopolistic competition implies that|sellers determine whom to sell their products to|different buyers pay different prices for the same product|the same product is available in different forms|different products are sold to the same buyer": "Monopolistic competition has many sellers selling similar but not identical products. Each firm makes its product look different through brand names, packaging, colour, quality or after-sales service: this is product differentiation. Examples are the many brands of detergent, bottled water, noodles or toothpaste in a Nigerian supermarket: basically the same product in different forms: C. Charging different buyers different prices for the same product (B) is price discrimination, a different idea. A and D do not describe differentiation. Differentiation gives each firm a small monopoly over its own brand, so its demand curve slopes downwards. Memory tip: same soap, different wrappers.",
 "|The only factor of production that plays an active role in the production process is|land|entrepreneur|labour|capital": "Production needs four factors: land, labour, capital and the entrepreneur. Land (natural resources) and capital (man-made aids such as machines) are passive factors: they produce nothing on their own until human effort is applied to them. Labour, the human physical and mental effort used in production, is the active factor that works on land and capital to produce goods: C. The entrepreneur organises the other factors and bears risk, but in many textbooks the entrepreneur is treated as a special kind of labour. The trap: B (entrepreneur) is tempting because the entrepreneur is also human and directs production, but the factor described as the active one is labour. Memory tip: land and capital wait; labour works.",
 "|A perfectly competitive firm is advised to close down when the|price is equal to the marginal revenue|price is below the average variable cost|marginal revenue is equal to the marginal cost|price is below the marginal cost": "In the short run a firm faces fixed costs, which it must pay even if it produces nothing, and variable costs, which it pays only if it produces. As long as the price covers average variable cost (AVC), the firm should keep producing, because its revenue pays all variable costs and something towards fixed costs, so its loss is smaller than if it shut down. If the price falls below AVC, every unit adds to the loss, so it should close down: B. Price = MR (A) is always true in perfect competition, and MR = MC (C) is the profit-maximising rule. The trap: D (price below marginal cost) only tells the firm to produce less. Memory tip: can't cover variable costs? Shut the door.",
 "|When the federal government guarantees a loan for a state government, such a loan constitutes|a transferred debt|a private debt|a public debt|an inter-governmental debt": "Public debt is the debt owed by government and its agencies, at any level (federal, state or local), to lenders at home or abroad. When a state government borrows, for example from a commercial bank or the World Bank, and the federal government guarantees the loan (agrees to pay if the state defaults), the loan is still a debt of the public sector: public debt, C. Private debt (B) is owed by individuals and firms. An inter-governmental debt (D) would be a loan from one government to another, while here the federal government only guarantees it. Nigeria's Debt Management Office (DMO), set up in 2000, records such debts. Memory tip: if government owes it, it is public debt.",
 "|If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.6 and consumption expenditure changes by ₦10 million, the equilibrium level of income will change by|₦4.0 million|₦2.5 million|₦15.0 million|₦25.0 million": "The investment or expenditure multiplier shows how much national income changes when spending changes.\nStep 1: Multiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.6) = 1 ÷ 0.4 = 2.5.\nStep 2: Change in income = k × change in spending = 2.5 × ₦10 million = ₦25 million: D.\nThe trap: B (₦2.5 million) is the multiplier itself mistaken for the answer, and A (₦4.0 million) comes from multiplying ₦10 million by the MPS, 0.4. Each round of spending becomes someone's income, who spends 60% of it, and so on. Memory tip: k = 1 ÷ MPS; bigger MPC, bigger multiplier.",
 "|The price index is calculated as|(Current price ÷ Base year price) × 100|(Base year price ÷ Current price) × 100|(Weighted price ÷ Current price) × 100|(Current price ÷ Weighted price) × 100": "A price index measures how prices have changed relative to a base year, whose index is set at 100.\nSimple price index = (current price ÷ base-year price) × 100: A.\nExample: a tin of milk cost ₦200 in the base year and ₦300 now. Index = (300 ÷ 200) × 100 = 150, meaning prices have risen by 50%.\nThe trap: B turns the fraction upside down, which would give 66.7 and wrongly show a fall in price. C and D bring in \"weighted price\", which is used in weighted indices such as the Consumer Price Index, but not in this way. Memory tip: Current over Base, times 100: C over B.",
 "|Personal income tax as a source of government revenue is increased when the|tax base is contracted|tax system is proportional|tax rate is raised|retirement age is reduced": "Revenue from any tax equals the tax rate multiplied by the tax base (the total income or value that is taxed). Personal income tax revenue therefore rises if the tax rate is raised, provided people do not stop working or evade the tax: C. A contracted tax base (A) means fewer incomes to tax, so revenue falls. A proportional system (B) says nothing about whether the amount collected rises. Reducing the retirement age (D) takes people out of employment sooner and shrinks the base. Revenue can also be raised by widening the base and improving collection. Memory tip: revenue = rate × base; raise either one.",
 "|If the Central Bank of Nigeria reduces the bank rate, this will cause|commercial banks to merge|commercial banks to reduce lending|money supply to reduce|money supply to increase": "The bank rate (minimum rediscount rate, now called the Monetary Policy Rate, MPR) is the rate at which the Central Bank lends to commercial banks. When the CBN reduces it, borrowing from the CBN becomes cheaper, so banks lower their own lending rates and lend more to the public. More bank lending creates more deposits, so the money supply increases: D. Lowering the rate encourages, not reduces, lending (B), and it has nothing to do with mergers (A). The trap: C is the effect of raising the bank rate, which is used to fight inflation. Memory tip: rate down, money up; rate up, money down.",
 "|The main source of government revenue in Nigeria is|import duties|capital gains tax|company income tax|rents and royalties": "Before the 1970s import and export duties were the main source of government revenue in Nigeria. Since the oil boom of the 1970s, the oil sector has provided most of the federal government's revenue through royalties and rents paid by oil companies for the right to extract crude oil, petroleum profits tax and sales of crude oil. In 2006 oil revenue ran into trillions of naira, while customs duties brought in only a few hundred billion. So the main source is rents and royalties: D. Company income tax (C) and capital gains tax (B) are much smaller. Today non-oil taxes such as VAT are growing, but oil remains central. Memory tip: in Nigeria, the money comes from the oil wells.",
 "|One way of controlling deflation is by|decreasing wages|rigid fiscal policy|contractionary monetary policy|deficit financing": "Deflation is a persistent fall in the general price level, usually caused by too little total spending, so firms cut output and jobs. To control it, government must increase aggregate demand: expansionary fiscal and monetary policy. Deficit financing means government deliberately spends more than its revenue, financing the gap by borrowing, which injects money and demand into the economy: D. Decreasing wages (A), rigid (tight) fiscal policy (B) and contractionary monetary policy (C) all reduce spending and would make deflation worse; they are cures for inflation. Memory tip: deflation needs a Deficit; inflation needs a squeeze.",
 "|The Revenue Mobilization, Allocation and Fiscal Commission in Nigeria has the primary responsibility for|ensuring equity in the sharing of fiscal responsibilities|evolving acceptable revenue-sharing formula|evolving an acceptable wage for public servants|maintaining the fiscal supremacy of the central government": "The Revenue Mobilization, Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) was established in 1989 and is backed by the 1999 Constitution. Its chief duty is to monitor the inflow of revenue into the Federation Account and to review and propose the formula for sharing that revenue among the federal, state and local governments, which the National Assembly then approves: B. It also fixes the pay of political office holders, not the wages of public servants (C), which are negotiated with labour unions. It does not exist to keep the central government supreme (D). Memory tip: RMAFC = the Federation Account's sharing-formula commission.",
 "|The major role of NDIC is to|regulate the activities of insurance companies|accept customers' deposits|ensure adequate capitalization of insurance companies|insure bank deposits": "The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) was established in 1988 and began work in 1989. It insures the deposits of customers in licensed banks, so that if a bank fails, depositors are paid up to a set limit. It also supervises banks, alongside the CBN, to prevent failures, and acts as liquidator of failed banks: D. Accepting deposits (B) is the work of commercial banks themselves, and insurance companies are regulated by NAICOM, not NDIC (A, C). The NDIC Act 2023 now governs it, and the cover for deposit money banks is ₦5 million per depositor. Memory tip: NDIC = Deposits Insured.",
 "|In computing national income, transfers are excluded because|they are not payments for productive activities|the figures reduce the size of national income|it is difficult to get the exact figures|they are received only by the poor": "National income measures the value of goods and services produced in a country in a year. Transfer payments are payments for which no good or service is given in return: pensions, unemployment benefits, scholarships, gifts and remittances from relatives. They merely move income from one person to another without any new production. Counting them would add income that matches no output and would double-count income already earned by someone else. So they are excluded because they are not payments for productive activities: A. They are not excluded because they are small, hard to measure (C) or received only by the poor (D). Memory tip: no production, no national income.",
 "|Choice involves opportunity cost because|available resources are inadequate|there are many goods to select from|goods give different utilities|not all goods have the same price": "Human wants are unlimited, but the resources for satisfying them (land, labour, capital, money, time) are scarce or inadequate. Because we cannot have everything, we must choose, and every choice means giving up the next best alternative. That sacrifice is the opportunity cost. So choice involves opportunity cost because available resources are inadequate: A. Having many goods to choose from (B), different utilities (C) or different prices (D) would not force a sacrifice if resources were unlimited. Scarcity is the root of both choice and opportunity cost. Memory tip: scarcity forces choice; choice creates opportunity cost.",
 "|A straight line indifference curve indicates that the two products are|inferior goods|perfect substitutes|normal goods|close substitutes": "An indifference curve shows combinations of two goods that give a consumer the same satisfaction. Normally it is convex to the origin, because the marginal rate of substitution (MRS) falls as one good is substituted for the other. A straight-line indifference curve has a constant slope, so the consumer is always willing to swap the goods at the same rate however much of each he has. That happens only when the goods are perfect substitutes, such as two brands of identical bottled water: B. The trap: D (close substitutes) gives a curve that is only slightly convex, not straight. An L-shaped curve shows perfect complements. Memory tip: straight line = perfect swap.",
 "|The law of demand can be expressed as|P = f(Qd)|Qd = f(1/P)|P = f(1/Qd)|Qd = f(P)": "The law of demand states that, other things being equal, the quantity demanded of a good varies inversely with its price. In functional form this is written Qd = f(P): quantity demanded is a function of price. The inverse direction is shown by the downward-sloping demand curve, or a negative coefficient as in Qd = a − bP: D. Options A and C put price as the dependent variable, which reverses the usual form. Option B, Qd = f(1/P), also hints at the inverse link and can be argued for, but the standard textbook expression is Qd = f(P). Memory tip: Quantity depends on Price: Qd = f(P).",
 "|A commodity will be demanded only if|consumers' income increases|it has no close substitutes|it has utility|the price is low": "Demand for a commodity exists only if consumers want it, and they want it only if it can satisfy a need or want, which economists call utility. A good with no utility, such as a broken pen, will not be demanded at any price. So a commodity will be demanded only if it has utility: C. Income rising (A), absence of substitutes (B) and a low price (D) affect how much is demanded, but none is a necessary condition: goods are demanded even when income does not rise, substitutes exist or prices are high. Effective demand also needs ability to pay. Memory tip: no utility, no demand.",
 "|The range of the data 14, 13, 15, 18, 20, 35 and 13 is|22|20|18|13": "The range is the simplest measure of dispersion: the difference between the highest and lowest values.\nStep 1: Arrange the data: 13, 13, 14, 15, 18, 20, 35.\nStep 2: Highest = 35; lowest = 13.\nStep 3: Range = 35 − 13 = 22: A.\nThe trap: B (20) is the second highest value, and D (13) is the lowest value (and the mode), not the range. The range is easily affected by one extreme value such as 35. Memory tip: range = top minus bottom.",
 "|The burden of a government tax on a commodity whose demand is inelastic will|be borne only by the government|be shared equally between consumers and producers|fall more heavily on consumers|fall more heavily on producers": "The incidence of a tax is who finally bears its burden. When a tax is placed on a commodity, sellers try to pass it on by raising the price. If demand is inelastic, consumers keep buying almost the same quantity even at the higher price, as with salt, fuel or essential drugs, so sellers can pass most of the tax on. The burden therefore falls more heavily on consumers: C. If demand were elastic, buyers would switch away and producers would bear more (D). Equal sharing (B) happens only in a special case, and government does not bear the tax (A); it collects it. Memory tip: inelastic demand, consumer pays.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nA movement from K to M will|decrease the consumer's utility|increase the price of the commodity|increase the consumer's utility|leave the consumer's utility unchanged": "An indifference curve joins all combinations of two goods X and Y that give a consumer the same level of satisfaction, so the consumer is indifferent between them. In the diagram, K (on the steep part) and M (on the flat part) both lie on the same indifference curve, labelled I. Moving from K to M changes the mix of X and Y but not the total satisfaction, so utility is unchanged: D. Utility would increase (C) only by moving to a higher curve such as II or III. Prices (B) are shown by the budget line ST, not by the indifference curve. Memory tip: same curve, same satisfaction.",
 "|Use the diagram above to answer the question. \nAt point P, the marginal rate of substitution is|less than one|greater than one|zero|unity": "The marginal rate of substitution (MRS) of X for Y is the amount of Y a consumer gives up for one more unit of X while staying equally satisfied: the slope of the indifference curve. At P the budget line ST just touches the highest attainable curve, III, so P is the consumer's equilibrium and the MRS equals the slope of ST (the price ratio). ST runs at about 45°, OS being about equal to OT, so the MRS at P is one: unity, D. This reading is debatable only because the slope comes from the drawing. The trap: C (zero) is the MRS on the flat stretches of the curves, as at M, not at P. Memory tip: at equilibrium, MRS = price ratio = slope of the budget line.",
 "|Derived demand is normally used with reference to|superior goods|the cost of production|the factors of production|inferior goods": "Derived demand means that something is demanded not for its own sake but for what it helps to produce. The term is normally used for the factors of production: firms demand land, labour, capital and entrepreneurs because consumers demand the goods those factors make. If the demand for houses rises, the demand for bricklayers, cement and land rises too: C. Superior and inferior goods (A, D) are about how demand changes with income, and cost of production (B) is a supply idea. Memory tip: Derived demand Depends on the demand for the final product.",
 "|If the marginal utility of good X exceeds that of good Y, this implies that|X will be cheaper than Y|Y is an inferior good|consuming more of X will increase total utility|a rational consumer will buy less of X": "Marginal utility (MU) is the extra satisfaction from consuming one more unit of a good. If the MU of X exceeds that of Y, then the MU of X is positive, and whenever MU is positive, consuming one more unit adds to total utility. So consuming more of X will increase total utility: C. Marginal utility says nothing about which good is cheaper (A) or whether Y is inferior (B), which depends on income. A rational consumer compares MU per naira (MUx/Px with MUy/Py), and if MUx/Px is higher he buys more of X, not less (D). Total utility stops rising only when MU reaches zero. Memory tip: positive MU, rising TU.",
 "|A distribution is said to be positively skewed if it|is bell-shaped|has a long tail to the left|is bimodal|has a long tail to the right": "Skewness describes how lopsided a frequency distribution is. A normal distribution is symmetrical and bell-shaped (A), with mean, median and mode equal. In a positively (right) skewed distribution, most values are bunched on the left and a few very large values stretch a long tail to the right: D. The mean is pulled up by the large values, so mean > median > mode. Income distribution in Nigeria is positively skewed: most people earn modest incomes, a few earn a great deal. A long tail to the left (B) is negative skewness, and bimodal (C) means two peaks. The trap: B is the mirror image, negative skewness. Memory tip: the tail tells the sign: right tail, positive.",
 "|An important determinant of price elasticity of demand is|the ease of substitution|the state of technology|the prices of the commodities|government policy": "Price elasticity of demand measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a change in price. Its main determinants are the availability (ease) of substitutes, whether the good is a necessity or a luxury, the proportion of income spent on it, the number of uses, habit and the time period. The most important is ease of substitution: if close substitutes exist, buyers switch when price rises, so demand is elastic; if not, demand is inelastic: A. The state of technology (B) affects supply, not demand elasticity, and government policy (D) is not a determinant of elasticity. Memory tip: more substitutes, more elastic.",
 "|A decision on input combination solves the economic problem of|for whom to produce|how to produce|when to produce|what to produce": "Every society must answer the basic questions of what to produce, how to produce, for whom to produce, and how efficiently. \"How to produce\" is about the method or technique of production: which combination of inputs (land, labour, capital) to use, for example whether to farm with hoes and many workers (labour-intensive) or with tractors and few workers (capital-intensive). So a decision on input combination solves the problem of how to produce: B. What to produce (D) concerns which goods and in what quantities, and for whom (A) concerns distribution. \"When to produce\" (C) is not one of the basic economic problems. Memory tip: How = the recipe of inputs.",
 "|Given that demand and price remain unchanged, an outward shift of the supply curve will lead to|hoarding|excess demand|a black market|excess supply": "At equilibrium, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. An outward (rightward) shift of the supply curve means producers offer more at every price, for example after a good harvest. If demand and price stay where they were, the new quantity supplied at that price is greater than the quantity demanded, so there is excess supply, a glut: D. Normally this surplus pushes the price down to a new equilibrium. Excess demand (B), hoarding (A) and black markets (C) arise from shortages, as under a maximum price, which is the opposite case. The trap: B is the result of an inward shift of supply or a rise in demand, the reverse situation. Memory tip: more supply at the same price = surplus.",
 "|A major objective of the Federal Government's initiative on cassava production in Nigeria is to|ensure the availability of cassava|make cassava a staple food in the country|provide raw materials for industries|diversify the export base of the economy": "In 2002 the Obasanjo government launched the Presidential Initiative on Cassava Production and Export. Nigeria was already the world's largest producer of cassava, but almost all of it was eaten at home as garri, fufu and lafun. The initiative set out to raise output and processing so that cassava chips, starch, flour and ethanol could be sold abroad, earning foreign exchange and reducing the country's heavy dependence on crude oil exports. That is diversifying the export base: D. Supplying raw materials to local industries (C) was part of the programme, but the headline objective was export earnings. Today the same aim continues under policies promoting cassava flour in bread and local starch production. Memory tip: oil-dependent country plus new export crop equals diversification.",
 "|Upstream oil activities involve the|marketing of finished products|exploration of crude oil|management of crude oil|refining of crude oil": "The oil industry is divided into stages. The upstream sector is everything involved in finding and getting crude oil out of the ground: exploration (seismic surveys), drilling and production. The midstream sector moves and stores it (pipelines, tankers, storage), and the downstream sector refines crude into petrol, diesel and kerosene and markets these products to consumers. Exploration of crude oil is therefore an upstream activity: B. Refining (D) and marketing of finished products (A) are downstream. The trap: D, refining, sounds like an early stage because it handles crude oil, but it is downstream since the crude has already been produced. Memory tip: upstream is up at the source, where the oil is found; downstream flows down to the filling station.",
 "|An upward review of the retirement age will affect the|wage rate|tax rate|supply of labour|demand for labour": "The supply of labour is the number of people willing and able to work at a given wage. If the government raises the retirement age, for example from 60 to 65 years, workers who would have left the workforce stay in it for five more years. The working population, and so the supply of labour, increases: C. The change does not directly set the wage rate (A) or tax rate (B), and the demand for labour (D) depends on employers' need for workers, which this policy does not change. Nigeria raised the retirement age of teachers to 65 in 2022, a real example. Memory tip: who may work affects supply; who wants workers affects demand.",
 "|Environmental degradation is more associated with|decreased urbanization|localization of industries|urban-rural migration|location of industries": "Localization of industries is the concentration of many firms of the same industry in one area, such as the oil companies around Port Harcourt or textile firms in Kano. When many factories crowd together, the area suffers air and water pollution, waste dumping, traffic congestion, slums and pressure on land: these are classic disadvantages of localization and add up to environmental degradation: B. Location of industries (D) only means where a single firm decides to site its plant, which need not degrade anything. The trap: D sounds almost the same as B, but location is a firm's choice of site, while localization is the clustering of an industry. Memory tip: localization is crowding, and crowding brings pollution.",
 "|The Malthusian theory of population has been rendered ineffective by|natural disasters|birth control|technical progress|government policies": "Thomas Malthus (1798) argued that population grows geometrically (2, 4, 8, 16) while food supply grows only arithmetically (1, 2, 3, 4), so population would outrun food and be checked by famine, war and disease. His prediction failed mainly because of technical progress: new farming methods, improved seeds, fertilizers, irrigation and machines (for example the Green Revolution) raised food output far faster than he imagined: C. Birth control (B) has slowed population growth in many countries, but Malthus himself discussed moral restraint, and the decisive reason his theory failed is that food production kept pace through technology. Memory tip: Malthus forgot the machine.",
 "|Industrialization is a prerequisite for balanced economic growth because it|enhances productivity in the manufacturing sector|expands employment opportunities in urban areas|encourages trade liberalization|stimulates development of all sectors": "Balanced growth means developing all sectors of the economy together so that each provides a market for the others. Industrialization is seen as a prerequisite because factories create demand for agricultural raw materials (cotton, cocoa, palm oil), minerals and energy, while supplying tools, fertilizers and processed goods to farmers and services to the rest of the economy. These linkages stimulate development in every sector: D. Higher manufacturing productivity (A) and urban jobs (B) are real benefits, but they concern only part of the economy and do not explain \"balanced\" growth. Trade liberalization (C) is a trade policy, not a result of industrialization. Memory tip: balanced growth means all sectors, so look for the word \"all\".",
 "|A country would develop its agricultural sector first so as to|stabilize the prices of industrial products|create a market for the industrial sector|stabilize the prices of agricultural products|create a market for the agricultural sector": "Many economists argue that a developing country should develop agriculture first because most people live and work on farms. When farm output and incomes rise, rural households can afford manufactured goods such as clothing, bicycles and radios, so agriculture creates a market for the industrial sector: B. A stronger agriculture also supplies food for factory workers, raw materials for industry and foreign exchange to import machinery. Stable agricultural prices (C) may be a by-product of more food, but they are not the reason for developing agriculture first; this item is debatable, and B is the stronger reason. The trap: D reverses the link, since industry is what creates a market for farm produce, not the other way round. Memory tip: rich farmers are customers for factories.",
 "|The labour force of a country best describes|people within the working age|people within the retirement age|the number of people in paid employment|the number of people receiving pension": "The labour force (working population) is the people within the legal working age, roughly 15 to 64 years in Nigeria, who are able and willing to work. It includes both the employed and the unemployed who are actively looking for work: A. People in paid employment (C) are only part of the labour force, because the unemployed job-seekers are left out, and the self-employed farmer or trader is not \"paid\" by an employer but still counts. Retirees and pensioners (B, D) are outside the labour force. The trap: C is tempting because workers come to mind, but it leaves out the unemployed who are still part of the labour force. Memory tip: labour force = employed + unemployed job-seekers of working age.",
 "|The movement of people from rural to urban areas affects the|sex distribution of population|geographical distribution of population|age distribution of population|occupational distribution of population": "Population can be studied by its distribution: by age (young, working, old), by sex (male, female), by occupation (farming, industry, services) and geographically (where people live). Rural-urban migration moves people from villages to towns such as Lagos, Kano and Port Harcourt, so it changes where the population lives, the geographical distribution: B. It may also change the occupational structure over time, as migrants leave farming for trade or factory work, but the direct effect of a movement of people is on location. Memory tip: migration is a movement on the map, so it changes the geographical distribution.",
 "|Foreign investment can be attracted to Nigeria through|a review of interest rates|a review of the naturalization policy|review of the tax policy|the revaluation of the naira": "Foreign investors put money where the after-tax return is highest and safest. Government can attract them through its tax policy: tax holidays (pioneer status) for new industries, lower company income tax, relief on dividends and profits sent home, and duty waivers on imported machinery. A review of the tax policy to offer such incentives is therefore the route given: C. Revaluation of the naira (D) makes Nigerian assets and labour dearer in foreign currency, discouraging investors. A review of interest rates (A) mainly affects local borrowing and portfolio flows, not direct investment. Nigeria today also offers export processing zones and pioneer status to foreign investors. Memory tip: investors follow returns, and tax cuts raise returns.",
 "|In a developing economy, productivity is measured by the|output-labour ratio|output growth rate|output per capita|capital-output ratio": "Productivity is the amount of output produced per unit of input over a period. The most common measure, especially in developing countries where labour is the most plentiful factor, is labour productivity: output divided by the number of workers or hours worked, the output-labour ratio: A. If a factory of 20 workers produces 1,000 bags of flour a day, productivity is 50 bags per worker. Output per capita (C) divides output by the whole population, including children and the old, so it measures average living standards, not productivity. The capital-output ratio (D) shows how much capital is needed per unit of output. Memory tip: productivity is output per worker.",
 "|The survival of a partnership depends on|political stability|the managerial ability of the partners|access to finance|the goodwill of the partners": "A partnership is a business owned by two to twenty partners who share capital, management and profits, usually with unlimited liability. Since there is no separate board of directors, the partners themselves make every decision; their skill in running the business determines whether it grows or collapses. The survival of a partnership therefore depends on the managerial ability of the partners: B. Mutual trust and goodwill among partners (D) also matter, since disagreement can break up a partnership, so this item is debatable. Access to finance (C) and political stability (A) affect all businesses alike. Memory tip: in a partnership the partners are the managers, so their ability decides its fate.",
 "|The main objective of WTO is to|provide a mechanism for tariff reductions|ensure adequate tariff protection for infant industries|help debtor nations solve their problems|help countries with chronic balance of payments problems": "The World Trade Organization was set up on 1 January 1995 to replace GATT (General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade, 1947). Its main purpose is to liberalise world trade by providing a forum and rules for negotiating the reduction of tariffs and other trade barriers, and a mechanism for settling trade disputes between members: A. Helping countries with balance of payments problems (D) is the job of the IMF, and help for debtor nations (C) is mainly from the IMF and World Bank. Protecting infant industries (B) is the opposite of WTO's free-trade aim. Nigeria has been a member since 1995. Memory tip: WTO = Way To Open trade.",
 "|A continuous increase in a nation's income accompanied by the structural transformation of all indices is called|economic reforms|economic planning|economic development|economic growth": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real national income or output. Economic development is broader: it is growth accompanied by structural transformation, meaning a shift from farming to industry and services, better education and health, less poverty, more employment and improved living standards. Because the question adds \"structural transformation of all indices\", the answer is economic development: C. The trap: D, economic growth, is the increase in income alone, without the change in the structure of the economy and welfare. Economic planning (B) and reforms (A) are tools a government uses to achieve development. Memory tip: growth is more; development is more and better.",
 "|One of the ways of correcting a deficit balance of payments is to|increase imports|revalue a country's currency|reduce exports|devalue a country's currency": "A deficit in the balance of payments means a country pays out more foreign exchange (for imports and other items) than it earns. Devaluation lowers the value of the home currency against foreign currencies. Nigerian exports become cheaper for foreigners to buy, so exports rise, while imports become dearer in naira, so imports fall. The gap closes: D. Revaluation (B) does the opposite, making exports dearer and imports cheaper, which would worsen the deficit. Increasing imports (A) and reducing exports (C) also worsen it. The trap: B, revalue, looks similar to devalue but works in the opposite direction. Memory tip: devalue to cut the deficit.",
 "|The minimum number of shareholders for a joint-stock company is|4|2|7|3": "A joint-stock company is a business owned by shareholders, with its capital divided into shares and limited liability. It may be a private limited company (Ltd) or a public limited company (Plc). The minimum number of members for a private company has long been two, so the minimum for a joint-stock company is two: B. In Nigeria, the Companies and Allied Matters Act 1990, in force in 2007, allowed any two persons to form a company, public or private. Seven (C) was the old minimum for a public company under the Companies Act 1968, and this item is debatable because some textbooks still teach that figure. CAMA 2020 now allows one person to form a private company. Memory tip: two to start any company.",
 "|The gains from international trade are shared on the basis of the|terms of trade|bilateral trade agreements|cost of production of each of the traded goods|quantity produced of each of the traded goods": "When countries specialise according to comparative advantage and trade, total world output rises. How that gain is divided depends on the rate at which one country's exports exchange for the other's, the terms of trade. Terms of trade = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100. If a country's export prices rise relative to its import prices, it gets more imports for each unit of exports and takes a bigger share of the gains: A. Costs of production (C) determine comparative advantage, that is whether trade is worthwhile, but not how the gain is shared. Memory tip: comparative advantage creates the gain; terms of trade share it.",
 "|A characteristic of the linear production function is that|the percentage change in output is less than one|the percentage change in inputs exceeds output|output and all inputs change by the same percentage|the percentage change in output is greater than one": "A production function shows how output depends on inputs. A linear (linearly homogeneous) production function is one in which multiplying all inputs by a factor multiplies output by the same factor. If labour and capital both rise by 10%, output rises by exactly 10%. This is constant returns to scale: output and all inputs change by the same percentage: C. Increasing returns to scale (output rises by a greater percentage than inputs, D) and decreasing returns (output rises by a smaller percentage, A and B) are not features of a linear function. The Cobb-Douglas function with exponents adding up to one is the standard example. Memory tip: linear means a straight line, so output keeps pace exactly with inputs.",
 "|The price per unit of a commodity to a buyer is the same as the|normal profit of the seller|average revenue of the seller of the commodity|marginal cost of the commodity|marginal revenue of the seller": "Average revenue is total revenue divided by the number of units sold. Total revenue = price × quantity, so AR = (P × Q) ÷ Q = P. The price per unit the buyer pays is therefore the seller's average revenue: B. Marginal revenue (D) is the extra revenue from selling one more unit; it equals price only under perfect competition, and is below price when the seller must lower price to sell more. The trap: D, marginal revenue, equals price only under perfect competition, so it is not always the same as the price paid. Normal profit (A) and marginal cost (C) are costs to the seller, not the price. Memory tip: AR is always the price; the demand curve is the AR curve.",
 "|If the total fixed cost is the same regardless of output, the average fixed cost will be|decreasing|minimum|maximum|increasing": "Total fixed cost (rent, insurance, salaries of permanent staff) does not change with output. Average fixed cost is TFC divided by output. Suppose TFC = ₦1,000: at 10 units AFC = ₦100; at 100 units AFC = ₦10; at 1,000 units AFC = ₦1. The same fixed amount is spread over more units, so AFC keeps falling as output rises, approaching but never reaching zero: A. AFC has no minimum (B) or maximum (C) point and is never increasing (D). Its curve is a rectangular hyperbola. Memory tip: fixed cost spread thinner over more units.",
 "|The division of labour requires that the task to be performed|requires no special knowledge|is simple but strenuous|must be undertaken by experts|must be done in stages": "Division of labour (specialisation by process) means breaking the production of a good into separate stages or operations, with each worker doing only one. Adam Smith's pin factory is the famous example: one man draws the wire, another straightens it, a third cuts it, and so on. This is only possible if the task can be done in stages: D. The task does not have to be simple, strenuous or free of special knowledge (A, B), and it does not need experts (C); in fact division of labour lets ordinary workers become skilled through repetition. Memory tip: divide the work into stages, divide the stages among workers.",
 "|The diagram above shows a firm operating under conditions of monopolistic competition. The curve W represents its|marginal cost|average cost|average revenue|marginal revenue": "Read the shapes. A monopolistically competitive firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve, which is its average revenue (AR) curve. Its marginal revenue (MR) curve also slopes downward but is steeper, lying below AR. In the diagram, X is the steep U-shaped curve rising sharply, the marginal cost; Y is the flatter U-shaped curve, the average cost; M is the steeper downward line, MR; and W is the flatter downward line above it, AR: C. The trap: D, marginal revenue, is the other downward-sloping line, but MR is always the steeper one lying below AR. Memory tip: of the two falling lines, the upper flatter one is AR, the lower steeper one is MR.",
 "|When a firm's average revenue curve is downward sloping, its price elasticity of demand will be|zero|greater than one|one|between zero and infinity": "Elasticity of demand is measured at each point on a curve. A horizontal AR curve (perfect competition) has infinite elasticity; a vertical one has zero elasticity. Along a downward-sloping straight-line AR curve, elasticity falls from very high near the price axis, through one at the midpoint, to very low near the quantity axis. So for a downward-sloping AR curve the only statement always true is that elasticity lies between zero and infinity: D. A profit-maximising monopolist does produce where demand is elastic (greater than one, B), because marginal revenue must be positive, but the question does not limit itself to that point, so this item is debatable. Memory tip: flat is infinite, vertical is zero, sloping is in between.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNo. of workers ... Total output (kg) ... MP\n1 ... 39 ... -\n2 ... 52 ... 13\n3 ... 60 ... 8\n4 ... 66 ... 6\n5 ... 66 ... 0\n6 ... 63 ... -3\n\nThe maximum number of workers that should be hired is|2|3|4|5": "Step 1: Read the total output column: 39, 52, 60, 66, 66, 63 kg for 1 to 6 workers.\nStep 2: Total output is highest, 66 kg, with 4 workers. The 5th worker adds nothing (MP = 0) and the 6th reduces output (MP = −3 kg).\nStep 3: A firm should never pay a worker who adds no output, so the most it should employ is 4 workers: C.\nSome textbooks say production can go up to the point where MP = 0 (5 workers), so this item is debatable, but hiring the 5th worker only adds to cost. Memory tip: stop hiring when the next worker adds nothing.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nNo. of workers ... Total output (kg) ... MP\n1 ... 39 ... -\n2 ... 52 ... 13\n3 ... 60 ... 8\n4 ... 66 ... 6\n5 ... 66 ... 0\n6 ... 63 ... -3\n\nWhat is the marginal productivity when the number of workers is increased from 2 to 3?|8kg|20kg|60kg|180kg": "Marginal product (marginal productivity) is the extra output from employing one more worker.\nStep 1: Total output with 3 workers = 60 kg.\nStep 2: Total output with 2 workers = 52 kg.\nStep 3: MP of the 3rd worker = 60 − 52 = 8 kg: A.\nThe MP column of the table confirms 8. Option C (60 kg) is the total output with 3 workers, not the change.\nThe trap: B (20 kg) is the average product, the slip students make when they divide total output by the number of workers instead of finding the change. Memory tip: marginal means the change, average means the share.",
 "|A firm incurs short-run costs when|it cannot increase prices|operation is at its later stages|operation is at its early stages|some inputs cannot be varied": "The short run is a period too short for a firm to change all its inputs: at least one factor, usually the size of the plant, machinery or buildings, is fixed, while others such as labour and raw materials can be varied. Because some inputs cannot be varied, the firm's costs divide into fixed costs and variable costs; these are short-run costs: D. In the long run every input can be changed, so all costs are variable. The short run is not defined by the stage of the firm's life (B, C) or by its ability to raise prices (A). Memory tip: short run, something stays fixed; long run, everything can change.",
 "|The world market for crude oil refers to the|total demand for and supply of crude oil|total supply by OPEC members|difference between the supply of and demand for oil|excess crude oil produced in the world": "A market in economics is not just a place: it is the whole arrangement through which buyers and sellers of a commodity meet and fix prices. The world market for crude oil is the total world demand for crude oil from refiners and countries everywhere, and the total supply from all producers, OPEC and non-OPEC (the USA, Russia, Norway and others): A. OPEC supplies only part of the world's oil (B). The difference between supply and demand (C) is a surplus or shortage, and excess crude (D) is only a glut; neither is the market. Brent crude is the main world price benchmark, and Nigeria's Bonny Light is priced against it. Memory tip: market = all buyers + all sellers.",
 "|An increase in the marginal cost of production causes|a downward movement along the supply curve|a leftward shift of the supply curve|a rightward shift of the supply curve|an upward movement along the supply curve": "A firm's short-run supply curve is the part of its marginal cost curve above average variable cost. If marginal cost rises, perhaps because wages, fuel or raw materials become dearer, the firm is willing to supply less at every price, or needs a higher price for each quantity. The whole supply curve shifts to the left (upwards): B. A movement along the supply curve (A, D) happens only when the good's own price changes. A rightward shift (C) comes from lower costs or better technology. Memory tip: higher costs, less supply, shift left.",
 "|The choice of what to produce in a command economy is determined by|the central government|the government and labour unions|industrial establishments|labour unions": "In a command (centrally planned or socialist) economy, the state owns most of the means of production, and a central planning authority set up by the central government decides the basic economic questions: what to produce, how to produce it and for whom. Prices and consumer choices play little part. The former Soviet Union and North Korea are examples. So the choice of what to produce is determined by the central government: A. In a capitalist economy the same choice is made by consumers and firms through the price mechanism. Labour unions (B, D) and industrial establishments (C) carry out the plan but do not decide it. Memory tip: command means the government gives the commands.",
 "|The term ceteris paribus implies that|resources are scarce|human problems can be solved|human beings are unpredictable|all factors are fully utilized": "Ceteris paribus is Latin for \"other things being equal\" or \"other things remaining constant\". Economists use it to isolate one relationship: the law of demand says that if price rises, quantity demanded falls, ceteris paribus, meaning income, tastes and the prices of other goods stay unchanged. Economists need this assumption because in real life many things change at once and human behaviour is not fully predictable. None of the options says this exactly. D is the answer intended, the only option about \"all factors\", but \"fully utilized\" is an assumption of the production possibility curve, not the meaning of ceteris paribus, so the intended answer is not fully correct: D. Memory tip: ceteris paribus = hold everything else still.",
 "|A commodity is described as inferior when the|substitution effect is negative|substitution effect is positive|income effect is positive|income effect is negative": "A rise in income has an income effect on demand. For a normal good the income effect is positive: people buy more of it when their income rises. For an inferior good the income effect is negative: as income rises, people buy less of it and switch to better goods, as a family moving from garri to rice or from public buses to cars. So a commodity is inferior when its income effect is negative: D. The substitution effect of a price fall (A, B) always encourages buying more of the cheaper good, for normal and inferior goods alike, so it does not define an inferior good. The trap: C, positive income effect, describes a normal good, the exact opposite. Memory tip: inferior goods go down when income goes up.",
 "|The coefficient of the price elasticity of supply is always|negative|positive|zero|constant": "Price elasticity of supply = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price. By the law of supply, a higher price brings a larger quantity supplied, so the two changes have the same sign and the coefficient is positive: B. For example, if the price of yam rises by 10% and the quantity supplied rises by 5%, elasticity = 5 ÷ 10 = +0.5. By contrast, price elasticity of demand has a negative coefficient because price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions. The value of elasticity of supply can be zero only in the special case of perfectly inelastic supply, and it is not constant along most curves. Memory tip: supply and price move together, so the sign is plus.",
 "|The range is used to|measure the difference between the mean and the mode|calculate the standard deviation|estimate the mean from grouped data|determine the spread of a set of data": "The range is the simplest measure of dispersion: range = highest value − lowest value. For the scores 12, 15, 9, 20 and 11, the range is 20 − 9 = 11. It tells us how widely the data are spread out: D. It does not measure the gap between the mean and the mode (A), is not used to calculate the standard deviation (B), which uses deviations from the mean, and is not used to estimate the mean (C). Its weakness is that it uses only two values, so one extreme figure can distort it. Memory tip: range = top minus bottom = spread.",
 "|If P = ½(Qs + 15), what is the quantity supplied at ₦9.00?|33.0|12.0|3.0|1.5": "The supply function links price (P) and quantity supplied (Qs).\nStep 1: Substitute P = 9: 9 = ½(Qs + 15).\nStep 2: Multiply both sides by 2: 18 = Qs + 15.\nStep 3: Subtract 15: Qs = 18 − 15 = 3.0: C.\nCheck: ½(3 + 15) = ½ × 18 = 9.\nThe trap: A (33.0) is what you get by adding 15 to 18 instead of subtracting it. Memory tip: clear the fraction first, then move the constant.",
 "|The major determinant of cross elasticity of demand is the|level of income of consumers|closeness of the substitutes|price of the good|degree of necessity of the good": "Cross elasticity of demand measures how the demand for one good responds to a change in the price of another: percentage change in quantity demanded of X ÷ percentage change in price of Y. For substitutes it is positive, for complements negative, and for unrelated goods zero. Its size depends mainly on how closely the two goods are related: close substitutes such as Peak and Three Crowns milk have a high positive cross elasticity, while distant substitutes have a low one. So the major determinant is the closeness of the substitutes: B. Income (A), the good's own price (C) and its necessity (D) determine income elasticity or own-price elasticity instead. Memory tip: cross elasticity = how closely two goods are tied.",
 "|The logical sequence of basic concepts in economics is|wants, scarcity, choice and opportunity cost|wants, choice, scarcity and opportunity cost|scarcity, wants, choice and scale of preference|scarcity, choice, scale of preference and opportunity cost": "Economics starts from human wants, which are unlimited. The resources to satisfy them are scarce, which is the basic economic problem. Because of scarcity, we must make a choice among wants, arranging them on a scale of preference. Whatever we choose, we give up the next best alternative, which is the opportunity cost. So the logical sequence is wants, scarcity, choice and opportunity cost: A. B puts choice before scarcity, but there would be no need to choose without scarcity. C and D begin with scarcity, but scarcity only exists relative to wants. The trap: B lists the same four concepts in a slightly different order, so read the order carefully. Memory tip: Wise Students Choose Opportunity (W-S-C-O).",
 "|When the demand for a good increases owing to an increase in income, it means that|inflation has taken place|demand has exceeded supply|there is an increase in demand|there is an increase in quantity demanded": "A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the demand curve, called a change in quantity demanded. A change in any other factor, such as income, tastes or the prices of related goods, shifts the whole demand curve; this is a change in demand. A rise in income shifts the demand curve for a normal good to the right, so there is an increase in demand: C. The trap: D, an increase in quantity demanded, is the term for a movement along the curve caused by a fall in the good's own price, not by income. Demand exceeding supply (B) is a shortage, and inflation (A) is a general rise in prices. Memory tip: own price moves you along; anything else shifts the curve.",
 "|A firm operating at full capacity will have a|fairly inelastic supply curve|fairly elastic supply curve|perfectly elastic supply curve|perfectly inelastic supply curve": "Elasticity of supply depends on how easily a firm can change its output when price changes. A firm with spare capacity can expand output quickly, so its supply is elastic. A firm already at full capacity is using all its machines, space and workers; it cannot produce more, at least in the short run, however high the price goes. Quantity supplied does not respond to price at all, so the supply curve is vertical and supply is perfectly inelastic: D. A perfectly elastic curve (C) is horizontal, the opposite case. The trap: A, fairly inelastic, is close, but at full capacity output cannot rise at all, so supply is perfectly, not fairly, inelastic. Memory tip: full capacity, fixed quantity, vertical line.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nClass mark ... 2 ... 5 ... 8 ... 11 ... 14\nFrequency ... 2 ... 3 ... 1 ... 3 ... 1\nThe table shows the wage distribution of workers.\n\nCalculate the mean wage|₦7.40|₦4.00|₦10.00|₦14.80": "For a frequency table, mean = Σfx ÷ Σf.\nStep 1: Multiply each class mark by its frequency: 2 × 2 = 4; 5 × 3 = 15; 8 × 1 = 8; 11 × 3 = 33; 14 × 1 = 14.\nStep 2: Σfx = 4 + 15 + 8 + 33 + 14 = 74.\nStep 3: Σf = 2 + 3 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 10.\nStep 4: Mean = 74 ÷ 10 = ₦7.40: A.\nThe trap: D (₦14.80) results from dividing 74 by 5, the number of class marks, instead of by 10, the number of workers. Memory tip: divide by the total frequency, never by the number of classes.",
 "|Use the table below to answer the question: \nClass mark ... 2 ... 5 ... 8 ... 11 ... 14\nFrequency ... 2 ... 3 ... 1 ... 3 ... 1\nThe table shows the wage distribution of workers.\n\nWhat is the median wage?|₦8.00|₦5.00|₦11.00|₦6.50": "The median is the middle value when all the values are arranged in order.\nStep 1: List all 10 wages: 2, 2, 5, 5, 5, 8, 11, 11, 11, 14.\nStep 2: With 10 values the middle lies between the 5th and 6th values ((10 + 1) ÷ 2 = 5.5th position).\nStep 3: The 5th value is 5 and the 6th is 8.\nStep 4: Median = (5 + 8) ÷ 2 = ₦6.50: D.\nThe trap: A (₦8.00) is the middle class mark of the five, the slip of ignoring the frequencies. Memory tip: spread out every value first, then find the middle.",
 "|An economic system in which the producer and the consumer are the same is referred to as a|capitalist economy|mixed economy|subsistence economy|socialist economy": "In a subsistence economy, households produce mainly to meet their own needs rather than to sell: a farming family grows its own yam and cassava, builds its own house and makes its own tools. Since what is produced is consumed by the people who produced it, the producer and the consumer are the same: C. There is little trade, money or specialisation. In capitalist (A), socialist (D) and mixed (B) economies, goods are produced for others, through markets or state planning. Traditional rural communities in Nigeria were largely subsistence before cash crops spread. Memory tip: subsistence = produce to survive, consume what you make.",
 "|A persistent rise in the prices of inputs will lead to|demand-pull inflation|cost-push inflation|stagflation|hyperinflation": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. Cost-push inflation occurs when the costs of production rise, such as wages, raw materials, imported inputs, fuel and electricity tariffs, and firms raise their prices to protect their profits. A persistent rise in input prices is exactly this: B. A rise in Nigerian diesel and petrol prices that makes transport and manufacturing dearer is a familiar example. Demand-pull inflation (A) is caused by too much spending (\"too much money chasing too few goods\"). Stagflation (C) is inflation combined with stagnant output and unemployment, and hyperinflation (D) is extremely rapid inflation. Memory tip: cost-push starts with costs; demand-pull starts with demand.",
 "|An advantage of electronic banking is that it|enables many customers to operate joint accounts|reduces the risk of bank fraud|enables a customer to operate many accounts from different locations|enables a customer to operate his account from different locations": "Electronic banking lets customers carry out transactions through ATMs, internet banking, mobile apps, POS terminals and USSD codes instead of visiting a branch. Its key advantage is convenience: a customer can check his balance, withdraw cash, pay bills and transfer money from his account wherever he is, at any time, from different locations: D. It does not create joint accounts (A), and it has not removed fraud (B); in fact cyber fraud is a risk of e-banking. The trap: C adds \"many accounts\", which is not the defining advantage; the point is operating one's account from anywhere. Nigeria's NIP instant transfer system and mobile money are today's examples. Memory tip: e-banking = your bank in your pocket.",
 "|The non-bank financial institutions that are entrusted with workers' contributions towards retirement are|mortgage institutions|investment companies|pension funds custodians|pension funds administrators": "Nigeria's Pension Reform Act 2004 set up the contributory pension scheme: the worker and the employer each pay a share of the salary into the worker's retirement savings account. Two kinds of non-bank financial institution handle this money. Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs) manage and invest the savings and pay the benefits, and Pension Fund Custodians (PFCs) receive the contributions and keep the pension assets in safe custody. Both are entrusted with workers' contributions, so the answer marked is pension funds administrators: D. Pension funds custodians (C) are also accepted. The National Pension Commission (PenCom) regulates them, and the scheme is now under the Pension Reform Act 2014. Mortgage institutions (A) and investment companies (B) do not hold retirement contributions. Memory tip: the administrator invests, the custodian keeps safe.",
 "|A reduction in the bank rate encourages commercial banks to create money by|decreasing the interest rate|maintaining the interest rate|doubling the interest rate|raising the interest rate": "The bank rate (now called the Monetary Policy Rate, MPR, in Nigeria) is the rate at which the Central Bank lends to commercial banks. When the CBN reduces it, banks can borrow from the CBN more cheaply, so they reduce the interest rates they charge their own customers. Cheaper loans encourage firms and households to borrow; every new loan creates a new deposit, so the money supply expands: A. Raising (D) or doubling (C) the interest rate would discourage borrowing and reduce money creation. This is expansionary monetary policy. Memory tip: bank rate down, lending rate down, borrowing and money supply up.",
 "|Corrections in the GNP figures for price changes require the use of a|retail price index|producer price index|price index|price deflator": "GNP measured at current prices rises both when real output grows and when prices rise. To find real GNP, we remove the effect of price changes by dividing by the GNP deflator (implicit price deflator): real GNP = nominal GNP ÷ deflator × 100. The deflator covers the prices of all goods and services in GNP, including capital goods and exports, so it is the correct tool: D. The retail price index (A) and consumer price index cover only goods bought by households, and the producer price index (B) only goods sold by producers, so they cannot correct the whole of GNP. Memory tip: deflate GNP with the GNP deflator.",
 "|The monetization policy is an offshoot of|PAYE|NEEDS|MDGs|SAP": "In 2003 the Obasanjo government introduced the monetisation policy: instead of giving public servants free houses, cars, drivers, utilities and other fringe benefits, it paid them their cash value. The aim was to cut government waste and reduce the cost of governance. It was part of the public sector reforms gathered into NEEDS, the National Economic Empowerment and Development Strategy launched in 2004: B. SAP (D) was the Structural Adjustment Programme of 1986 under Babangida. PAYE (A) is a method of collecting income tax, and the MDGs (C) were United Nations goals for 2000 to 2015. NEEDS was later followed by Vision 20:2020 and other plans. Memory tip: monetisation and NEEDS are both Obasanjo-era reforms.",
 "|In the circular flow of income model, the outflow variables represent|income|expenditure|injections|leakages": "The circular flow of income shows money flowing from households to firms (spending) and from firms to households (incomes). Some income leaves the flow instead of being spent on domestic output: savings, taxes and imports. These outflows are called leakages or withdrawals: D. Money entering the flow from outside the household-firm circle, namely investment, government spending and exports, is called injections (C). National income is in equilibrium when total leakages equal total injections (S + T + M = I + G + X). The trap: C, injections, are the inflows, the exact opposite of outflows. Memory tip: leakages leak out; injections are injected in.",
 "|Life insurance companies contribute to economic development by holding a part of their assets in|long-term financial instruments|equipment|cash and near money|money-market instruments": "Life insurance companies collect premiums over many years but pay out mainly on death or when policies mature, often decades later. Because their liabilities are long-term, they invest a large part of their assets in long-term financial instruments such as government bonds, company shares and debentures, and mortgages. This provides long-term capital for industry and government projects, promoting economic development: A. Cash and near money (C) and money-market instruments (D) are short-term assets, held only for day-to-day liquidity. Equipment (B) is a physical asset used in their own offices, not an investment channel. Memory tip: long-life policies, long-term investments.",
 "|One way of solving the problem of double counting is by computing national income using the|value-added method|income method|output method|expenditure method": "Double counting happens when the value of intermediate goods is counted more than once: if a farmer sells wheat to a miller for ₦100, the miller sells flour to a baker for ₦150, and the baker sells bread for ₦200, adding all sales gives ₦450, but the true output is only ₦200. The value-added method counts only the value each firm adds: ₦100 + ₦50 + ₦50 = ₦200, which equals the final value: A. The output method (C), if it counts total sales, is where the problem arises; value added (or counting final goods only) is its correction. Memory tip: count only what each stage adds.",
 "|The production possibility curve can be used to explain the underlying concepts of|scale of preference and choice|opportunity cost and choice|wants and means|opportunity cost and scale of preference": "The production possibility curve (PPC) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce when all its resources are fully used. Because resources are scarce, the economy cannot have everything, so it must choose one point on the curve. Moving along the curve to get more of one good means giving up some of the other, and the amount given up is the opportunity cost. So the PPC illustrates scarcity, choice and opportunity cost: B. Scale of preference is a list of an individual's wants in order of importance; it is not shown by the curve. The trap: D pairs opportunity cost with scale of preference, which sounds close but drops choice. Memory tip: the PPC is a \"menu\" of choices, and every move along it has a price called opportunity cost.",
 "|The deregulation of telecommunication services in Nigeria is a characteristic of|command economy|traditional economy|market economy|feudal economy": "In a market (capitalist) economy, private individuals and firms own resources and decide what to produce, guided by prices and profit, with little government control. Deregulation means removing government restrictions so that private firms can enter and compete. When Nigeria deregulated telecommunications (licensing private GSM operators from 2001, with NITEL's monopoly ended), it moved that sector towards the market system: C. A command economy is run by state planning, a traditional economy follows custom, and a feudal economy rests on lords and serfs. Today the sector is run by private operators such as MTN, Airtel and Glo under the Nigerian Communications Commission as regulator. Memory tip: deregulate, privatise, liberalise all point to the Market.",
 "|If the increase in the price of yams is used to estimate the inflation rate, this is an example of|deductive reasoning|inductive reasoning|normative reasoning|positive reasoning": "Economists reason in two ways. Deductive reasoning starts from a general principle and works down to particular conclusions. Inductive reasoning starts from particular observed facts and builds up to a general conclusion. Using the observed rise in the price of one item, yams, to estimate the general rate of inflation is moving from a particular fact to a general statement, so it is inductive reasoning: B. Normative reasoning involves value judgements about what ought to be, and positive reasoning deals with what is, but neither describes the direction of reasoning here. The trap: A (deductive) is its close partner; remember the direction of the reasoning. Memory tip: INductive builds IN from facts to a general rule; DEductive comes DOWN from a rule to facts.",
 "|If the standard deviation of a given data is 8.2, find its variance.|4.1|67.2|82.0|91.8": "Standard deviation and variance both measure how spread out data are. The variance is the average of the squared deviations from the mean, and the standard deviation is its square root. So to get the variance from the standard deviation, square it.\nStep 1: Variance = (standard deviation)².\nStep 2: 8.2 × 8.2 = 67.24.\nStep 3: To one decimal place, 67.2: B.\nHalving 8.2 (4.1) or multiplying it by 10 (82.0) does not give the variance. Memory tip: SD is the root, variance is the square; go from SD to variance by squaring, and back by taking the square root.",
 "|Use the data below to answer the question: \n54, 36, 62, 79, 83, 36, 62, 42, 62 and 42.\n\nCalculate the mean|45.5|55.6|55.8|65.8": "The arithmetic mean is the sum of all the values divided by the number of values.\nStep 1: Add the scores: 54 + 36 + 62 + 79 + 83 + 36 + 62 + 42 + 62 + 42 = 558.\nStep 2: Count the scores: there are 10.\nStep 3: Mean = 558 ÷ 10 = 55.8: C.\nCheck by grouping: 36 × 2 = 72, 42 × 2 = 84, 62 × 3 = 186, plus 54, 79 and 83 = 216; 72 + 84 + 186 + 216 = 558.\nThe trap: B (55.6) differs only in the last digit, so a small slip in adding gives it; always check the total twice. Memory tip: mean = total ÷ number; check the total by grouping repeated values.",
 "|Use the data below to answer the question: \n54, 36, 62, 79, 83, 36, 62, 42, 62 and 42.\n\nWhat is the modal score?|36|42|54|62": "The mode is the value that occurs most often in a set of data. Count each score in 54, 36, 62, 79, 83, 36, 62, 42, 62 and 42:\nStep 1: 36 appears twice; 42 appears twice; 62 appears three times; 54, 79 and 83 appear once each.\nStep 2: The highest frequency is 3, for 62.\nSo the modal score is 62: D. Note that the mode need not be near the mean (55.8) or the median; it simply records the most frequent value. Memory tip: MOde = MOst Often.",
 "|A major determinant of the demand for a luxury good is|the price of the good|the price of other goods|the income of consumers|tastes and fashion": "A luxury good is one people buy more of as they become richer, such as cars, jewellery and expensive phones. Its demand responds strongly to two things. First, its own price: luxuries have elastic demand, so a rise in price sharply cuts the quantity bought (A). Second, the income of consumers: luxuries have high income elasticity, so demand grows fast as incomes rise (C). Both A and C are correct, so either is accepted. The price of other goods and tastes also affect demand, but less centrally. Memory tip: necessities are bought whatever happens; luxuries depend on what they cost and how much you earn.",
 "|If 100 units and 120 units of commodity X are supplied at ₦80 at different times, it means that there is|a change in supply|a change in the quantity supplied|an increase in the quantity supplied|a decrease in the quantity supplied": "A change in the quantity supplied is a movement along one supply curve caused only by a change in the good's own price. A change in supply is a shift of the whole curve caused by other factors, such as costs of production, technology, weather or the number of firms. Here the price stays the same at ₦80, yet 100 units are offered at one time and 120 units at another. Since price has not changed, the difference must come from other factors shifting the curve: a change in supply, A. The trap: B, C and D all speak of \"quantity supplied\", which can change only when price changes. Memory tip: same price, different quantity = the curve has Shifted.",
 "|If income rises from ₦2000 to ₦4000 and quantity demanded increases from 80 units to 120 units, find the income elasticity of demand.|0.5|1.2|2.5|4.0": "Income elasticity of demand = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in income.\nStep 1: Change in quantity = 120 − 80 = 40 units; % change = 40/80 × 100 = 50%.\nStep 2: Change in income = ₦4000 − ₦2000 = ₦2000; % change = 2000/2000 × 100 = 100%.\nStep 3: Income elasticity = 50% ÷ 100% = 0.5: A.\nA positive figure below 1 shows a normal good that is a necessity. Always put the quantity change on top; dividing the other way round gives 2, which is wrong. Memory tip: elasticity of demand always has the change in Quantity on top.",
 "|If the demand elasticity coefficient of petrol is 0.5, it implies that the demand for petrol is|elastic|perfectly inelastic|inelastic|perfectly elastic": "Price elasticity of demand measures how much quantity demanded responds to a change in price. A coefficient greater than 1 means elastic demand; exactly 1 is unitary; between 0 and 1 is inelastic; 0 is perfectly inelastic; infinity is perfectly elastic. A coefficient of 0.5 means a 10% rise in price cuts the quantity demanded by only 5%, so demand for petrol is inelastic: C. This fits petrol, a necessity with few substitutes. The trap: B (perfectly inelastic) applies only when the coefficient is exactly zero. Memory tip: less than one, the buyers hardly run; zero means none.",
 "|As long as marginal utility is positive, total utility must be|negative|increasing|zero|decreasing": "Total utility is the whole satisfaction from all units consumed; marginal utility is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. Each additional unit adds its marginal utility to the total. As long as marginal utility is positive, every extra unit adds something, so total utility keeps rising: B. When marginal utility reaches zero, total utility is at its maximum (the satiation point), and if marginal utility becomes negative, total utility starts to fall. Total utility cannot be negative or zero while units are still adding satisfaction. Memory tip: MU positive, TU climbs; MU zero, TU peaks; MU negative, TU falls.",
 "|Diminishing marginal utility implies that|total utility decreases as consumption increases|marginal utility increases as consumption increases|marginal utility decreases as consumption increases|marginal utility decreases as consumption decreases": "The law of diminishing marginal utility states that as a consumer takes more and more units of a commodity in a given period, the extra satisfaction from each additional unit falls. The first bottle of cold water on a hot day gives great satisfaction; the fourth gives much less. So marginal utility decreases as consumption increases: C. Total utility still rises (as long as MU is positive), so A is wrong. The trap: D also says marginal utility decreases, but ties it to consumption decreasing, which reverses the law. Memory tip: more and more, enjoy it less and less per unit.",
 "|The excess benefit derived from the purchase of goods over the amount paid for them is referred to as consumer|rationality|surplus|sovereignty|credit": "Consumer surplus is the difference between the maximum price a consumer is willing to pay for a good and the price he actually pays. If you would pay ₦500 for a meal but it costs ₦300, your consumer surplus is ₦200. It arises because the market price is set by the marginal unit, while earlier units are worth more to the buyer. This \"excess benefit\" is consumer surplus: B. Consumer sovereignty is the power of consumers to decide what is produced through their spending; it is a different idea. Memory tip: Surplus = what you would pay minus what you do pay.",
 "|An upward movement along the same supply curve results in|an increase in supply|a decrease in price|a decrease in quantity supplied|an increase in quantity supplied": "A supply curve slopes upwards: at higher prices, sellers offer more. Moving up along the same curve means the price has risen and sellers respond by offering a larger quantity. This is an increase (extension) in quantity supplied: D. An increase in supply (A) is different: it is a rightward shift of the whole curve caused by factors other than price, such as better technology. A decrease in price would mean a movement down the curve, and that gives a decrease (contraction) in quantity supplied. Memory tip: along the curve = quantity supplied; shift of the curve = supply.",
 "|Use the information below to answer the question: \nGiven that Qd = 20 − 4P and Qs = 6P + 12.\n\nDetermine the equilibrium quantity.|14.2|16.8|20.8|30.2": "Equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nStep 1: Set Qd = Qs: 20 − 4P = 6P + 12.\nStep 2: Collect terms: 20 − 12 = 6P + 4P, so 8 = 10P and P = 0.8.\nStep 3: Substitute: Qd = 20 − 4(0.8) = 20 − 3.2 = 16.8.\nCheck: Qs = 6(0.8) + 12 = 4.8 + 12 = 16.8. So the equilibrium quantity is 16.8: B. Note that 0.8 is the equilibrium price, not the quantity. Memory tip: solve for P first, then substitute P into both equations to check that they give the same Q.",
 "|Use the information below to answer the question: \nGiven that Qd = 20 − 4P and Qs = 6P + 12.\n\nIf price is increased to ₦3, how much is the excess supply?|30|22|12|8": "When price is set above equilibrium, sellers offer more than buyers want, giving excess supply (a surplus).\nStep 1: At P = ₦3, quantity supplied Qs = 6(3) + 12 = 18 + 12 = 30.\nStep 2: Quantity demanded Qd = 20 − 4(3) = 20 − 12 = 8.\nStep 3: Excess supply = Qs − Qd = 30 − 8 = 22: B.\nThe trap: A (30) and D (8) are the quantities supplied and demanded themselves, not the gap between them. Memory tip: excess supply = Qs − Qd; always find both quantities and subtract.",
 "|One of the factors affecting the supply of manufactured goods is|weather|technology|consumer tastes|availability of inputs": "The supply of a good depends on its price and on other factors such as the cost and availability of factors of production, technology, government policy, the prices of related goods and the number of firms. For manufactured goods, better technology (B) lowers unit costs and increases output, shifting supply to the right. The availability of inputs such as raw materials, power and labour (D) is just as direct: a factory that cannot get raw materials cannot produce. Both B and D are correct, so either is accepted. Weather mainly affects farm supply, and consumer tastes affect demand. Memory tip: supply factors are things that change the producer's costs or ability to produce.",
 "|Individual markets differ from each other according to|the degree of competition|the volume of trade|the number of buyers|its composition": "Markets are classified by their structure, and the main feature that separates them is the degree of competition among sellers. Perfect competition has many sellers of identical goods; monopolistic competition has many sellers of slightly different goods; oligopoly has a few large sellers; monopoly has one seller with no close substitutes. So individual markets differ according to the degree of competition: A. The volume of trade or the number of buyers can vary within any structure, so they do not define the type of market. Memory tip: from Perfect competition to Monopoly, count the sellers and you know the market.",
 "|Use the information below to answer the question.\n\nIn the diagram above, there is an excess|demand for 30 units|supply of 30 units|demand for 20 units|supply of 20 units": "When the price is above the equilibrium price, sellers are willing to supply more than buyers want, so there is excess supply.\nStep 1: The horizontal price line cuts the demand curve DD at 20 units: quantity demanded = 20.\nStep 2: It cuts the supply curve SS at 40 units: quantity supplied = 40.\nStep 3: Excess supply = 40 − 20 = 20 units: D.\nThe equilibrium quantity (where DD and SS cross) is 30, at a lower price. The trap: C names the right figure, 20, but calls it excess demand; excess demand happens only when price is below equilibrium. Memory tip: price above equilibrium gives a surplus; price below gives a shortage.",
 "|To protect farmers during a bumper harvest, the government usually|sets a maximum price|releases products from the buffer stock|sells the excess to consumers|sets a minimum price": "A bumper harvest means a very large supply of farm produce, which pushes prices sharply down and cuts farmers' incomes. To protect them, the government sets a minimum (floor or guaranteed) price above the market equilibrium, below which the produce may not be sold, and often buys up the surplus to keep prices up: D. A maximum price (A) protects consumers when prices are too high, not farmers. Releasing products from a buffer stock (B) increases supply and lowers prices further, which is done in times of scarcity. Memory tip: MINimum price protects the producer's MINimum income; MAXimum price protects the buyer.",
 "|The use of modern weaving machines in the production of local fabric will result in|an increase in the demand for labour|a decrease in the demand for labour|an increase in wages|a decrease in wages": "Labour and capital can often be substituted for each other. When producers of local fabric adopt modern weaving machines, each machine does the work of many hand weavers, so the same output needs fewer workers. The demand for labour in fabric weaving therefore falls: B. Wages may later fall as a result, but the direct effect of the machines is on the number of workers wanted, not on the wage. In the long run new jobs may appear in making and repairing machines, but in weaving itself demand for labour declines. Memory tip: machines that replace hands reduce the demand for hands.",
 "|In a perfectly competitive condition, a firm uses 10 units of labour at ₦25 each and 11 units at ₦36 each. What is the marginal cost of labour?|₦396|₦323|₦250|₦146": "Marginal cost of labour is the addition to total labour cost from employing one more unit of labour.\nStep 1: Total cost of 10 units = 10 × ₦25 = ₦250.\nStep 2: Total cost of 11 units = 11 × ₦36 = ₦396.\nStep 3: Marginal cost of the 11th unit = ₦396 − ₦250 = ₦146: D.\nNote that the wage rose for all workers, not just the last one, which is why the marginal cost (₦146) is far above the new wage (₦36). The trap: A (₦396) and C (₦250) are the two total costs, not the change between them. Memory tip: marginal = new total − old total.",
 "|From the graph above, point M shows that MC|cuts AC at its minimum point|and AC rises simultaneously|cuts AC at its maximum point|is falling": "In the short run, average cost (AC) first falls and then rises, giving a U shape. Marginal cost (MC) falls faster and starts rising earlier. Whenever MC is below AC it pulls the average down, and whenever MC is above AC it pushes the average up. So MC must cut AC exactly at the lowest point of the AC curve, which is point M in the diagram: A. The same rule holds for MC and AVC: MC cuts AVC at its minimum point too. The trap: C says \"maximum point\", which reverses the rule. Memory tip: a new test score below your average pulls it down; above it pushes it up; MC meets AC at the bottom of the U.",
 "|The characteristic of entry and exit ensures that firms|earn excess profit|earn normal profit|break-even|expand their operations": "Free entry and exit is a key feature of perfect competition. If firms are earning supernormal (excess) profits, new firms enter, supply increases, price falls and the extra profit disappears. If firms are making losses, some leave, supply falls, price rises and losses disappear. In the long run, therefore, each firm earns only normal profit, the minimum needed to keep it in the industry: B. Breaking even in the accounting sense is not the same as earning normal profit, which is counted as part of cost. Memory tip: free entry kills extra profit; free exit kills losses; what remains is Normal profit.",
 "|Which of the following producers is closest to being a monopolist?|A baker|A wheat farmer|A large chain store|An automobile plant": "A monopolist is a sole seller of a product with no close substitutes, protected by high barriers to entry. Bakers and wheat farmers are many and sell similar products, so they are close to perfect competition. A large chain store competes with other shops and traders. An automobile plant needs enormous capital, advanced technology and a large market, so very few firms can enter, and each car brand has some control over its price. Of these, it is closest to being a monopolist: D. Memory tip: the higher the barriers to entry, the closer a firm is to monopoly.",
 "|The output approach to measuring national income is based on the computation of|final output|factor cost|value added|profits earned": "National income can be measured by the output, income or expenditure method. The output method, also called the value-added method, adds up the value each firm or industry adds to its inputs at each stage of production. Value added = value of output − value of inputs bought from other firms. Adding the value added at every stage gives the value of final goods without counting intermediate goods more than once: C. Counting the full output of every firm would cause double counting. Factor cost and profits relate to the income method. The trap: A (final output) gives the same total, but the method's working is built on value added at each stage. Memory tip: Output method = sum of Value Added.",
 "|An example of injection into the circular flow of income is|taxes|pensions|imports|exports": "In the circular flow, injections are additions to spending that do not come from households' consumption: investment (I), government expenditure (G) and exports (X). Withdrawals (leakages) take money out of the flow: savings (S), taxes (T) and imports (M). Exports bring foreigners' spending into the domestic economy, so they are an injection: D. Imports and taxes are leakages, and pensions are transfer payments rather than spending on output. Memory tip: injections are I, G, X; leakages are S, T, M.",
 "|The theoretical relationship between money supply and prices is weakened by changes in the|money supply|general price level|velocity of money|interest rate": "The quantity theory of money (Irving Fisher) is MV = PT, where M is money supply, V is the velocity of circulation, P is the price level and T is the volume of transactions. The theory predicts that prices rise in proportion to money supply only if V and T stay constant. If people spend money faster or slower, V changes, so a given change in M no longer gives a proportionate change in P; the relationship is weakened: C. Money supply and price level are the two variables being related, not things that weaken the link. Memory tip: MV = PT works neatly only when V and T sit still.",
 "|The high rate of inflation in Nigeria can be attributed to|increasing cost of production|the appreciation of the naira|decreasing cost of production|high capacity utilization": "Inflation is a persistent rise in the general price level. In Nigeria a major cause has been cost-push inflation: rising costs of production from fuel price increases, poor power supply forcing firms to run generators, expensive imported raw materials after the naira's fall, high interest rates and poor transport. Firms pass these higher costs on as higher prices: A. An appreciation of the naira would make imports cheaper and reduce inflation, and decreasing costs and high capacity utilisation would also tend to keep prices down. Memory tip: cost-push: costs rise, prices follow.",
 "|If the required reserves of a bank is 20% and ₦10000 is paid into its demand deposit account, what is the excess reserves?|₦2000|₦8000|₦12000|₦18000": "Banks must keep part of each deposit as a required (legal) reserve; whatever they hold above that is excess reserves, which they can lend.\nStep 1: Required reserve = 20% of ₦10000 = 0.2 × ₦10000 = ₦2000.\nStep 2: Excess reserves = deposit − required reserve = ₦10000 − ₦2000 = ₦8000: B.\nThe trap: A (₦2000) is the required reserve itself, not the excess. The excess reserve is the amount the bank can lend, and with a 20% ratio the banking system can create up to 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5 times the deposit. Memory tip: excess reserve = deposit × (1 − reserve ratio).",
 "|Contractionary monetary policy is used to|control inflation|bridge the deflationary gap|expand the output level|deregulate the economy": "Monetary policy is the use of money supply, interest rates and credit by the Central Bank to achieve economic goals. A contractionary (tight) policy reduces money supply and credit: raising the policy rate, selling securities in open market operations, or raising the cash reserve ratio. Less money and dearer credit reduce spending, which brings down inflation: A. Bridging a deflationary gap and expanding output need the opposite, an expansionary policy. Deregulation is not a monetary policy tool. Memory tip: too much money chasing goods? Contract the money to cool the prices.",
 "|The use of government revenue and expenditure to achieve set objectives is known as|budget|fiscal policy|revenue allocation|monetary policy": "Fiscal policy is the government's use of its revenue (mainly taxation) and expenditure to achieve objectives such as full employment, price stability, growth and fair income distribution. For example, cutting taxes and raising spending during a recession boosts demand. The budget (A) is only the annual statement of planned revenue and spending through which fiscal policy is carried out. Monetary policy uses money supply and interest rates, and revenue allocation is the sharing of federal revenue among the tiers of government. So the answer is B. The trap: A (budget) is the tool, not the policy itself. Memory tip: Fiscal = the government's purse (taxing and spending).",
 "|An example of an indirect tax is|profit tax|sales tax|capital gain tax|poll tax": "A direct tax is paid by the person on whom it is levied and cannot be shifted, such as personal income tax, company profit tax, capital gains tax and poll tax. An indirect tax is levied on goods and services, and the seller can pass its burden on to the buyer through a higher price. Examples are sales tax (value added tax in Nigeria today), excise duty and import duty. So sales tax is the indirect tax: B. Memory tip: taxes on what you earn or own are Direct; taxes on what you buy are Indirect.",
 "|A good indicator of economic development is an increase in|gross national product|per capita income|real national income|real per capita income": "Economic development means a rise in the welfare and living standards of the people, not just in total output. Gross national product or real national income can rise simply because the population grows or prices rise. Per capita income divides income by the population, but money figures may only reflect inflation. Real per capita income removes both effects, showing how much the average person can actually buy: D. Fuller measures today such as the Human Development Index add health and education. The trap: B (per capita income) leaves out the effect of inflation. Memory tip: Real removes prices; Per capita removes population.",
 "|Economic growth takes place when there is an increase in|savings over time|investment over time|population over time|output over time": "Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real output of goods and services over time, usually measured by the growth rate of real GDP. It is a quantitative change, while economic development is a qualitative improvement in living standards. Savings and investment can lead to growth, but they are causes, not growth itself, and a rising population may even lower output per head. So growth takes place when output increases over time: D. Memory tip: Growth = more Goods over time.",
 "|One of the effects of instability in farmers' income in Nigeria is|lack of infrastructural facilities|rural-urban migration|inadequate supply of fertilizer|low level of technology": "Farmers' incomes in Nigeria are unstable because farm output depends on weather, pests and seasons, and prices of farm produce swing widely between bumper harvests and scarcity. When their income is so uncertain, many young people leave the villages for towns and cities where wages are more regular, causing rural-urban migration: B. Lack of infrastructure, inadequate fertilizer and low technology are causes of low and unstable farm income, not its effects. Memory tip: unstable farm income pushes youths from farm to town.",
 "|A major impediment to the development of agriculture in Nigeria is|land use system|inadequate supply of farm inputs|low income of farmers|limited research on seedlings": "The problems of Nigerian agriculture include the land tenure system, inadequate farm inputs, poor finance, low technology, poor storage and transport, and pests and diseases. The land use (land tenure) system is the one usually singled out: land is held by families and communities and divided among heirs into small scattered plots, making large-scale mechanised farming difficult, and farmers without title cannot use land as collateral for loans. The Land Use Act of 1978 vested land in state governments but did not end these problems: A. The other options are also real problems, so the question is somewhat debatable. Memory tip: no secure land, no large farm, no loan.",
 "|A major consideration in locating a cement factory is the availability of|skilled labour|power|infrastructural facilities|limestone": "Industries are located near the factor that would be most costly to transport. Cement making uses large amounts of limestone, which is bulky and heavy, and loses much of its weight in processing. It is cheaper to build the factory next to the limestone deposit than to carry the raw material far. That is why Nigerian cement plants are at Ewekoro and Shagamu (Ogun), Nkalagu (Ebonyi), Sokoto, Ashaka (Gombe) and Obajana (Kogi), all near limestone: D. Power and infrastructure matter, but they can be brought to the site. Memory tip: weight-losing raw material, build beside the raw material.",
 "|The export-promotion strategy is aimed at producing|consumer goods originally imported|machinery for industries|more goods for exports|more goods for domestic consumption": "Developing countries use two main industrialisation strategies. Import substitution produces at home goods that were formerly imported, protected by tariffs. Export promotion encourages industries to produce more goods for export, often through incentives such as export grants, duty drawbacks and export processing zones, to earn foreign exchange and use large world markets: C. Option A (consumer goods originally imported) describes import substitution. The trap: A is the other strategy and is often confused with export promotion. Memory tip: Import Substitution replaces Imports; Export Promotion pushes Exports.",
 "|The activities in the oil and gas industry are classified into|exploration and transportation|upstream and downstream|discovery and extraction|onshore and offshore": "Activities in the oil and gas industry are divided into upstream and downstream: B. The upstream sector covers exploration (searching for oil and gas) and production (drilling and extracting crude). The downstream sector covers refining crude oil into products such as petrol, kerosene and diesel, and their transport, distribution and marketing to consumers. Onshore and offshore describe where oil is found, not how activities are classified. Today Nigeria's Petroleum Industry Act 2021 also names a midstream sector (gas processing, pipelines, storage), with the NUPRC regulating upstream and the NMDPRA regulating midstream and downstream. Memory tip: Up is getting oil out of the ground; Down is getting products to the pump.",
 "|The economic policy of privatization came up as a result of the poor performance of|commercial banks|small-scale businesses|public enterprises|private enterprises": "Privatisation is the sale of all or part of government-owned enterprises to private investors. Nigeria adopted it (under SAP from 1988, then through the Bureau of Public Enterprises from 1999) because public enterprises such as NEPA, NITEL and the refineries performed poorly: they were inefficient, made losses, depended on government subsidies, were overstaffed and suffered from political interference: C. Commercial banks and private enterprises were already privately owned. Today NEPA's successor PHCN has been unbundled and sold to private generation and distribution companies. Memory tip: privatise the Public firms that are failing.",
 "|Joint-stock companies can raise funds from|the money market|the capital market|various sources|government": "A joint-stock company (private or public limited liability company) can raise funds from many sources: issuing ordinary and preference shares, issuing debentures, bank loans and overdrafts, trade credit, hire purchase and leasing, and retained (ploughed-back) profits. So the best answer is various sources: C. The capital market (B) is one important source, especially for public companies that sell shares on the stock exchange, and the money market provides short-term funds, but each is only one of many. Memory tip: shares, debentures, loans, profits: a company has many purses.",
 "|The active population of a country determines the size of its|economic resources|national income|labour force|gross domestic product": "The active (economically active or working) population is the part of the population of working age, usually 15 to 64 years, who are willing and able to work, whether they are employed or looking for work. This group is the labour force of the country: C. The labour force helps to produce national income and GDP, but it does not by itself determine their size, since output also depends on capital, technology and land. Memory tip: active population = labour force = employed + unemployed.",
 "|From the shaded area of the diagram above, an appropriate population policy will be to|encourage early marriages|discourage immigration|discourage polygamy|encourage the use of contraceptives": "The curve shows output per head rising as population grows, reaching a peak at the optimum population, and then falling. To the left of the peak a country is underpopulated: it has too few people to use its resources fully, so more people would raise output per head. To the right it is overpopulated. The shaded area is on the left, rising part of the curve, so the country is underpopulated and should encourage population growth, for example by encouraging early marriages: A. Contraceptives and discouraging polygamy would reduce population, which suits overpopulation. Memory tip: left of the peak, add people; right of the peak, reduce them.",
 "|The population of a country will decrease if|the death rate exceeds the birth rate|immigration is equal to emigration|the birth rate exceeds the death rate|there is rapid economic growth": "Population changes through births, deaths and migration. Natural increase = birth rate − death rate. If the death rate is higher than the birth rate, more people die than are born each year, so the population falls (assuming migration does not make up the difference): A. If immigration equals emigration, migration has no net effect, so the result depends on births and deaths; if births exceed deaths (C), the population grows. Rapid economic growth tends to lower the death rate. Memory tip: population change = births − deaths + immigrants − emigrants.",
 "|The OPEC marketing policy of fixing minimum price allows members to|sell above minimum price|sell below minimum price|determine the price and quantity to sell|negotiate price with customers": "A minimum price is a price floor: the lowest price at which a product may be sold. When the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) fixed a minimum price for its members' crude oil, members could not undercut each other by selling below it, but they were free to sell at any price above it when market conditions allowed: A. Selling below the floor (B) is exactly what the policy forbids. Today OPEC, working with other producers as OPEC+ since 2016, influences prices mainly through production quotas rather than a fixed minimum price. Memory tip: a floor stops you going down, not up.",
 "|A major trading problem facing ECOWAS is|the absence of common currency|political instability|high poverty level|non-implementation of decisions": "ECOWAS (Economic Community of West African States, 1975) aims to promote trade among its members. A major trading problem is the absence of a common currency: each member uses its own currency, and the French-speaking members use the CFA franc, so traders face currency conversion costs, exchange controls and payment problems, and trade within the region is discouraged: A. Political instability and non-implementation of decisions are general problems of the community. Today a single ECOWAS currency, the Eco, has been planned for many years but has not yet been launched. Memory tip: many currencies, many barriers.",
 "|A high rent on land will affect the cost of producing maize|positively|negatively|at the early state of production|at the later stage of production": "Rent is the payment for the use of land, a factor of production. When rent on land is high, the farmer's cost of producing maize rises, which reduces his profit and can discourage him from producing as much. So high rent affects the production of maize negatively (adversely): B. It affects costs throughout production, not just at the early or later stage. Memory tip: higher factor prices hurt the producer.",
 "|If the active population is 50 million and unemployed is 5 million, what is the unemployment rate?|10%|45%|55%|90%": "The unemployment rate is the percentage of the labour force (active population) that is without work but willing and able to work.\nStep 1: Unemployment rate = number unemployed ÷ active population × 100.\nStep 2: = 5 million ÷ 50 million × 100.\nStep 3: = 0.1 × 100 = 10%: A.\nThe trap: D (90%) is the employment rate (45 million employed ÷ 50 million × 100), not the unemployment rate. B (45%) simply repeats the 45 million who have jobs. Memory tip: unemployment rate = jobless ÷ labour force × 100.",
 "|A classroom teacher is promoted to the rank of a principal. This is an example of|vertical mobility|lateral mobility|social mobility|horizontal mobility": "Mobility of labour is the ease with which workers move between jobs or places. Geographical mobility is a change of place. Occupational mobility is a change of job, and it can be vertical or horizontal. Vertical mobility is moving up or down the ranks within the same occupation, as when a classroom teacher is promoted to principal: A. Horizontal (lateral) mobility is moving to a different job at the same level, for example from teaching to working as a bank clerk with similar pay. Social mobility refers to a change in social class generally. Memory tip: vertical goes up the ladder; horizontal moves across to another ladder."
};
